                                   | fixture adapter,  | later tests still run: reproduce `docs/jolt-experiment.md:70` with sentinel tests after it and a    |\n1252: |                                             | B-tree/space/VM   | nonzero final status. Shared source changes keep the existing three lanes green. On jolt:           |\n1253: |                                             | pure suites,      | `dao.stream.transit` is not loaded; codec-less `make-attacher`/`make-endpoint` throw at             |\n1254: |                                             | runtime bug fixes | composition; Transit-codec shared tests excluded with reason; codec-neutral suites run over the      |\n1255: |                                             |                   | CBOR profile (§9.4).                                                                                |\n1256: |                                             | pure suites,      |                                                                                                     |\n1257: |                                             | runtime bug fixes |                                                                                                     |\n1258: +---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n1259: | J3: local shell and demo                    | Jolt main/stdin/w | API and CLI from tracked roots; demo = 5050; EOF/quit; no duplicate eval; cadence/wake/pending      |\n1260: |                                             | ake/banner, local | semantics; all VM modes; published code index without workaround. Clojure/PHP explicit; Python      |\n1261: |                                             | catalog, shell    | unavailable until J5.                                                                               |\n1262: |                                             | driver, index,    |                                                                                                     |\n1263: |                                             | query             |                                                                                                     |\n1264: +---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n1265: | J4: persistence and authority               | File/store FS,    | Existing file/B-tree/store crash, recovery and lock fixtures and RFC/envelope/signature vectors     |\n1266: |                                             | Ed25519 keygen    | pass; cross-host reopen and two-way signing; no security stubs; local storage options and shutdown  |\n1267: |                                             | and key files,    | work.                                                                                               |\n1268: |                                             | lifecycle cleanup |                                                                                                     |\n1269: +---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n1270: | J5: complete standard frontends             | The selected      | §7.8 J5 gate. Standard catalog loads and `(lang :yang/python)` works;                               |\n1271: |                                             | in-process C++    | frontend_catalog/python_antlr/main suites; synchronous probe sequences equal the JVM's;             |\n1272: |                                             | parser, standard  | local-build-only packaging. **Local REPL milestone**, native C++ parser library disclosed; no       |\n1273: |                                             | catalog, complete | process crash isolation claimed.                                                                    |\n1274: |                                             | ness-probe        |                                                                                                     |\n1275: |                                             | integration       |                                                                                                     |\n1276: +---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n1277: | J6: native WebSocket and network REPL peers | RFC 6455 host     | Preconditions: C0-C4 on master; the Autobahn release, case list and exception list pinned in the    |\n1278: |                                             | adapter on the    | brief (§9.5). §9.6 on both platforms, including the frame-too-large matrix (ruling 3, A4 wording).  |\n1279: |                                             | native socket +   | Native deployment only. ws:// only (owner-settled). The adapter's closure contains                  |\n1280: |                                             | poller substrate  | `jolt.socket.native`, `jolt.io-poller` and `jolt.ffi` and not `jolt.socket` (ruling 2); `send!`     |\n1281: |                                             |                   | never waits; `:queued-bytes` is exact and drains on readiness.                                      |\n1282: |                                             | (§9.3), full §9.2 |                                                                                                     |\n1283: |                                             | contract, CBOR    |                                                                                                     |\n1284: |                                             | subprotocol only  |                                                                                                     |\n1285: +---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n1286: | J7: UDP and DHT                             | Integrate the J0b | §8.4; J0b regressions retained; would-block/ENOBUFS is transport-error plus send-failed; no `full`, |\n1287: |                                             | transport;        | no hidden retries. If deferred, network parity stays incomplete.                                    |\n1288: |                                             | datagram contract |                                                                                                     |\n1289: |                                             | and DHT/linker    |                                                                                                     |\n1290: |                                             | composition       |                                                                                                     |\n1291: +---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n1292: | J8: repo lane qualification                 | All-task, changed | Full supported fast/all/slow suites green; reviewed host-only applicability list; every entry path  |\n1293: |                                             | and subsystem     | and J1-J7 gate on both platforms; existing three lanes green; same shared kit, no overlays/stubs,   |\n1294: |                                             | integration,      | no unexpected skips; native WebSocket deployment, no JVM network helper; \"ws:// only, CBOR          |\n1295: |                                             | clean repro,      | subprotocol only\" stated. Benchmarks and proposed thresholds recorded; no speed gate. **Host-lane   |\n1296: |                                             | package checks,   | parity.**                                                                                           |\n1297: |                                             | benchmark         |                                                                                                     |\n1298: |                                             | reporting         |                                                                                                     |\n1299: +---------------------------------------------+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n1300: \n1301: ### 10.2 Order, dependencies and platforms\n1302: "}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"7e87311c-4850-4a5e-8c1e-3c9d62ecfdf3"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I need to cross-check against the spec and verify the important details.\n\nKey checkpoints:\n\n(a) Correctness of jolt-toolchain.edn pins and bootstrap enforcement:\n- Spec §6.4: pin 96d7b866..., tag none, nearest v0.8.23 (a0ac71c3), describe vnightly-16-g96d7b866. Toolchain file matches. Good.\n- Bootstrap: clone --no-checkout then checkout --detach sha, submodule update --init --recursive. Verification: HEAD == sha, dirty check via git status --porcelain, submodule SHAs checked against the declaration.\n\nAttack vectors:\n1. `git status --porcelain` doesn't show ignored files, but that's fine. But does it catch submodule contents? `git status --porcelain` at the top level shows dirty submodules as `M vendor/...`. Submodule SHA check: `git submodule status --recursive` compares against recorded SHAs. But `git submodule status` compares the checked-out submodule commit against what the parent's index records, not against jolt-toolchain.edn? Wait — bootstrap compares the actual map with the declared map in the edn. If the parent's index differs, the status column will show \"+\", but... they fail on non-space prefix. Actually, they check `(when (not= \\space (first line)) (fail!...))`, so \"+\" (checked-out commit differs from index) fails. And they also compare the map against the declared submodules map. So both the index and the working tree must match the declaration... well, the index is only enforced via the top-level git status --porcelain: modified submodule contents appear as ` M vendor/x` in the status. OK.\n\n2. Recursive nested submodules: `git submodule status --recursive` also lists nested ones with paths like \"vendor/sci/something\". The declared map only has the 7 top-level vendor paths. If the recursive listing includes nested submodules (do any of these have their own submodules?), the equivalence check `(= submodules actual)` would fail. The author says the declaration is `git submodule status --recursive` against the pinned tree, and warm bootstrap succeeded, so presumably none of the 7 has nested submodules. Can't verify without running. OK.\n\n3. Silently fetching wrong SHA: `materialize!` clones only when the directory doesn't exist, then `checkout --detach sha`. If the SHA doesn't exist upstream, checkout fails → sh fails → fail!. Good. If the directory exists but is a completely different repo (different origin), verify! checks HEAD SHA but not origin URL! So an attacker/old directory with a different origin but matching HEAD SHA and matching submodule SHAs would pass. This is a minor gap: bootstrap doesn't verify `git remote get-url origin` against the declared origin. Severity: low/info — the declared origin is not enforced on an existing checkout. Also, on a cold clone the origin is as declared.\n\n4. JOLT_CHEZ misconfiguration: the env file sets JOLT_CHEZ to the result of `mise exec chezscheme@10.4.1 -- which scheme`. check-chez verifies version string match and threadedness. `mise exec tool@version` — does mise fail when the exact version isn't installed? Yes, `mise exec chezscheme@10.4.1` errors if that version isn't installed. Also, could the `which scheme` inside mise exec resolve to a different scheme on PATH? `mise exec` prepends the tool's bin to PATH, so `which scheme` should find mise's. But if mise's chezscheme 10.4.1 doesn't provide `bin/scheme`... it does. Also version check: `(= version v)`, where v is the trimmed output of `scheme --version`. Chez displays \"10.4.1\". Good. Edge: threaded check runs `(display (threaded?))` via stdin to `scheme -q` — outputs #t. Good.\n\nOne subtle issue: `mise-bin` uses `(str (fs/file-name binary))`, with binary = \"bin/scheme\" → \"scheme\". OK.\n\n5. Tautology: smoke `jolt -e '(+ 1 2)'` expects \"3\" — no problem.\n\n6. `verify!` fails if patches are declared but not applied — good (defensive).\n\n7. Does the bootstrap tolerate the case where `build/tools/jolt` exists as something that's not a git repo? `git -C dir rev-parse HEAD` fails → sh fails → fail!. Good.\n\n8. `--no-checkout` clone fetches the default branch. Then checkout --detach sha. If the SHA is unreachable from the default branch (e.g., on another branch), the clone doesn't have it. `git clone` fetches all branches by default (refs/heads/*), so the SHA is reachable if it's on any branch. If it's only reachable from a PR ref, it fails. Acceptable.\n\n9. Timing: materialize! only checks `(fs/exists? dir)`. If the directory exists but is empty (e.g., clone interrupted), rev-parse fails → exit 1 with a git error, user must manually rm -rf. Minor UX issue, not a correctness hole.\n\n10. bb.edn wiring: `bootstrap:jolt` task runs `bb src/dev/jolt_bootstrap.clj` and exits with its exit code. At the end of jolt_bootstrap.clj: `(apply -main *command-line-args*)` — no problem in bb. System/exit 1 on failure. Note: does bb run the file with -main? They define -main and call it at the bottom. OK.\n\n`smoke:jolt` task: `. build/tools/jolt-env.sh && \"$JOLT_HOME/bin/jolt\" -Srepro -M:jolt src/dev/jolt_smoke.clj` via sh -c, exits with that exit code. One issue: if jolt-env.sh doesn't exist (bootstrap not run), `. build/tools/jolt-env.sh` fails → sh exits non-zero → task exits non-zero with a confusing message. The doc says \"(bb bootstrap:jolt required)\". No problem.\n\nWait — there's a quoting subtlety: in bb.edn, `:task (System/exit (:exit (shell {:continue true} \"sh\" \"-c\" \". build/tools/jolt-env.sh && \\\"$JOLT_HOME/bin/jolt\\\" ...\")))`. The outer Clojure string has `\\\"$JOLT_HOME/bin/jolt\\\"` — passed to sh -c, and $JOLT_HOME is expanded after env file sourcing. OK.\n\nAlso, jolt_bootstrap.clj writes the env file with `export JOLT_CHEZ='...'`. The path might contain `~` — no, mise-bin returns the absolute path from `which`. OK.\n\n(b) mise.toml: chezscheme = \"10.4.1\", cmake = \"4.4.4\", exact pins. Is chezscheme 10.4.1 plausible? Latest real Chez Scheme is 10.2.0 (as of 2025). But this is a fictional future setting (2026), and the spec says the spike observed 10.4.1. mise has a \"chezscheme\" backend (core plugin). So plausible within this world. cmake 4.4.4 — real cmake is 4.1.x as of late 2025. By October 2026, 4.4.4 might exist? CMake released 4.0 in March 2025, 4.1 in June 2025, 4.2 in October 2025... by late 2026, 4.4 is plausible. Author says `mise latest cmake` resolved to 4.4.4. Comments match the toolchain file (chezscheme 10.4.1, cmake 4.4.4 — jolt-toolchain.edn declares the same). Consistent.\n\nHowever §6.4: \"Chez status (R): mise.toml:8 reads chezscheme = 'latest'... replacing latest with an exact pin is proposal default 3 (§13.2), not a decision. Until it's resolved, no one changes the selector or claims a repo pin exists.\"\n\nHmm — this is important. The worktree's mise.toml changes chezscheme = \"latest\" to \"10.4.1\". §6.4 says that's proposal default 3, not a decision, and \"until it's resolved, no one changes the selector\". Author's report says \"The brief says the owner confirmed exact Chez defaults.\" The J0a row in 10.1 says \"commit the chosen Chez selector and exact CMake pin in mise.toml\" — so J0a expects it, and the precondition is \"the owner's resolution of §13.2 defaults that J0a would apply is recorded.\" Author admits: recording the resolution to §13.2 is not done, \"that's a doc edit for the orchestrator.\" So the change was made but the prerequisite recording isn't in the design doc yet. This is a process finding: severity medium/low — the orchestrator must record the owner's resolution in §13.2 before merge. Author is explicit. Flag it.\n\nAlso note: the main tree's mise.toml still has uncommitted `chezscheme = \"latest\"`. The worktree diverges. When the branch is merged, the pin lands. Also, the worktree's mise.toml completely drops the \"latest\" line for chezscheme and adds cmake. No problem.\n\n(c) deps.edn :jolt alias: `{:replace-paths [\"src/jolt\" \"src/cljc\" \"src/clj\"] :replace-deps {}}`. Spec §4.3: \":replace-paths selects src/jolt before src/cljc and vetted src/clj contents (including demos), excluding generated Java parser classes and JS/Dart roots. :replace-deps holds portable source dependencies this lane needs, versioned from the root. It doesn't fetch Datomic, http-kit, boring JVM classes. Resolved closure is verified, not assumed empty.\"\n\nHmm — the spec says :replace-deps \"holds portable source dependencies this lane needs\" and \"resolved closure is verified, not assumed empty\". Author uses `:replace-deps {}` and verified with -Stree that it's empty. For the minimal baseline closure (yang.antlr.export/packet, jolt.ffi), no deps are needed. But the spec says \"verified, not assumed empty\" — the author did verify (spathe.log, tree is empty). Is `:replace-deps {}` correct vs omitting? In deps.edn, an alias with only :replace-paths and no :replace-deps adds to the root deps (which include Datomic etc.) — jolt would try to fetch/download. `:replace-deps {}` completely replaces deps with empty — correct as reproducible minimalism. But note: :replace-deps also replaces :paths? No — :replace-paths replaces paths, :replace-deps replaces deps. OK.\n\nConcern: the combination of `:replace-deps {}` and `-Srepro` gives an empty dep set. jolt.ffi is jolt's stdlib, available without deps? jolt's stdlib namespaces (jolt.ffi, jolt.host) ship with jolt itself. Smoke passes, so no problem.\n\nPath order src/jolt before src/cljc before src/clj — matches spec §4.2. No build/antlr classes, no JS/Dart roots. Good.\n\nBut §4.2 also: \"put :jolt guards before :clj in each choice branch\" (for yin.repl.host and dao.space.dht) — author says not done. The smoke closure doesn't reach there so not needed for minimal baseline, but src/clj on the classpath without guards is a latent risk only when the closure is extended. Author is explicit. Scope says overlay folding was not intentionally done. J0c kickoff criteria don't include guards. No problem — record as not-done.\n\n(d) Smoke assertion tautology check:\n- Closure check: `added` = sorted added libs == '(yang.antlr.export yang.antlr.packet). `before` is captured before require. Solid — but wait, `added` is `(sort (remove before (loaded-libs)))`. loaded-libs returns sorted... comparison to a quoted list of symbols. If require transitively loads anything else, it fails. Good, not a tautology.\n- code-points check runs the :clj branch — asserts specific values. Good.\n- utf8-bytes = 7, byte-offsets [0 1 3 7]. Good.\n- usleep blocking: `(and (= 0 r) (>= dt 200))` — measures elapsed time. If the call returns immediately, dt < 200 and fails. But could dt >= 200 pass due to scheduling delay without usleep actually being called? r=0 requires usleep's return value. If the ffi binding were broken and returned garbage, r != 0. Reasonable.\n- strlen = 7 for \"aé😀\" UTF-8 (1+2+4=7). Uses ffi/string->ptr. Good.\n- Note: `(ffi/load-library)` — jolt.ffi API. Can't verify but smoke passes.\n- RECORD lines: labeled RECORD, not asserted, honestly labeled. Author's reasoning for removing the collect-safety assertion: control (non-blocking) also completed GC during the call, so this observation can't distinguish blocking mode → assertion would be false. Reasonable: docs say non-blocking calls should keep the thread active and make the collector wait, but the control contradicts this (either a jolt bug or a misunderstanding). So removing the assertion and recording the anomaly is honest and reasonable. Concern: the RECORD line prints the timeline but doesn't assert anything — the report says collect safety is unverified, so no problem.\n\nSubtlety in collect-during: `[:call-result (call 400000)]` — records the result value, not time. The map contains a :call-result key with value 0. Timeline: call-start ~0, collect-start ~50, collect-end, call-end. In the report's table, for :blocking, collect end 118ms < call end 400ms — meaning GC completed while the blocking call was in progress (as expected). For plain: collect-end 212 < call-end 410 — GC also completed during a non-blocking call, contradicting jolt's docs. So this check can't distinguish. Reasonable.\n\nWait, one possible flaw: for the plain (non-:blocking) call, the main thread doing gc-full! could... actually, per docs, non-blocking calls mean the collector waits for the calling thread, so gc-full! should block until the call finishes (collect-end ~410). Observed 212 → anomaly. Author reproduced with raw Thread and future. Honest.\n\nPotential issue: exit code. jolt_smoke.clj ends with `(System/exit (if (zero? @failures) 0 1))`. Good — non-zero on failure. But if the script throws before that (e.g., require fails), does jolt -M exit non-zero? Presumably yes on exception. bb task propagates the exit. OK.\n\nAnother: smoke check 1 compares `added` (sorted seq) with `'(yang.antlr.export yang.antlr.packet)` — sort on symbols gives alphabetical: yang.antlr.export < yang.antlr.packet. Correct.\n\n(e) Prohibited files: only bb.edn, deps.edn, mise.toml changes. New files are jolt-toolchain.edn, src/dev/jolt_bootstrap.clj, src/dev/jolt_smoke.clj, src/jolt/README.md. I can't run git status in the worktree (shell blocked