ure status when needed\". Fix: use a static const message or a noexcept path for -2/-3 (e.g., return without a buffer, or copy e.what() via a non-throwing path using malloc).\n\nC2 (low/info): C++ LexerBase patch: `positionedToken(Python3Parser::DEDENT, getCharIndex(), getCharIndex() - 1)` — getCharIndex() returns size_t; at index 0 it underflows to SIZE_MAX and saturates to 0xFFFFFFFF via idx() (\"none\"). Also NEWLINE passes text \"\" to _factory->create — wait, in the C++ patch text \"\" is passed explicitly, whereas Java passes null text (read back from source). Hmm: the manifest comment says for C++ \"text is read back from input\": the comment says \"text is read back from input\" but the patch passes \"\"? `_factory->create({this,_input}, type, \"\", DEFAULT_TOKEN_CHANNEL, ...)`. In ANTLR C++'s CommonToken, if _text is set to \"\" (non-null?), getText() returns _text if _text is non-null… CommonToken::getText(): `if (!_text.empty()) return _text; ... read from source`. Actually in ANTLR C++ CommonToken, if _text is non-empty return it, otherwise extract from the input stream between start..stop. So \"\" → read from source. OK, the comment is accurate. But DEDENT with start=k, stop=k-1 (underflow to SIZE_MAX): text extraction from start..stop with stop < start → ANTLR C++'s getText from source: `if (_input == nullptr) return \"\"; ... _input->getText(misc::Interval(start, stop))` — Interval with huge stop → could throw or return a weird value. ANTLR's CharStream::getText with an invalid interval — ANTLRInputStream::getText clamps stop to size-1 and returns \"\" if start > stop? In the C++ runtime, ANTLRInputStream::getText(interval): if interval.stop >= size then stop = size-1; if start > stop returns \"\". Probably safe. Java version has the same zero-width pattern (stop=start-1) and it's landed and works. So at most info. Mention briefly as info: size_t wraparound is intentionally mapped to the documented 0xFFFFFFFF \"none\" encoding; verify against the oracle.\n\nActually, wait — for a zero-width DEDENT at index k: start=k, stop=k-1. The wire writes idx(k-1) — if k>0 that's fine (k-1). Only k=0 wraps. DEDENT at charIndex 0 can't happen (DEDENT requires a prior indent). NEWLINE at EOF: at EOF getCharIndex()=size, stop=size-1, fine.\n\nC3 (info): fetch coupling — adding Cpp helpers to the manifest's :files means gen:antlr for any target will fetch + digest-check them (see r2-gen-verify.log fetching all files); a network/CDN failure on a Cpp file will also break Java/JS/Dart generation. Outputs and digests for the three existing targets are unchanged (digests byte-identical in manifest; generator changes are opt-in via :exts). Note only.\n\nC4 (info): the report/author's own callouts are honest: zip-vs-tag provenance unverified (report line 31), .a is not reproducible, exported symbols (~1556 antlr4:: symbols exported — interposition risk), helper license UNRESOLVED, no parity with JVM, divergence (line/col from lastToken; getIndentationCount byte-wise). All disclosed.\n\nC5 (info): brief says orchestrator observed \"6 PASS lines\"; final-probe.log has 7 PASS + SUMMARY ok. Discrepancy in orchestrator brief, not the change itself.\n\nC6? probe's render function — stack logic complexity, cosmetic only. Skip.\n\nC7 (info): CMakeLists `file(GLOB GENERATED_SOURCES \"${GENERATED_DIR}/*.cpp\")` — glob picks up any cpp in the directory; the generation directory contains only pinned 8 files (digest-checked). GLOB without CONFIGURE_DEPENDS is a stale-cache hazard on rebuild, but jolt_native.clj deletes the build tree each time (fs/delete-tree bld). Fine.\n\nC8: jolt_native.clj uses system `c++` for the record but cmake picks its own default compiler — the recorded cxx is presumably the same Apple clang. Also `unzip` availability is assumed. Info at most; skip for word budget.\n\nConfirming (b) \"byte-identical\" behavior of shared tools: changes in yang_antlr_gen.clj:\n1. docstring usage line (comment only),\n2. `target-exts` added,\n3. run-antlr!: exts vector + not-any? — equivalent to single-extension filter for old targets,\n4. generated-digests: some ends-with? against [ext] — equivalent,\n5. generate-target! passes (target-exts target) — equivalent.\nOne subtle behavioral difference: old code filtered output files by `(not (str/ends-with? name ext))`; new `(not-any? #(str/ends-with? name %) exts)` with exts=[\".java\"] — identical predicate. And generated-digests old `(str/ends-with? name ext)` vs new `(some ... exts)` — identical. So existing targets' outputs + digests are byte-identical. ✓ Also java/js/dart manifest :generated digests unchanged from base. ✓ And verify re-run log confirms digests for cpp target; for other targets r2-gen log? r2-gen.log presumably runs --target cpp only. Existing-target regeneration hasn't been re-run by the author? They claim unchanged via code path. Orchestrator can run bb gen:antlr. This is a \"couldn't verify\" item: I haven't re-run Java/JS/Dart generation to confirm identical outputs; equivalence is by code read (strong).\n\n(e) Forbidden files: touched files = manifest, bb.edn, jolt-toolchain.edn, yang_antlr_gen.clj + new native/ and src/dev files. No changes to sources under dao.*/yin.vm; main tree untouched (couldn't run git but confirmed main-tree manifest + gen file are the base versions; bb.edn diff is additive only).\n\nAlso confirm nothing else in the worktree changed: deps.edn/mise.toml/src/dev/jolt_bootstrap.clj etc. — the jolt-j0c branch is based on jolt-j0a commit 9f1f9144, which contains the J0a content (committed). Declared changes are 4 modifications + 4 new. jolt_bootstrap.clj inside the j0c worktree — unverified; probably committed as of base. Skip — note reliance on file reads of the declared list.\n\nWord budget: <700. Verdict: SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES (fix C1) or SIGNED OFF? C1 is a genuine (rare) escape path that violates §7.3's \"non-allocating failure status when needed\" and \"no exception shall cross the C boundary\". In an