 |\n17: |---|---|\n18: | **F1 (required)**: the catch handlers allocated | `native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp` `message()` is now `noexcept`. It sets `*out = NULL` and `*out_len = 0` first, then copies `e.what()` (or `\"unknown exception\"`) using only `strlen`/`malloc`/`memcpy`. If that `malloc` fails, the buffer stays NULL and only the status (−2/−3) reports the failure. No `std::string`/`std::vector` remain in any failure path, and nothing in a handler can throw. The header comment documents this. |\n19: | F1 test | New exported **test seam** `int32_t jolt_parser_inject_fault(int32_t mode)`. It is off (0) unless called, and answers the previous mode. Mode 1 throws `std::bad_alloc` inside the parse's `try`; mode 2 throws a non-`std::exception` (`42`); mode 3 throws `bad_alloc` and also makes the handler's own `malloc` fail. The probe adds four checks (below). The seam is always compiled in: it is one atomic int, disclosed here, and gating it behind a build option is a later-round decision. |\n20: | F2 (info) | No change, as the review asked. DEDENT at index 0 wrapping and text semantics stay under the deferred JVM-oracle span parity. |\n21: | F3 (info) | `antlr/python3/manifest.edn`, beside the `Cpp/` `:files` entries: \"Every target's gen:antlr run fetches and digest-checks ALL of these, so a Cpp helper fetch failure also blocks the Java, JavaScript and Dart generation; all targets re-pin together.\" |\n22: | F4 (orchestrator note) | Acknowledged. The probe had 7 checks and now has **11**. |\n23: \n24: ## F1 evidence: `src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj`, 11/11 PASS (`fix-probe.log`, `fix-probe-final.log`, both exit 0)\n25: \n26: The 7 round-1 checks are unchanged and still pass. The new checks:\n27: \n28: | check | result |\n29: |---|---|\n30: | `[injected]` `std::bad_alloc` inside the parse | `{:status -2, :message \"std::bad_alloc\"}` PASS |\n31: | `[injected]` non-std exception | `{:status -3, :message \"unknown exception\"}` PASS |\n32: | `[injected]` `bad_alloc`, and the handler's own malloc fails | `{:status -2, :buffer-bytes 0}` (NULL buffer, no throw) PASS |\n33: | after the injected failures, the same process parses `\"1 + 2\\n\"` again | `{:status 0, :buffer-bytes 3196}` PASS |\n34: \n35: - The jolt process survives every injected failure and reads the failure status. Each injection is reset to 0 in a `finally`.\n36: - **Rebuild:** `bb build:jolt-native` exit 0, 49.62 s (`fix-build.log`).\n37: - New dylib SHA-256: `00c27f065ced03717ac378d560b2a781fbd2aabd3232efdf5aa9eadc9c515419`. It changed because the source changed.\n38: - `nm -gU` now shows four C entries: `jolt_parser_parse`, `jolt_parser_free`, `jolt_parser_layout_version`, `jolt_parser_inject_fault`.\n39: \n40: ## Cross-target verification (Java, JavaScript, Dart unchanged vs base `jolt-j0a`)\n41: \n42: - **Task name:** `docs/agents/build-n-test.md:120,254,407` names `bb gen:python-antlr`, but `bb.edn` defines only `gen:antlr`. That is a doc/task naming mismatch, pre-existing and not changed here. I ran **`bb gen:antlr`** (every target, foreground, with no other generation running in this worktree).\n43:   - Result: exit 0, 3.65 s (`fix-gen-all.log`). \"generated 8 cpp / 4 dart / 4 java / 4 javascript files\".\n44:   - The generator throws on any difference from a target's recorded `:generated` digests, so a zero exit already proves each output matches its record.\n45: - **Network:** none was needed. `build/antlr/python3/upstream/` already held all 12 pinned files from round 1. The log's \"fetched\" label is printed for cache hits as well, and the generator downloads only when a file is absent.\n46: - **Recorded digests:** the `:java`, `:javascript` and `:dart` target maps, including `:generated`, are `=` to `jolt-j0a`'s `antlr/python3/manifest.edn` (read with `clojure.edn`).\n47: - **Before/after:** before is `jolt-j0a`'s recorded digests; after is this run's `generated-digests.edn` (`fix-gen-compare.log`, first 16 hex digits shown). All equal.\n48: \n49: | file | before | after |\n50: |---|---|---|\n51: | Python3Lexer.java | bb61a463af3d2c5d | bb61a463af3d2c5d |\n52: | Python3LexerBase.java | 1c8ac0a2bd1cc149 | 1c8ac0a2bd1cc149 |\n53: | Python3Parser.java | 8ba1f2398e52994e | 8ba1f2398e52994e |\n54: | Python3ParserBase.java | 332745797ab6423d | 332745797ab6423d |\n55: | Python3Lexer.js | 855f47de669d2f04 | 855f47de669d2f04 |\n56: | Python3LexerBase.js | 4724a9da8856cf08 | 4724a9da8856cf08 |\n57: | Python3Parser.js | 37f27bdb7036ae99 | 37f27bdb7036ae99 |\n58: | Python3ParserBase.js | a569e60fdf199334 | a569e60fdf199334 |\n59: | Python3Lexer.dart | a29f3f8cceb6ec2b | a29f3f8cceb6ec2b |\n60: | Python3LexerBase.dart | e3b11f882d64f64d | e3b11f882d64f64d |\n61: | Python3Parser.dart | fa00271187b2ba97 | fa00271187b2ba97 |\n62: | Python3ParserBase.dart | e4c4693a640e53b9 | e4c4693a640e53b9 |\n63: \n64: Nothing differs.\n65: - Generated output went to gitignored `build/antlr/python3/` and `lib/antlr/python3/`. Nothing was staged.\n66: \n67: ## Commands and exit codes (foreground, in the worktree)\n68: \n69: | command | exit |\n70: |---|---|\n71: | `bb build:jolt-native` | 0 (49.62 s) |\n72: | `. build/tools/jolt-env.sh && \"$JOLT_HOME\"/bin/jolt -Srepro -M:jolt src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj` (×2) | 0, 11/11 PASS |\n73: | `bb gen:antlr` | 0 (3.65 s) |\n74: | digest comparison vs `git show jolt-j0a:antlr/python3/manifest.edn` | all equal |\n75: | `bb smoke:jolt` | 0, `SUMMARY ok` (`fix-smoke.log`) |\n76: | `mise exec -- cljstyle check` on the changed files | clean. `manifest.edn` still shows only the pre-existing JS-patch indentation hunks that base has |\n77: \n78: ## Files changed this round (worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c`)\n79: - `native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp` (F1: nonallocating `noexcept` failure path, fault-injection test seam)\n80: - `src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj` (F1: four new checks, the seam binding)\n81: - `antlr/python3/manifest.edn` (F3 note)"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_f44efc0d71cf4a989ce2504f","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 212 lines\n1: // jolt-parser: the extern \"C\" boundary between jolt and the ANTLR4 C++\n2: // Python3 parser (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §7.3).\n3: //\n4: // J0c round 1 probe surface. Bytes in, bytes out: the caller passes UTF-8\n5: // source by pointer and length and receives one private marshalling buffer,\n6: // which it copies and returns through jolt_parser_free. No C++ class, layout\n7: // or exception crosses this boundary; every entry catches std::exception and\n8: // (...) and answers an explicit status. This is process-local marshalling (like\n9: // a sockaddr), not a value codec: it never enters a stream. Packets are built\n10: // by the shared jolt/Clojure exporter from these records, never here.\n11: //\n12: // Buffer layout, all integers little-endian:\n13: //\n14: //   magic   \"JPT1\"\n15: //   u32     layout version (1)\n16: //   u32     status (0 ok, 1 syntax errors)\n17: //   u32     rule-name count R,     then R  x (u32 len, UTF-8 bytes)\n18: //   u32     symbolic-name count S, then S  x (u32 len, UTF-8 bytes)\n19: //   u32     node count N, then N preorder records:\n20: //             u8  kind (0 rule, 1 token)\n21: //             rule:  u32 rule index, u32 child count\n22: //             token: i32 token type, u32 start, u32 stop (code-point indexes,\n23: //                    0xFFFFFFFF = none), u32 text len, UTF-8 text\n24: //   u32     error count E, then E x (u32 line, u32 column, u32 len, UTF-8 msg)\n25: //\n26: // Status codes returned by jolt_parser_parse:\n27: //    0  parsed, buffer written (status field says whether errors occurred)\n28: //   -1  invalid arguments\n29: //   -2  C++ exception (std::exception); *out holds its message, UTF-8\n30: //   -3  unknown exception\n31: //   -4  output would exceed the bound\n32: // On -2/-3 the message buffer is built without anything that can throw\n33: // (strlen/malloc/memcpy only); if even that malloc fails, *out stays NULL and\n34: // only the status reports the failure. No handler can throw.\n35: //\n36: // jolt_parser_inject_fault is a TEST SEAM (off unless called): it makes the\n37: // next parses fail deterministically so the containment paths can be shown\n38: // from jolt without exhausting memory.\n39: \n40: #include <atomic>\n41: #include <cstdint>\n42: #include <cstdlib>\n43: #include <cstring>\n44: #include <limits>\n45: #include <string>\n46: #include <string_view>\n47: #include <vector>\n48: \n49: #include \"antlr4-runtime.h\"\n50: #include \"Python3Lexer.h\"\n51: #include \"Python3Parser.h\"\n52: \n53: #define JOLT_EXPORT extern \"C\" __attribute__((visibility(\"default\")))\n54: \n55: namespace {\n56: \n57: constexpr size_t kMaxOutput = 64u * 1024u * 1024u;\n58: \n59: // 0 off; 1 throw std::bad_alloc inside the parse; 2 throw a non-std\n60: // exception; 3 as 1, and the failure handler's malloc fails too.\n61: std::atomic<int32_t> g_fault{0};\n62: \n63: struct Out {\n64:   std::vector<uint8_t> buf;\n65:   void u8(uint8_t v) { buf.push_back(v); }\n66:   void u32(uint32_t v) {\n67:     for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) buf.push_back(static_cast<uint8_t>(v >> (8 * i)));\n68:   }\n69:   void str(std::string_view s) {\n70:     u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(s.size()));\n71:     buf.insert(buf.end(), s.begin(), s.end());\n72:   }\n73: };\n74: \n75: uint32_t idx(size_t i) {\n76:   return i > std::numeric_limits<uint32_t>::max()\n77:              ? std::numeric_limits<uint32_t>::max()\n78:              : static_cast<uint32_t>(i);\n79: }\n80: \n81: struct Error {\n82:   uint32_t line, column;\n83:   std::string msg;\n84: };\n85: \n86: class Collect : public antlr4::BaseErrorListener {\n87:  public:\n88:   std::vector<Error> errors;\n89:   void syntaxError(antlr4::Recognizer*, antlr4::Token*, size_t line,\n90:                    size_t column, const std::string& msg,\n91:                    std::exception_ptr) override {\n92:     errors.push_back({idx(line), idx(column), msg});\n93:   }\n94: };\n95: \n96: void walk(antlr4::tree::ParseTree* t, Out& out, uint32_t& count) {\n97:   ++count;\n98:   if (auto* term = dynamic_cast<antlr4::tree::TerminalNode*>(t)) {\n99:     antlr4::Token* tok = term->getSymbol();\n100:     out.u8(1);\n101:     out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(static_cast<int32_t>(tok->getType())));\n102:     out.u32(idx(tok->getStartIndex()));\n103:     out.u32(idx(tok->getStopIndex()));\n104:     out.str(tok->getText());\n105:     return;\n106:   }\n107:   auto* ctx = dynamic_cast<antlr4::ParserRuleContext*>(t);\n108:   out.u8(0);\n109:   out.u32(idx(ctx->getRuleIndex()));\n110:   out.u32(idx(ctx->children.size()));\n111:   for (auto* c : ctx->children) walk(c, out, count);\n112: }\n113: \n114: int32_t hand_off(const std::vector<uint8_t>& bytes, uint8_t** out,\n115:                  size_t* out_len) {\n116:   if (bytes.size() > kMaxOutput) return -4;\n117:   auto* p = static_cast<uint8_t*>(std::malloc(bytes.empty() ? 1 : bytes.size()));\n118:   if (!p) return -3;\n119:   if (!bytes.empty()) std::memcpy(p, bytes.data(), bytes.size());\n120:   *out = p;\n121:   *out_len = bytes.size();\n122:   return 0;\n123: }\n124: \n125: // The failure answer: `status`, plus the message copied into a malloc'd\n126: // buffer when that is possible. Nonallocating apart from that one malloc,\n127: // whose failure leaves *out NULL. Never throws.\n128: int32_t message(const char* what, int32_t status, uint8_t** out,\n129:                 size_t* out_len) noexcept {\n130:   *out = nullptr;\n131:   *out_len = 0;\n132:   const char* m = what ? what : \"\";\n133:   size_t n = std::strlen(m);\n134:   void* p = g_fault.load() == 3 ? nullptr : std::malloc(n == 0 ? 1 : n);\n135:   if (p) {\n136:     std::memcpy(p, m, n);\n137:     *out = static_cast<uint8_t*>(p);\n138:     *out_len = n;\n139:   }\n140:   return status;\n141: }\n142: \n143: }  // namespace\n144: \n145: // Parse UTF-8 `src[0, len)` from the `file_input` rule.\n146: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_parse(const uint8_t* src, size_t len,\n147:                                       uint8_t** out, size_t* out_len) {\n148:   if (!out || !out_len || (!src && len != 0)) return -1;\n149:   *out = nullptr;\n150:   *out_len = 0;\n151:   try {\n152:     switch (g_fault.load()) {\n153:       case 1:\n154:       case 3: throw std::bad_alloc();\n155:       case 2: throw 42;\n156:       default: break;\n157:     }\n158:     std::string text(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(src), len);\n159:     antlr4::ANTLRInputStream input(text);\n160:     Python3Lexer lexer(&input);\n161:     antlr4::CommonTokenStream tokens(&lexer);\n162:     Python3Parser parser(&tokens);\n163:     Collect errors;\n164:     lexer.removeErrorListeners();\n165:     parser.removeErrorListeners();\n166:     lexer.addErrorListener(&errors);\n167:     parser.addErrorListener(&errors);\n168: \n169:     antlr4::tree::ParseTree* tree = parser.file_input();\n170: \n171:     Out o;\n172:     o.buf.insert(o.buf.end(), {'J', 'P', 'T', '1'});\n173:     o.u32(1);\n174:     o.u32(errors.errors.empty() ? 0 : 1);\n175:     const auto& rules = parser.getRuleNames();\n176:     o.u32(idx(rules.size()));\n177:     for (const auto& r : rules) o.str(r);\n178:     const auto& vocab = parser.getVocabulary();\n179:     size_t max_type = vocab.getMaxTokenType();\n180:     o.u32(idx(max_type + 1));\n181:     for (size_t i = 0; i <= max_type; ++i) o.str(vocab.getSymbolicName(i));\n182:     size_t count_at = o.buf.size();\n183:     o.u32(0);\n184:     uint32_t count = 0;\n185:     walk(tree, o, count);\n186:     for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i)\n187:       o.buf[count_at + i] = static_cast<uint8_t>(count >> (8 * i));\n188:     o.u32(idx(errors.errors.size()));\n189:     for (const auto& e : errors.errors) {\n190:       o.u32(e.line);\n191:       o.u32(e.column);\n192:       o.str(e.msg);\n193:     }\n194:     return hand_off(o.buf, out, out_len);\n195:   } catch (const std::exception& e) {\n196:     return message(e.what(), -2, out, out_len);\n197:   } catch (...) {\n198:     return message(\"unknown exception\", -3, out, out_len);\n199:   }\n200: }\n201: \n202: // Release a buffer jolt_parser_parse handed out. NULL is accepted.\n203: JOLT_EXPORT void jolt_parser_free(uint8_t* p) { std::free(p); }\n204: \n205: // TEST SEAM: set the fault mode (0 off, 1-3 as g_fault above) for later\n206: // parses. Answers the previous mode.\n207: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_inject_fault(int32_t mode) {\n208:   return g_fault.exchange(mode);\n209: }\n210: \n211: // The marshalling layout version this library writes.\n212: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_layout_version(void) { return 1; }"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"09a1f016-3e9e-4a06-86d2-3590bb503e6f"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Adversarial check against the new handler:\n\nmessage(): noexcept; sets *out=nullptr and *out_len=0; strlen on e.what() (const char*, doesn't throw; could e.what() itself theoretically throw? No, it's virtual noexcept). malloc/memcpy don't throw. g_fault.load() is atomic and doesn't throw. Returns status. `message` is noexcept, and if it were to throw → terminate, but nothing throws. Can `e.what()` on the exception caught inside catch(const std::exception& e) throw? By standard, what() is noexcept. No problem. No errno/strerror, no static buffer. Thread safety: g_fault is std::atomic — no problem; the message path only uses local state.\n\nRemaining paths where something could be thrown: the -2 handler calls message(e.what(),...) — safe. -3: message(\"unknown exception\",...) — safe. -4 path: hand_off inside try, no allocation on failure. -1: before the try. Good.\n\nOne nitpick: `throw std::bad_alloc()` inside case 1/3 — constructing bad_alloc doesn't allocate; no problem, and this is the test seam.\n\nFault injection sanity: the injection throws inside the actual try block of jolt_parser_parse, so the actual catch handler runs — not a mock. Mode 3 simulates malloc failure of the handler via the g_fault check inside message() — this is a seam inside the actual path (production malloc isn't hooked, but the NULL-p branch is the same branch that a real malloc failure would take). Sound as an exercise of the control flow; the real OOM behavior of malloc itself is not exercised (acceptable for a probe).\n\nRisk assessment of the always-compiled-in seam: an exported `jolt_parser_inject_fault` that any code within the process can call, which can deterministically break parsing (DoS-like, or masking of actual results). Threat model: process-local dylib, loaded by jolt, which is our own host; the symbol is exported (visible via nm) so another loaded library or the guest could call it. Severity: low for a feasibility probe (single-user local process), but it must not ship to a production/J8 package. Recommendation: gate it behind a CMake option (default OFF, e.g., `-DJOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAM=ON` that the probe build uses) or an exported symbol list so production builds don't include it; since there's a plan for an exported symbol list anyway, the simplest thing is: keep it compiled in for now but add a gating decision to the J0c checklist before any packaging. Also a caveat: could the guest (Clojure code) theoretically call this via ffi — but the guest is our own code. So: low severity, recommend gating before the J5/J8 milestone.\n\nNext, check the probe file and the note in the manifest. Read the tail of the probe (the new check), and grep for the F3 note in the manifest.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the probe's new checks and the manifest F3 note.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_17e2219255e24a2b88dfacf0","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"inject|injected|fault","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj","output_mode":"content","-n":true,"-C":10}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_1aeeae1907534195962f870c","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"Every target's|re-pin 