geId":"431d9717-4c39-43c7-8d28-d0bbe4fe9a4d"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All checks complete. Let me verify whether the fault injection logs exist: fix-probe.log, fix-probe-final.log, fix-gen-all.log. A quick directory check of the logs isn't strictly required, but it's preferable to have one. There's enough information; the orchestrator independently re-ran and confirmed 11 PASS.\n\nAdversarial evaluation of message(): noexcept, only strlen/malloc/memcpy; g_fault.load() is atomic; no static buffer, no strerror/errno, no thread-safety issues (only touches caller-provided pointers and the atomic variable). e.what() is virtual noexcept. The catch(...) path uses a string literal. The -1/-4 paths don't allocate. F1 CLOSED.\n\nFault injection validity: throws at the top of the actual try block → goes through the actual handler; mode 3 executes the *out=NULL branch inside message() (same branch as actual malloc failure). Not a mock of the handler itself. Sound. One caveat: mode 3 simulates malloc failure with a flag rather than actual allocation failure — the simulated branch is identical to the real one.\n\nSeam risk: in the production dylib, jolt_parser_inject_fault is exported — any in-process code can deterministically disable parsing or fake failures. Severity is low in a local single-process probe (attacker would need in-process code execution, and at that point they can do worse things); becomes problematic at packaging time (J5 local-build-only deploy, J8). Recommendation: keep it for now, but gate it with a CMake option (default OFF, probe build is ON), or exclude it via the planned export symbol list before J5 packaging; record the decision in the J0c checklist.\n\nF3: manifest.edn:22-24 has a note. Cross-target digest claim: according to the report, bb gen:antlr exits 0 (the generator throws on digest mismatch, so exit 0 proves all :generated digests match), and the target maps for java/js/dart match jolt-j0a in EDN. The base manifest digests I compared in round 1 were byte-for-byte identical; the current manifest retains them (in the earlier j0c manifest read I confirmed the same digests; the fix round only added comment lines 22-24 — this is consistent with the fix report which states manifest.edn was modified only for the F3 note). The 12 digests in the report's table match the base values I read (bb61a463..., 855f47de..., a29f3f8c..., e4c4693a...). CLOSED.\n\nVerdict: SIGNED OFF. Under 300 words.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 01:41:22 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 08:41:22 +07\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\n# Review: J0c round 1 fixes - confirmation\nRole: Reviewer (read-only) | Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max\n\n## Verdict: SIGNED OFF\n\n**F1: CLOSED.** jolt_parser.cpp:128-141: `message()` is `noexcept`, sets `*out=nullptr; *out_len=0` first, then only `strlen`/`malloc`/`memcpy` (plus one atomic load); on malloc failure the buffer stays NULL and only the status reports. Adversarial pass: `e.what()` is noexcept by standard; the `catch(...)` path passes a string literal; no `std::string`/`std::vector`, no `strerror`/errno, no static buffers, no per-thread state beyond the `std::atomic<int32_t>` seam; the `-1`/`-4` paths allocate nothing. Nothing throwable remains on any failure path. Header comment (:32-34) documents it.\n\n**Fault injection is sound, not a mock.** Modes 1/2 throw at the top of the real `try` in `jolt_parser_parse` (:152-157), so the actual catch handlers run; mode 3 exercises the same NULL-buffer branch inside `message()` a real malloc failure would take. Probe checks (jolt_parser_probe.clj:176-199) assert status -2 with `\"std::bad_alloc\"`, -3 with `\"unknown exception\"`, -2 with zero-length buffer, and a clean re-parse afterwards, each resetting the seam in `finally`. Orchestrator's independent 11-PASS run matches.\n\n**Seam risk: low now, gate before packaging.** `jolt_parser_inject_fault` is exported in the production dylib, so any in-process code can force fake parse failures. The threat model requires code already running in the process, which can do worse; for a local feasibility probe this is acceptable. Recommendation: before J5 packaging/J8, exclude it via the already-planned exported-symbols list or a CMake option defaulting OFF (probe build ON), and record the decision in the J0c checklist. Not a blocker this round.\n\n**F3: CLOSED.** manifest.edn:22-24 carries the coupling note verbatim. Cross-target claim holds: the `:java`/`:javascript`/`:dart` `:generated` digests are byte-identical to base jolt-j0a (verified against my round-1 read; report's 12-row table matches), `bb gen:antlr` exit 0 proves outputs match records (the generator throws on drift), and this round's manifest change is comment-only.\n\n**Not verified:** dylib SHA `00c27f06…` and `nm` output (no shell); fix logs beyond the orchestrator's re-run."}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792460620759,"messageId":"b8654f57-69d1-497f-9afc-fdf216d45747"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 09:10:22 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 16:10:22 +07\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\n# Task: review the platform-aware J0b probe and its Linux x86_64 evidence\nRole: Reviewer (read-only)\nImplementers:\n- Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max | Assigned: 2026-10-20 16:10:22 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: owner routing \"cmd for review\"; author claude-opus-5-5; resume the reviewer\n\nReview READ-ONLY: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/build/jolt-spike/j0b/udp_probe.clj and run.sh (platform-aware port; port report /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md) against your earlier J0b-era design reading (spec: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md section 8.3). Evidence: macOS arm64 runs build/jolt-spike/j0b/run-7..11.log (36 assertions pass, 3 records) and the REAL Linux x86_64 runs from shiva (Ubuntu 24.04.5, jolt 96d7b866, Chez 10.4.1): build/jolt-spike/j0b/shiva/j0b-shiva-1..3.log (37 assertions pass, 0 fail, 3 records, three identical results) plus j0a-smoke-shiva.log and j0a-bootstrap-shiva.log. The probe files on shiva are byte-identical to the local ones (sha256 verified by the orchestrator).\nCheck: (a) the Linux constants (EAGAIN 11, EMSGSIZE 90, ENOBUFS 105, EBADF 9, SO_SNDBUF 7, SO_RCVBUF 8, MSG_TRUNC 0x20, SOCK_DGRAM 2) and platform detection via jolt.socket.native/os: sound? any macOS value left hardcoded in a Linux path (or the reverse)? (b) the Linux-only assertion (SO_SNDBUF read-back at least twice the request): correct for Linux semantics (the log shows request 2048 read back 4608 = clamped minimum doubled) and honest, not a tautology; (c) whether the Linux log truly satisfies section 8.3 items 1-5 or only passes because expectations were relaxed per platform (compare the check labels in Linux and macOS logs; list any item the Linux run weakens or skips, e.g. natural would-block was NOT observed on either platform and rests on injection, short send injection-only); (d) does the report/run claim more than the logs show; (e) run.sh portability (POSIX sh, no macOS-only command on Linux) and that the header records enough to reproduce. Under 450 words, final response only, header fields, verdict SIGNED OFF / SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES / NOT SIGNED OFF, findings table (id, severity, file:line, evidence, correction), and what you could not verify."}],"meta":{"source":"user","createdAt":1792487428087,"messageId":"cb7c28f3-b45a-4e8a-96e1-43c8eb48ef19"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let's read spec §8.3, the probe file, run.sh, the port report, and the logs.\n\nFiles:\n- build/jolt-spike/j0b/udp_probe.clj\n- build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh\n- collab/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md\n- Spec §8.3 (lines 931–967)\n- Logs: run-7..11.log, shiva/j0b-shiva-1..3.log, j0a-smoke-shiva.log, j0a-bootstrap-shiva.log\n\nThere's a lot to read. I'll start with spec + probe + run.sh + report first.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I'll read the spec §8.3, the probe, run.sh, and the port report first.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_b02c9a17ce9b48d0b5f9f32c","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md","offset":931,"limit":37}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_2c8103f22715478ba15cb773","name":"read_file","input":{"paths":["/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh","/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md"]}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792487452609,"messageId":"5ca444d5-0f06-417c-88d2-e17d50accee0"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_b02c9a17ce9b48d0b5f9f32c","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 37 lines\nNote: showing lines 931-967. More of the file remains. Use offset=968 to continue.\n931: ### 8.3 J0b probe (owner addition, 2026-10-19)\n932: \n933: Separate macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 records are required. It establishes raw\n934: transport feasibility only, not datagram/DHT conformance.\n935: \n936: 1. Bind nonblocking SOCK_DGRAM loopback sockets through FFI and read back the\n937:    ephemeral port. Send/receive via sendto/recvfrom; compare payload bytes and\n938:    source address for zero-length, embedded-NUL, distinct consecutive and\n939:    limit-sized packets. One successful send submits one whole packet; one\n940:    successful receive consumes at most one packet. A short send is an error,\n941:    never success and never permission to send the rest as a second packet.\n942: 2. N = 1200, the landed default (V: `datagram.cljc:55-60`), plus one configured\n943:    larger N below the observed platform send maximum; receive buffers hold at\n944:    least N+1 bytes. Record platform limits and sysctls rather than assume them.\n945:    Verify SOCK_DGRAM and EMSGSIZE from headers (review-reported 2 on both,\n946:    EMSGSIZE 40 macOS / 90 Linux; R until checked). Above-platform-limit send\n947:    failure (EMSGSIZE) is tested separately and is an error, not a truncation.\n948:    Send N, N+1 and greater than the receive buffer; any length over N surfaces\n949:    an oversize/truncation error, never a successful prefix. If recvmsg/MSG_TRUNC\n950:    is used instead, bind and verify it on both platforms.\n951: 3. Send EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK (and macOS ENOBUFS) appear as a distinct raw result,\n952:    e.g. `:would-block`, with errno and no accepted packet. At the datagram\n953:    writer surface the outcome is the landed clean transport-error with reason\n954:    plus the send-failed event (§8.1). Where loopback cannot induce EAGAIN, use\n955:    deterministic native-seam injection, labelled separately from natural\n956:    observations, and also exercise real successful syscalls.\n957: 4. An empty receive waits through the poller, yielding the fiber instead of\n958:    spinning or blocking the scheduler; another fiber progresses; a packet wakes\n959:    the waiter. Send-side readiness waits and harness retries are probe\n960:    observations only, never synchronous `append!` behaviour or an adapter queue.\n961:    Test cancellation, close and cleanup.\n962: 5. Record toolchain/runtime SHA, architecture, commands, packet lengths and\n963:    counts, error/outcome traces, poller wakeup evidence and cleanup results. An\n964:    unavailable machine is an incomplete gate, not a pass. The Linux x86_64\n965:    machine is shiva (§10.2).\n966: \n967: ### 8.4 J7 integration"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_2c8103f22715478ba15cb773","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 2/2 files\n--- File Contents ---\n\n// File: build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh (shell)\n#!/bin/sh\n# J0b probe runner (foreground), macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64. POSIX sh\n# (Ubuntu's /bin/sh is dash). Run from the repository root.\n#\n#   JOLT       jolt binary. Default: build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt (J0a layout),\n#              else build/jolt-spike/jolt/bin/jolt (spike layout).\n#   JOLT_CHEZ  threaded Chez 10.x. Default: build/tools/jolt-env.sh's value when\n#              that file exists (J0a layout), else the mise 10.4.1 install path\n#              when present; otherwise left unset and jolt searches PATH.\ndir=$(cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\" && pwd)\n\nif [ -z \"$JOLT_CHEZ\" ] && [ -f build/tools/jolt-env.sh ]; then\n  . build/tools/jolt-env.sh\nfi\nif [ -z \"$JOLT\" ]; then\n  if [ -x build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt ]; then\n    JOLT=build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt\n  else\n    JOLT=build/jolt-spike/jolt/bin/jolt\n  fi\nfi\nif [ -z \"$JOLT_CHEZ\" ]; then\n  mise_chez=\"$HOME/.local/share/mise/installs/chezscheme/10.4.1/bin/scheme\"\n  [ -x \"$mise_chez\" ] && JOLT_CHEZ=$mise_chez\nfi\n[ -n \"$JOLT_CHEZ\" ] && export JOLT_CHEZ\n\njolt_home=$(cd \"$(dirname \"$JOLT\")/..\" 2>/dev/null && pwd)\necho \"jolt: $JOLT\"\necho \"jolt-sha: $(git -C \"$jolt_home\" rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null) dirty=$(git -C \"$jolt_home\" status --porcelain 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\"\nif [ -n \"$JOLT_CHEZ\" ]; then\n  echo \"chez: $JOLT_CHEZ $(\"$JOLT_CHEZ\" --version 2>&1)\"\nelse\n  echo \"chez: JOLT_CHEZ unset (jolt searches PATH)\"\nfi\n\ncase \"$(uname -s)\" in\n  Darwin)\n    echo \"uname: $(uname -sm) macOS $(sw_vers -productVersion 2>/dev/null)\"\n    echo \"sysctl: $(sysctl -n net.inet.udp.maxdgram net.inet.udp.recvspace kern.ipc.maxsockbuf 2>/dev/null | tr '\\n' ' ')(maxdgram recvspace maxsockbuf)\"\n    echo \"lo0 mtu: $(ifconfig lo0 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/.*mtu \\([0-9]*\\).*/\\1/p' | head -1)\"\n    ;;\n  Linux)\n    os=$(sed -n 's/^PRETTY_NAME=\"\\(.*\\)\"$/\\1/p' /etc/os-release 2>/dev/null)\n    echo \"uname: $(uname -sm) $os\"\n    sys=\"\"\n    for k in core/wmem_default core/wmem_max core/rmem_default core/rmem_max ipv4/udp_wmem_min ipv4/udp_rmem_min ipv4/udp_mem; do\n      sys=\"$sys net.$(echo $k | tr / .)=$(tr '\\t' ' ' < /proc/sys/net/$k 2>/dev/null)\"\n    done\n    echo \"sysctl:$sys\"\n    echo \"lo mtu: $(cat /sys/class/net/lo/mtu 2>/dev/null)\"\n    ;;\n  *)\n    echo \"uname: $(uname -sm) (unsupported platform for this probe)\"\n    ;;\nesac\n\n\"$JOLT\" \"$dir/udp_probe.clj\"\nrc=$?\necho \"exit: $rc\"\nexit $rc\n\n\n// File: collab/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md (markdown)\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-20 08:39:47 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 15:39:47 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nSession-ID: 8f45af4f-0557-4cfa-a375-78525ee6a40e\n# Findings: J0b UDP probe made platform-aware (macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64)\nRole: Stream & Network Engineer\nImplementers:\n- Model: claude-opus-5-5 | Assigned: 2026-10-20 15:39:47 +07 | Status: done (macOS verified; Linux NOT RUN by me) | Rationale: owner: \"push the jolt-j0a branch and run J0b on shiva\"\n\n**macOS behaviour is unchanged:** `SUMMARY 36 assertions: 36 pass, 0 fail; 3 records`, exit 0, on 5 foreground runs. The PASS/RECORD labels are identical to `run-6.log`.\n\n**The Linux expectations below are written but UNTESTED.** I cannot run on shiva.\n\nFiles touched: only `build/jolt-spike/j0b/udp_probe.clj`, `build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh`, and new logs `run-7..11.log`. `run-1..6.log` are kept. Nothing was staged.\n\n## What changed\n\n### `udp_probe.clj`\n\n1. **Platform selection.** The platform is `jolt.socket.native/os`, the same value jolt's socket layer and sockaddr helpers branch on (`:macos`, `:linux`, `:windows`, derived inside `native.clj`). The probe never reads `os.name` itself. The constants are one table per platform:\n\n| constant | `:macos` (MacOSX.sdk, as before) | `:linux` (shiva headers, orchestrator-verified) |\n|---|---|---|\n| SOCK_DGRAM / IPPROTO_UDP | 2 / 17 | 2 / 17 |\n| SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF | 0x1001 / 0x1002 | 7 / 8 |\n| MSG_TRUNC (unused) | 0x10 | 0x20 |\n| EAGAIN = EWOULDBLOCK | 35 | 11 |\n| EMSGSIZE | 40 | 90 |\n| ENOBUFS | 55 | 105 |\n| EBADF | 9 | 9 |\n\n   Any other platform throws at load. The header line prints `platform=`, the `os.name`/`os.arch` the shim gives (possibly nil on Linux) and the whole constant table. The sockaddr layout (macOS `sin_len` byte versus Linux 16-bit family) is already handled per platform by `native/make-sockaddr` and `sockaddr-*`, so nothing in the probe changed for it.\n2. **Constants flow through everywhere:**\n   - send-error classification: EAGAIN/ENOBUFS give `:would-block`, EMSGSIZE gives `:emsgsize`;\n   - recv `:would-block`;\n   - the injected-errno cases use the platform's EAGAIN and ENOBUFS (11 and 105 on Linux), and stay labelled `[injected]`;\n   - the EBADF check after close;\n   - the label of the empty-receive check now prints the platform's errno (still reads \"errno 35\" on macOS).\n3. **The platform maximum is measured, not assumed.** The bisection over [1, 65507] was already measured, and it is unchanged:\n   - macOS stops at 9216 (`maxdgram` = SO_SNDBUF);\n   - on Linux it should reach 65507, the IPv4 UDP payload limit.\n\n   The EMSGSIZE-above-max check uses the platform's EMSGSIZE. The \"configured larger N (8192) below the observed max\" check asserts `(< 1200 8192 max-ok)` against the measured value on both platforms.\n4. **SO_SNDBUF read-back.**\n   - **macOS (unchanged):** the requested values 2048 and 9216 are read back as RECORDs only, to keep the 36/3 counts.\n   - **Linux (one extra assertion):** `[linux] SO_SNDBUF read-back follows socket(7): >= 2 x requested`. It checks that both setsockopt calls answer 0 and that the read-back values are at least 4096 and at least 18432. Linux doubles the request for bookkeeping, clamps it at `net.core.wmem_max` (212992 on shiva, above both requests) and floors it at `SOCK_MIN_SNDBUF`. I chose \"≥ 2×\" so a floor above 2×2048 still passes, never equality.\n   - The RECORD lines still show the raw values on both platforms.\n5. **Natural would-block stays a RECORD** on both platforms (20000 × 1024-byte sends with no reader, histogram plus delivered count). Whatever Linux does is recorded, not asserted.\n6. **Comments:** sendto all-or-nothing now says (macOS, Linux); socklen_t is 32-bit unsigned on both.\n\n**Expected Linux totals: 37 assertions, 3 records** (the one extra Linux check).\n\n### `run.sh` (POSIX sh; checked with `sh -n` and run under `/bin/dash`)\n\n- **jolt binary:** `$JOLT`, else `build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt` (J0a layout), else `build/jolt-spike/jolt/bin/jolt` (spike layout). The probe path comes from the script's own directory, so it can be run from any checkout root.\n- **`JOLT_CHEZ`:**\n  1. env;\n  2. else source `build/tools/jolt-env.sh` when present (J0a layout);\n  3. else the mise 10.4.1 path if it exists;\n  4. else unset, and jolt searches PATH (a line says so).\n- **Prints:** the jolt path, jolt SHA and dirty count (git -C the jolt home), and the Chez version. Then, per OS:\n  - **Darwin:** `uname -sm`, `sw_vers`, sysctls `net.inet.udp.maxdgram`, `net.inet.udp.recvspace`, `kern.ipc.maxsockbuf`, and the lo0 MTU.\n  - **Linux:** `uname -sm`, the `/etc/os-release` PRETTY_NAME, `/proc/sys/net/core/{wmem_default,wmem_max,rmem_default,rmem_max}`, `/proc/sys/net/ipv4/{udp_wmem_min,udp_rmem_min,udp_mem}`, and `/sys/class/net/lo/mtu`.\n\n  No macOS-only command runs on Linux, and every probe of the environment tolerates a missing file.\n- **Ends with** `exit: N`.\n\n## macOS evidence (foreground, this Mac, jolt 96d7b866, Chez 10.4.1)\n\n| run | how | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `run-7.log`, `run-8.log`, `run-9.log` | `build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh` from the repo root (spike layout default) | exit 0, `SUMMARY 36 assertions: 36 pass, 0 fail; 3 records` |\n| `run-10-dash-j0a.log` | `JOLT=…/jolt-j0a/build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt dash build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh` | exit 0, 36/0/3 |\n| `run-11-j0a-default.log` | from the jolt-j0a worktree root with `JOLT`/`JOLT_CHEZ` unset (exercises the `build/tools/jolt` + `jolt-env.sh` default; that worktree stayed clean) | exit 0, 36/0/3 |\n\n- **Header** (`run-7.log`):\n  - `platform= :macos` with constants `{:so-sndbuf 4097 … :eagain 35 :emsgsize 40 :enobufs 55 :ebadf 9}` (0x1001 = 4097);\n  - sysctls `9216 786896 8388608`, lo0 MTU 16384;\n  - max sendable 9216, EMSGSIZE errno 40.\n- **Labels:** the PASS/FAIL/RECORD label sequence of `run-7.log` is identical to `run-6.log` (diff empty).\n\n## Linux x86_64 expectations, all UNTESTED (for the shiva run)\n\n1. **Platform and constants:**\n   - `native/os` answers `:linux` and the header shows the Linux constant table. If the shim's nil `os.name` made `native/os` fall to `:linux` on shiva, that is the right answer there.\n   - Bind, readback and sockaddr decoding work through the Linux sockaddr layout. This is jolt's code path and is unexercised by me.\n2. **Packet semantics (item 1):** the same assertions as macOS, with EAGAIN 11 on the empty receive.\n3. **Platform maximum (item 2):**\n   - The bisection should find **65507**, and 65508 should give EMSGSIZE errno **90** with nothing delivered.\n   - Big sends land in a 212992-byte receive buffer, which may drop some of the bisection's packets. The drain counts them; that is not asserted.\n   - Item 2 for N=1200 and N=8192 should pass unchanged (8192 < 65507).\n4. **SO_SNDBUF:** the read-back for 2048 is expected around 4608 (the doubled request, possibly floored at SOCK_MIN_SNDBUF), and 18432 for 9216. The `[linux]` assertion requires only ≥ 2× the request.\n5. **Natural would-block (RECORD):** Linux may or may not produce EAGAIN/ENOBUFS when blasting with a small SO_SNDBUF. Loopback frees the send buffer on delivery, so I expect `{:sent 20000}` again with silent receiver drops, but it is unknown.\n6. **Injected cases:** EAGAIN 11 and ENOBUFS 105 should give `:would-block` with no packet accepted.\n7. **Poller and fibers (item 4):** this runs on jolt's epoll backend, not kqueue. The parked waiter, wakeup, `cancel!`, `forget!`, close then EBADF 9, and the empty poller table are all unexercised on Linux by me.\n8. **`run.sh` on dash/Ubuntu:** the `/proc` and `/sys` reads and `/etc/os-release` parsing are untested on Linux. They were only shown not to run on Darwin. The script did run under `/bin/dash` on macOS.\n9. **Toolchain on shiva:** the J0a provisioning of Chez 10.4.1 and the jolt checkout is a precondition. `run.sh` uses `build/tools/jolt-env.sh` if bootstrap has run there.\n\n**Expected Linux total: 37 assertions + 3 records.** Any FAIL there is a real finding to report, not something to adjust the probe to.\n\nReport: `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md`\n\n"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"3ab3638d-8008-4a18-8a1d-4f98a1baef40"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Here I'll read the probe itself and the shiva log, plus one macOS log for label comparison. The probe is potentially large. Let me try reading udp_probe.clj along with shiva log 1 and run-7.log.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the probe source and the logs (one macOS, one shiva, plus the shiva J0a logs).\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_8cf92d1e7ce84f0ba50b73f9","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/build/jolt-spike/j0b/udp_probe.clj"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792487466617,"messageId":"d5fbd557-18f2-4a85-922f-bd5916fc8ce8"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_8cf92d1e7ce84f0ba50b73f9","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 355 lines\n1: ;; J0b: raw nonblocking SOCK_DGRAM over jolt.ffi on macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64.\n2: ;; Probe only: no datagram/DHT conformance. Run from the repo root with\n3: ;;   build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh\n4: (ns udp-probe\n5:   (:require [jolt.ffi :as ffi]\n6:             [jolt.socket.native :as native]\n7:             [jolt.io-poller :as poller]\n8:             [jolt.fibers :as fibers]\n9:             [clojure.string :as str]))\n10: \n11: ;; -- constants per platform ---------------------------------------------------\n12: ;; The platform is jolt.socket.native/os (:macos, :linux, :windows), the same\n13: ;; selection jolt's socket layer and sockaddr helpers use; os.name is never\n14: ;; read here (jolt's System/getProperty shim can answer nil).\n15: ;;\n16: ;; :macos from MacOSX.sdk: sys/socket.h:112,155,156,574; netinet/in.h:121;\n17: ;;   sys/errno.h:96,129-130,137,170.\n18: ;; :linux x86_64 from shiva's headers (asm-generic/errno-base.h, errno.h,\n19: ;;   x86_64-linux-gnu/asm/socket.h, bits/socket.h), checked by the\n20: ;;   orchestrator: EAGAIN = EWOULDBLOCK = 11.\n21: (def platform native/os)\n22: \n23: (def consts\n24:   (case platform\n25:     :macos {:sock-dgram 2 :ipproto-udp 17 :so-sndbuf 0x1001 :so-rcvbuf 0x1002\n26:             :msg-trunc 0x10 :eagain 35 :emsgsize 40 :enobufs 55 :ebadf 9}\n27:     :linux {:sock-dgram 2 :ipproto-udp 17 :so-sndbuf 7 :so-rcvbuf 8\n28:             :msg-trunc 0x20 :eagain 11 :emsgsize 90 :enobufs 105 :ebadf 9}\n29:     (throw (ex-info \"J0b probe: unsupported platform\" {:platform platform}))))\n30: \n31: (def SOCK-DGRAM (:sock-dgram consts))\n32: (def IPPROTO-UDP (:ipproto-udp consts))\n33: (def SO-SNDBUF (:so-sndbuf consts))\n34: (def SO-RCVBUF (:so-rcvbuf consts))\n35: (def MSG-TRUNC (:msg-trunc consts)) ; not used: N+1 sentinel instead\n36: (def EAGAIN (:eagain consts))\n37: (def EMSGSIZE (:emsgsize consts))\n38: (def ENOBUFS (:enobufs consts))\n39: (def EBADF (:ebadf consts))\n40: \n41: ;; ssize_t sendto(int, const void*, size_t, int, const struct sockaddr*, socklen_t)\n42: ;; ssize_t recvfrom(int, void*, size_t, int, struct sockaddr*, socklen_t*)\n43: ;; socklen_t is 32-bit unsigned on both (arm/_types.h:117; glibc\n44: ;; __socklen_t is __U32_TYPE). Not :blocking: the fd is\n45: ;; nonblocking, so neither call can park the carrier.\n46: (ffi/defcfn c-sendto \"sendto\" [:int :pointer :size_t :int :pointer :uint] :ssize_t\n47:   {:capture-native-error true})\n48: (ffi/defcfn c-recvfrom \"recvfrom\" [:int :pointer :size_t :int :pointer :pointer] :ssize_t\n49:   {:capture-native-error true})\n50: \n51: ;; -- injection seam -------------------------------------------------------------\n52: ;; The probe's sendto goes through this var. Injected runs rebind it and every\n53: ;; observation they produce is tagged :injected, never :natural.\n54: (def ^:dynamic *sendto* c-sendto)\n55: \n56: (defn- classify-send-error [e]\n57:   (cond (or (= e EAGAIN) (= e ENOBUFS)) :would-block\n58:         (= e EMSGSIZE) :emsgsize\n59:         :else :error))\n60: \n61: ;; -- raw surface ------------------------------------------------------------------\n62: (defn udp-open\n63:   \"A nonblocking, close-on-exec SOCK_DGRAM fd bound to 127.0.0.1:0.\"\n64:   []\n65:   (let [[fd e] (native/c-socket native/af-inet SOCK-DGRAM IPPROTO-UDP)]\n66:     (when (neg? fd) (throw (ex-info \"socket failed\" {:errno e})))\n67:     (native/close-on-exec! fd)\n68:     (let [[r e] (native/set-blocking! fd false)]\n69:       (when (neg? r) (throw (ex-info \"O_NONBLOCK failed\" {:errno e}))))\n70:     (let [[sa len] (native/make-sockaddr native/af-inet \"127.0.0.1\" 0)]\n71:       (try\n72:         (let [[r e] (native/c-bind fd sa len)]\n73:           (when (neg? r) (throw (ex-info \"bind failed\" {:errno e}))))\n74:         (finally (ffi/free sa))))\n75:     fd))\n76: \n77: (defn send!\n78:   \"One sendto of the whole byte array to addr ({:ip :port}). Answers\n79:    {:result :sent :n} | {:result :would-block :errno} | {:result :emsgsize :errno}\n80:    | {:result :error :errno} | {:result :short-send :n :len}. A short send is an\n81:    error and is never completed by a second packet.\"\n82:   [fd ^bytes payload {:keys [ip port]}]\n83:   (let [len (alength payload)\n84:         buf (ffi/alloc (max 1 len))\n85:         [sa salen] (native/make-sockaddr native/af-inet ip port)]\n86:     (try\n87:       (when (pos? len) (ffi/write-array buf payload))\n88:       (let [[n e] (*sendto* fd buf len 0 sa salen)]\n89:         (cond\n90:           (neg? n) {:result (classify-send-error e) :errno e}\n91:           (not= n len) {:result :short-send :n n :len len}\n92:           :else {:result :sent :n n}))\n93:       (finally (ffi/free buf) (ffi/free sa)))))\n94: \n95: (defn recv!\n96:   \"One recvfrom with a buffer of limit+1 bytes. Answers\n97:    {:result :packet :bytes :from} | {:result :oversize :received :limit :from}\n98:    | {:result :would-block :errno} | {:result :error :errno}. A packet longer\n99:    than limit is an error; its prefix is never returned.\"\n100:   [fd limit]\n101:   (let [cap (inc limit)\n102:         buf (ffi/alloc cap)\n103:         [sa lenp] (native/alloc-sockaddr)]\n104:     (try\n105:       (let [[n e] (c-recvfrom fd buf cap 0 sa lenp)]\n106:         (cond\n107:           (neg? n) {:result (if (= e EAGAIN) :would-block :error) :errno e}\n108:           :else\n109:           (let [from {:ip (native/sockaddr-ip sa) :port (native/sockaddr-port sa)}]\n110:             (if (> n limit)\n111:               {:result :oversize :received n :limit limit :from from}\n112:               {:result :packet :bytes (if (zero? n) (byte-array 0) (ffi/read-array buf n))\n113:                :from from}))))\n114:       (finally (ffi/free buf) (ffi/free sa) (ffi/free lenp)))))\n115: \n116: (defn local [fd] (native/local-address fd))\n117: \n118: (defn close! [fd]\n119:   (poller/cancel! fd)\n120:   (poller/forget! fd)\n121:   (native/c-close fd))\n122: \n123: ;; -- harness ------------------------------------------------------------------------\n124: (def results (atom []))\n125: \n126: (defn check [item label ok? detail]\n127:   (swap! results conj {:item item :label label :ok? (boolean ok?) :detail detail})\n128:   (println (if ok? \"PASS\" \"FAIL\") item label (pr-str detail)))\n129: \n130: (defn record\n131:   \"An observation that cannot fail: printed as RECORD, never counted as PASS.\"\n132:   [item label detail]\n133:   (swap! results conj {:item item :label label :record? true :detail detail})\n134:   (println \"RECORD\" item label (pr-str detail)))\n135: \n136: (defn drain!\n137:   \"recv! until :would-block; answers the packet count. A :error result is a\n138:    FAIL and stops the drain instead of looping on it.\"\n139:   [item fd limit]\n140:   (loop [n 0]\n141:     (let [r (recv! fd limit)]\n142:       (case (:result r)\n143:         :would-block n\n144:         :error (do (check item \"drain stopped on recv error\" false r) n)\n145:         (recur (inc n))))))\n146: \n147: (defn ->bytes [xs] (byte-array (map unchecked-byte xs)))\n148: (defn pattern [n seed] (->bytes (map #(bit-and (+ seed (* 31 %)) 0xff) (range n))))\n149: \n150: (defn recv-wait\n151:   \"recv! until a non-would-block result, waiting on the poller between tries.\n152:    Counts the wakeups. Thread or fiber, whichever the caller is.\"\n153:   [fd limit]\n154:   (loop [waits 0]\n155:     (let [r (recv! fd limit)]\n156:       (if (= :would-block (:result r))\n157:         (do (poller/wait-ready fd :read) (recur (inc waits)))\n158:         (assoc r :waits waits)))))\n159: \n160: (defn same-bytes? [a b] (= (vec a) (vec b)))\n161: \n162: ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n163: ;; Item 1: bind, readback, whole-packet semantics\n164: (defn item-1 [a b N]\n165:   (let [la (local a) lb (local b)\n166:         to-b {:ip \"127.0.0.1\" :port (:port lb)}]\n167:     (check 1 \"bound ports read back\" (and (pos? (:port la)) (pos? (:port lb))\n168:                                           (not= (:port la) (:port lb)))\n169:            {:a la :b lb})\n170:     (check 1 \"empty socket answers :would-block\" (= :would-block (:result (recv! b N)))\n171:            (recv! b N))\n172:     (doseq [[label payload] [[\"zero-length\" (byte-array 0)]\n173:                              [\"embedded-NUL\" (->bytes [0 1 0 0 255 0 65 0])]\n174:                              [\"limit-sized N\" (pattern N 7)]]]\n175:       (let [s (send! a payload to-b)\n176:             r (recv-wait b N)]\n177:         (check 1 (str label \" round-trip\")\n178:                (and (= :sent (:result s)) (= (alength payload) (:n s))\n179:                     (= :packet (:result r)) (same-bytes? payload (:bytes r))\n180:                     (= (:from r) {:ip \"127.0.0.1\" :port (:port la)}))\n181:                {:len (alength payload) :send s :recv (dissoc r :bytes)\n182:                 :recv-len (some-> (:bytes r) alength)})))\n183:     ;; distinct consecutive packets: each receive consumes exactly one\n184:     (let [ps (mapv #(pattern (+ 10 %) %) (range 5))\n185:           sends (mapv #(send! a % to-b) ps)\n186:           recvs (vec (repeatedly 5 #(recv-wait b N)))\n187:           after (recv! b N)]\n188:       (check 1 \"5 consecutive packets, one per receive, in order, then empty\"\n189:              (and (every? #(= :sent (:result %)) sends)\n190:                   (= (mapv vec ps) (mapv (comp vec :bytes) recvs))\n191:                   (= :would-block (:result after)))\n192:              {:lens (mapv alength ps) :recv-lens (mapv (comp alength :bytes) recvs)\n193:               :after after}))\n194:     ;; short send: sendto on SOCK_DGRAM is all-or-nothing (macOS, Linux); a short\n195:     ;; count cannot be produced naturally, so inject one through the seam.\n196:     (let [p (pattern 100 3)\n197:           s (binding [*sendto* (fn [fd buf len fl sa sl] [(dec len) 0])]\n198:               (send! a p to-b))\n199:           r (recv! b N)]\n200:       (check 1 \"[injected] short send surfaces :short-send, nothing completes it\"\n201:              (and (= :short-send (:result s)) (= :would-block (:result r)))\n202:              {:send s :recv-after r}))))\n203: \n204: ;; Item 2: sizes, EMSGSIZE, oversize\n205: (defn item-2 [a b N]\n206:   (let [to-b {:ip \"127.0.0.1\" :port (:port (local b))}]\n207:     (doseq [[label len expect] [[\"N\" N :packet] [\"N+1\" (inc N) :oversize]\n208:                                 [\"N+100 (> recv buffer N+1)\" (+ N 100) :oversize]]]\n209:       (let [p (pattern len 11)\n210:             s (send! a p to-b)\n211:             r (recv-wait b N)]\n212:         (check 2 (str \"N=\" N \" send \" label \" (\" len \" bytes) -> \" expect)\n213:                (and (= :sent (:result s)) (= expect (:result r))\n214:                     (if (= expect :packet) (same-bytes? p (:bytes r)) (not (contains? r :bytes))))\n215:                {:send s :recv (dissoc r :bytes)})))\n216:     ;; the oversize packet was consumed whole: nothing of it is left\n217:     (check 2 (str \"N=\" N \" oversize consumed whole, queue empty\") (= :would-block (:result (recv! b N)))\n218:            (recv! b N))))\n219: \n220: (defn emsgsize [a b]\n221:   (let [to-b {:ip \"127.0.0.1\" :port (:port (local b))}\n222:         sndbuf (native/get-int-option a native/sol-socket SO-SNDBUF)\n223:         rcvbuf (native/get-int-option b native/sol-socket SO-RCVBUF)\n224:         probe (fn [len] (send! a (pattern len 1) to-b))\n225:         ;; largest len that sends, by bisection over [1, 65507]\n226:         ;; (65507 is the IPv4 UDP payload limit; macOS stops lower, at\n227:         ;; net.inet.udp.maxdgram / SO_SNDBUF)\n228:         max-ok (loop [lo 1 hi 65507]\n229:                  (if (>= lo hi) lo\n230:                      (let [mid (quot (+ lo hi 1) 2)]\n231:                        (if (= :sent (:result (probe mid))) (recur mid hi) (recur lo (dec mid))))))\n232:         over (probe (inc max-ok))]\n233:     ;; drain what the bisection sent\n234:     (drain! 2 b 65536)\n235:     (check 2 \"above platform max: sendto fails EMSGSIZE (an error, nothing sent)\"\n236:            (and (= :emsgsize (:result over)) (= EMSGSIZE (:errno over))\n237:                 (= :would-block (:result (recv! b 65536))))\n238:            {:so-sndbuf sndbuf :so-rcvbuf rcvbuf :max-sendable max-ok\n239:             :send-max+1 over})\n240:     max-ok))\n241: \n242: ;; Item 3: would-block\n243: (defn item-3 [a b]\n244:   (let [to-b {:ip \"127.0.0.1\" :port (:port (local b))}\n245:         ;; natural: blast sends with nothing reading and a small SO_SNDBUF\n246:         set-r (native/set-int-option! a native/sol-socket SO-SNDBUF 2048)\n247:         sndbuf-actual (native/get-int-option a native/sol-socket SO-SNDBUF)\n248:         p (pattern 1024 5)\n249:         outcomes (frequencies (map #(select-keys % [:result :errno])\n250:                                    (repeatedly 20000 #(send! a p to-b))))\n251:         nat-wb (some #(when (= :would-block (:result (key %))) %) outcomes)]\n252:     (record 3 \"[natural] 20000 x 1024B sends, SO_SNDBUF requested 2048, no reader: outcome histogram\"\n253:             {:setsockopt set-r :so-sndbuf-read-back sndbuf-actual\n254:              :outcomes outcomes :would-block-observed? (boolean nat-wb)})\n255:     (record 3 \"[natural] drained receiver\" {:packets-delivered (drain! 3 b 2048)})\n256:     (let [r (native/set-int-option! a native/sol-socket SO-SNDBUF 9216)\n257:           restored (native/get-int-option a native/sol-socket SO-SNDBUF)]\n258:       (record 3 \"SO_SNDBUF restored\" {:setsockopt r :so-sndbuf-read-back restored})\n259:       ;; Linux documents socket(7): the kernel doubles the requested SO_SNDBUF\n260:       ;; (bookkeeping overhead), clamped by net.core.wmem_max and floored at\n261:       ;; SOCK_MIN_SNDBUF; getsockopt answers the doubled value. So the Linux\n262:       ;; assertion is \"at least twice the request (both requests are below\n263:       ;; wmem_max 212992 on shiva)\", never equality. macOS keeps its read-back\n264:       ;; as a RECORD (unchanged: requested value read back as set).\n265:       (when (= platform :linux)\n266:         (check 3 \"[linux] SO_SNDBUF read-back follows socket(7): >= 2 x requested\"\n267:                (and (zero? (first set-r)) (>= sndbuf-actual (* 2 2048))\n268:                     (zero? (first r)) (>= restored (* 2 9216)))\n269:                {:requested [2048 9216] :read-back [sndbuf-actual restored]})))\n270:     ;; injected: the seam answers -1 with each errno; nothing reaches the peer\n271:     (doseq [e [EAGAIN ENOBUFS]]\n272:       (let [s (binding [*sendto* (fn [& _] [-1 e])] (send! a p to-b))]\n273:         (check 3 (str \"[injected] sendto -1/errno \" e \" -> :would-block, no packet accepted\")\n274:                (and (= {:result :would-block :errno e} s) (= :would-block (:result (recv! b 2048))))\n275:                {:send s})))\n276:     ;; real successful syscall after the injected ones\n277:     (let [s (send! a p to-b) r (recv-wait b 2048)]\n278:       (check 3 \"[natural] real sendto/recvfrom succeed after injection\"\n279:              (and (= :sent (:result s)) (same-bytes? p (:bytes r))) {:send s :recv-len (some-> (:bytes r) alength)}))\n280:     ;; receive side would-block is natural on loopback\n281:     (let [r (recv! b 2048)]\n282:       (check 3 (str \"[natural] recvfrom on empty socket -> :would-block errno \" EAGAIN)\n283:              (= {:result :would-block :errno EAGAIN} r) r))))\n284: \n285: ;; Item 4: poller, fibers, cancellation, close\n286: (defn item-4 [a b N]\n287:   (let [to-b {:ip \"127.0.0.1\" :port (:port (local b))}\n288:         trace (atom [])\n289:         note! (fn [x] (swap! trace conj [(long (poller/clock-ms)) x]))\n290:         ticks (atom 0)\n291:         waiter (fibers/spawn (fn [] (note! :waiter-start)\n292:                                (let [r (recv-wait b N)] (note! [:waiter-got (:result r) (:waits r)]) r)))\n293:         worker (fibers/spawn (fn []\n294:                                (dotimes [i 50] (swap! ticks inc) (Thread/sleep 2))\n295:                                (note! [:worker-ticked @ticks :waiter-state (fibers/state waiter)])\n296:                                (note! [:worker-sends (send! a (pattern 77 9) to-b)])))\n297:         _ (fibers/join worker)\n298:         r (fibers/join waiter 5000 :timeout)]\n299:     (check 4 \"empty receive parks the fiber; another fiber progresses; packet wakes the waiter\"\n300:            (and (map? r) (= :packet (:result r)) (= 77 (alength (:bytes r)))\n301:                 (>= (:waits r) 1)\n302:                 (some #(= [:worker-ticked 50 :waiter-state :parked] (second %)) @trace))\n303:            {:trace @trace :recv (dissoc r :bytes)}))\n304:   ;; cancellation: a parked waiter is released by cancel!, takes no packet\n305:   (let [c (udp-open)\n306:         out (atom nil)\n307:         f (fibers/spawn (fn [] (poller/wait-ready c :read)\n308:                           (reset! out {:after-wake (recv! c N)}) :released))\n309:         _ (Thread/sleep 50)\n310:         st (fibers/state f)\n311:         _ (poller/cancel! c)\n312:         jr (fibers/join f 2000 :timeout)]\n313:     (check 4 \"cancel! releases a parked receive; retry is :would-block (no packet)\"\n314:            (and (= st :parked) (= jr :released) (= :would-block (get-in @out [:after-wake :result])))\n315:            {:state-before-cancel st :join jr :out @out})\n316:     ;; a wait on a cancelled fd returns at once\n317:     (let [t0 (poller/clock-ms) _ (poller/wait-ready c :read) dt (- (poller/clock-ms) t0)]\n318:       (check 4 \"wait on cancelled fd returns immediately\" (< dt 100) {:ms dt}))\n319:     (poller/forget! c)\n320:     (let [[r e] (native/c-close c)]\n321:       (check 4 \"close after forget!\" (zero? r) {:close [r e]}))\n322:     (let [r (recv! c N)]\n323:       (check 4 \"recvfrom on closed fd is an error (EBADF), not a packet/would-block\"\n324:              (= {:result :error :errno EBADF} r) r))\n325:     (check 4 \"poller table holds no entry for the closed fd\"\n326:            (not (contains? (:fds (poller/debug-state)) c)) {:debug (poller/debug-state)})))\n327: \n328: (defn -main []\n329:   (println \"jolt UDP probe, platform=\" platform \"os.name=\" (System/getProperty \"os.name\")\n330:            \"os.arch=\" (System/getProperty \"os.arch\") \"constants=\" (pr-str consts))\n331:   (let [a (udp-open) b (udp-open)\n332:         max-ok (emsgsize a b)\n333:         big (- (* 8 1024) 0)]  ; configured larger N, below max-ok\n334:     (println \"platform max sendable =\" max-ok \"configured larger N =\" big)\n335:     (check 2 \"configured larger N is below the observed platform send maximum\"\n336:            (< 1200 big max-ok) {:N 1200 :larger-N big :max-sendable max-ok})\n337:     (item-1 a b 1200)\n338:     (item-2 a b 1200)\n339:     (item-1 a b big)\n340:     (item-2 a b big)\n341:     (item-3 a b)\n342:     (item-4 a b 1200)\n343:     (let [ra (close! a) rb (close! b)]\n344:       (check 5 \"cleanup: both sockets closed\" (and (zero? (first ra)) (zero? (first rb)))\n345:              {:a ra :b rb})\n346:       (let [d (poller/debug-state)]\n347:         (check 5 \"cleanup: poller table has no fd, pending or waiter left\"\n348:                (and (empty? (:fds d)) (empty? (:pending d))) {:debug d}))))\n349:   (let [rs @results]\n350:     (let [asserts (remove :record? rs) recs (filter :record? rs)]\n351:       (println \"SUMMARY\" (count asserts) \"assertions:\" (count (filter :ok? asserts)) \"pass,\"\n352:                (count (remove :ok? asserts)) \"fail;\" (count recs) \"records\")\n353:       (System/exit (if (every? :ok? asserts) 0 1)))))\n354: \n355: (-main)"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"2aef8b6d-d36e-40fa-abb3-5fde9af723f5"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Reading the logs: shiva j0b-shiva-1.log (and verify whether 2 and 3 are identical), run-7.log for macOS, j0a-smoke-shiva.log, j0a-bootstrap-shiva.log. Also run-11 might be needed. Let's read the shiva-1, run-7, and j0a shiva logs. These logs could be long (36 assertions + records). Reading them.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the logs: shiva run 1, macOS run-7, and the shiva J0a logs.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_5bbbf301e03e4f9fbd9d8178","name