c. Not a silent pass; acceptable but an unfriendly error. Recommend either removing the spike fallback (tracked-tree task) or emitting an explicit \"run bb bootstrap:jolt\" error. Keep-or-remove: remove in the tracked version; the spike layout won't exist in a clean checkout. LOW/info.\n\nAlso: `sh src/dev/jolt_udp_probe.sh` in the bb task — the script needs to be run from the repo root (relative env file path); bb tasks run in cwd = project root. No problem. Script mode 755 is preserved but invoked via `sh` — no problem. No tracked logs/artifacts: author's git status shows only 3 changes; build/ is ignored ✓.\n\nBranch 1 evaluation summary:\n- Ordering: check-os-packages is the first step of -main (:172), before check-chez ✓. Note: the toolchain slurp happens before that, no problem.\n- No sudo/apt execution ✓ (strings are only in failure messages; dpkg-query is read-only).\n- Debian detection: /etc/os-release's ID/ID_LIKE, word-split; correct on Ubuntu (ID_LIKE=debian), Debian, Mint; the regex handles quoted/unquoted. Edge cases: os-release lines like `ID=\"debian\"` are fine. False positive if ID_LIKE contains debian as one of multiple words — that's intended (family). Sound.\n- Seam risk: the env var can force the Debian path on any host, and could fake \"installed\" in production if leaked. Mitigated: prints \"[test seam]\" (visible, not silent), default is unset → real path; fail-closed direction on Debian? Actual risk direction: seam set to all 4 packages in a CI env → checks pass without dpkg on a real Debian missing packages. Low likelihood; recommend later gating the seam to non-zero-exit or a bb task flag, or at minimum the current logging. LOW.\n- dpkg-installed? requires exact \"install ok installed\" — libssl3's \"deinstall ok config-files\" is treated as not installed: correct strictness.\n- Required packages: libncurses-dev is justified (evidence from shiva); gcc/make are reasonable (Chez's source build compiles/links C; presence on shiva discloses that we haven't independently proven the absence); git is obvious. OK, honest.\n- libssl3 finding: confirm the orchestrator's finding. On Ubuntu 24.04 (noble), due to the time_t 64 transition, libcrypto.so.3 is provided by libssl3t64; libssl3 is a removed/transitional package that shows as \"deinstall ok config-files\". Recommendation: don't enumerate release-specific package names in :unverified; declare the requirement as soname/library (libcrypto.so.3 + version check, which §6.3 already preserves), and in J1 either resolve the provider package on failure via `dpkg -S` / `apt-file`, or add a per-release map {:ubuntu/noble \"libssl3t64\" :debian/* \"libssl3\"}. Since :unverified is unchecked documentation, the minimal fix: annotate the entry as \"libssl3 (pre-noble Ubuntu/Debian; libssl3t64 on Ubuntu 24.04+)\" — or replace with a library-based description. Severity LOW (docs-only field, not checked).\n- Design doc: the change log cites both of the owner's sentences verbatim ✓; §6.1 paragraph + bullets are consistent; the Linux bullets in §6.3 preserve the path/version check and Q4 (\"the above path and version checks are preserved\") ✓ nothing contradictory; new lines in §13.1. Consistent with the owner's wording. One nuance: owner's wording \"lets assume debian distribution and apt get for dependency management of package\" — the doc interprets as Debian family + apt and records the interpretation. No problem.\n\nAnything else? The check message shows \"all 4 required Debian packages installed: gcc git libncurses-dev make\" and matches.\n\nClean-checkout concerns branch 1: check-os-packages reads jolt-toolchain.edn — present. No problem.\n\nVerdict: branch jolt-j0a: SIGNED OFF (with advisory on libssl3 naming — :unverified and unchecked so LOW; the orchestrator asks \"state how the declaration should name per-release packages, or whether we should check the library itself\"). jolt-j0b: SIGNED OFF (recommend removing the fallback, info).\n\nShould libssl3 force \"SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES\"? It's a known finding, an entry in the unchecked docs list. The orchestrator explicitly asks for a recommendation on the fix. I could sign j0a off with changes… the entry is already annotated \"package name per release\". Given that it's :unverified and never checked, and that J1 owns the libcrypto gate, SIGNED OFF with the recommendation is appropriate; the annotation update can ride along with J1. But the orchestrator asks to \"confirm\" the finding — confirmed, severity LOW, fix recommended before J1 or now (one-line comment). Go with SIGNED OFF for both branches and enumerate the findings.\n\nHmm, the seam too: assessment — acceptable (visible, opt-in, default is the real path), recommend removal/gating before packaging in J8. INFO.\n\nWord limit 500. Writing.\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 09:56:10 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 16:56:10 +07\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\n# Review: apt prerequisites (jolt-j0a) + tracked J0b probe (jolt-j0b)\nRole: Reviewer (read-only) | Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max\n\n## Verdicts: jolt-j0a SIGNED OFF · jolt-j0b SIGNED OFF\n\n## Findings\n\n| id | sev | branch | file:line | evidence | correction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | low | j0a | jolt-toolchain.edn:55-56 | Confirmed: `\"libssl3\"` is wrong for Ubuntu 24.04 - noble's t64 transition moved `libcrypto.so.3` to `libssl3t64`; `libssl3` there is `deinstall ok config-files`, which `dpkg-installed?` (jolt_bootstrap.clj:138-142, exact `install ok installed`) would correctly reject if it were ever promoted to `:required`. Harmless today (`:unverified`, never checked). | Declare the requirement as the **library**, not the package: soname `libcrypto.so.3` + the §6.3 path/version check (already retained), resolving the provider at failure time via `dpkg -S`/apt. If a package name is kept, make it per-release (`libssl3t64` on Ubuntu ≥24.04 / Debian trixie, `libssl3` otherwise). Do this when J1 promotes the entry, and fix the comment now if convenient. |\n| F2 | info | j0a | jolt_bootstrap.clj:148-154 | Seam assessed: `JOLT_BOOTSTRAP_TEST_DEBIAN_INSTALLED` can force the Debian path with a fake installed set in production, but every use prints `[test seam]` (never silent), the default is the real dpkg path, and it fails closed on a real Debian host when unset. Acceptable for the probe era. | Drop or flag-gate the seam before J8 packaging. |\n| F3 | info | j0b | jolt_udp_probe.sh:17-21 | Spike-layout fallback (`build/jolt-spike/jolt/bin/jolt`) is dead in a tracked clean checkout; if neither layout exists the shell fails with \"not found\" (nonzero, not silent, but cryptic). | Drop the fallback in the tracked script; fail with \"run bb bootstrap:jolt first\". Not a blocker. |\n\n## Checks\n\n**(1) jolt-j0a.** Ordering: `check-os-packages` is the first step of `-main` (:171-172), before any `mise exec` ✓. No sudo/apt execution: both appear only inside the `fail!` message text; the only external command is read-only `dpkg-query` ✓. Detection (debian-family?, :124-135): word-split of `ID`/`ID_LIKE` from `/etc/os-release`, quoted or unquoted - correct for Debian/Ubuntu/Mint, false for Fedora (author's parse log agrees); non-Debian hosts print one skip line ✓. Required set justified: `libncurses-dev` has real shiva failure evidence; `gcc`/`make` are honestly labelled as inferred from the same Chez source build (present on shiva, absence not independently shown); `git` is used by the code ✓. Design-doc amendment: change note \"fourth\" (:70-79) quotes both owner sentences verbatim; §6.1 (:518-529, :541-542), §6.3 (:599-603) and the §13.1 row (:1478-1481) match the owner's words, retain Q4 and the libcrypto path/version check, and remove nothing ✓. Seam-negative evidence (missing `libncurses-dev`, exact `sudo apt-get install -y` command, failure before the Chez line) matches the code paths; orchestrator's real-Debian run on shiva covers the path the author could not.\n\n**(2) jolt-j0b.** I compared the tracked probe against the scratch original myself: same 355-line structure, item-4 region byte-identical (grep of :291-307 matches exactly), differences confined to lines 3-4 (comment + `ns jolt-udp-probe`, matching the filename convention) - consistent with the author's `3,4c3,4` diff claim; script differs only in the probe filename (:58). No logic touched, so my prior J0b review carries over unchanged. bb wiring (:18-21) propagates the exit code exactly like `smoke:jolt`; the script's repo-root-relative env sourcing is fine under bb (cwd = project root); clean checkout needs `bb bootstrap:jolt` first (doc'd, fails nonzero otherwise). No logs/artifacts tracked: only ` M bb.edn` + two new `src/dev` files; `build/` ignored. Orchestrator's macOS re-run: 36/36/3, exit 0, matching the reviewed probe's macOS total.\n\n## Could not verify\n\n- Full byte-level `diff` of the two probe files (no shell); my comparison is structural plus targeted byte-exact greps and the identical orchestrator totals.\n- shiva real-Debian-path logs and the `dpkg -S libssl3t64` output (orchestrator evidence, trusted).\n- Mode bits (755) on jolt_udp_probe.sh; git statuses of both worktrees beyond the reports."}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792490384473,"messageId":"fc96e2ea-a5f0-49b5-a8f0-9f3bf7787ea6"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 10:12:02 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 17:12:02 +07\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\n# Task: review J0c round 2 (corpus parity, jolt cst adapter, exporter entry)\nRole: Reviewer (read-only)\nImplementers:\n- Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max | Assigned: 2026-10-20 17:12:02 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: owner routing \"cmd for review\"; author claude-opus-5-5\n\nREAD-ONLY review of the UNCOMMITTED round-2 change in /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c (branch jolt-j0c, HEAD fd0adf80): modified native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp, src/jolt/README.md; new src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt, src/dev/jolt_j0c_oracle.clj, src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj, src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub/yang/python/antlr/parser.clj, test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_corpus.edn and jolt_j0c_oracle.edn. Report: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792489765000-engineer-jolt-j0c-r2.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md; spec: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md sections 7.3 to 7.8. The orchestrator re-ran: oracle regenerates (51 entries, 40 probe texts), native rebuild exit 0, parity run \"5 diverged, 0 failed\", all 51 completeness results and 40 probe answers match. The standard for the 5 syntax-error divergences is being ruled by the Architect separately: DO NOT review that question; review correctness and honesty of the work.\nCheck: (a) ORACLE INTEGRITY: is the oracle generated only from the repo's existing JVM path, never from jolt output; is the order of events (oracle digest recorded before the first jolt run) credible; could the corpus have been tuned to pass; is the corpus faithful to design 7.8 (arithmetic, nested indent/dedent, empty/EOF, non-ASCII and astral, malformed, incomplete multiline, triple quotes, open brackets, early error under an open bracket) and does it avoid ints beyond 64 bits and floats (W8); (b) the new native entry jolt_parser_export and cst.jolt: exception containment preserved (nonallocating failure paths, fault-injection tests still green), buffer ownership, validation of the native buffer before the shared exporter sees it, a failed parse throws and never yields a packet, no pointer or C++ object visible to guest code; (c) the first-failure rule: the author says the C++ token buffer fills lazily like Java's so the adapter uses the JVM rule, and that the JS/Dart rule would be wrong for \"f(1,\\n\": is that evidence solid; (d) the probe stand-in (src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub/...parser.clj): does it make the probe run through the real shared code or could it hide a gap; (e) the experiment that temporarily patched the generated C++ helper: was it fully reverted (digests, byte-identical library claim; verify the manifest digests and the generated files against the recorded ones); (f) is the report honest about what is NOT done. Under 600 words, final response only, header fields, verdict SIGNED OFF / SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES / NOT SIGNED OFF, findings table (id, severity, file:line, evidence, correction), what you could not verify."}],"meta":{"source":"user","createdAt":1792491131225,"messageId":"db8e1a1a-a66a-4f7d-88d8-92dbf6352a96"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a larger review. First read the report, then read the new files. Files:\n\n- collab/1792489765000-engineer-jolt-j0c-r2.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md\n- native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp (has changes — new export entries)\n- src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt (new)\n- src/dev/jolt_j0c_oracle.clj\n- src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj\n- src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub/yang/python/antlr/parser.clj\n- test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_corpus.edn\n- test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_oracle.edn\n- src/jolt/README.md (has changes)\n\nStarting with the report first.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I'll start with the author's report, then the changed/new files.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_173d140c8d42472c874949df","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792489765000-engineer-jolt-j0c-r2.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792491157579,"messageId":"253825df-42d7-42f7-8b61-ede451b1075f"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_173d140c8d42472c874949df","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 211 lines\n1: Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 10:08:08 GMT\n2: Created-Local: 2026-10-20 17:08:08 +07\n3: Coding-Agent: claude\n4: Session-ID: 4f6a4fdd-c79b-44eb-97f2-1ce81ad82aa3\n5: # Findings: J0c round 2 - Python corpus parity of the jolt adapter against the JVM oracle\n6: Role: Compiler & AST / Stream engineer\n7: Implementers:\n8: - Model: claude-opus-5-5 | Assigned: 2026-10-20 16:49:25 +07 | Status: done (round 2, macOS arm64); parity NOT reached, STOPPED per Q3 | Rationale: owner routing \"opus-5.5 for implementation, cmd for review\"\n9: \n10: ## Verdict\n11: \n12: **Parity is not reached.** 46 of 51 corpus packets equal the JVM oracle. 5 differ, all in syntax-error records (line, column or message). Every successful parse packet is equal.\n13: \n14: - All 51 `first-failure` results (`:end`/`:early`/nil) equal the oracle.\n15: - All 40 completeness-probe answers equal the oracle, and so does each probe's sequence of `first-failure` calls.\n16: \n17: Per Q3 (\"no fall back\") I **stopped at reporting the divergences.**\n18: - I did not change the oracle.\n19: - I did not keep any helper or runtime patch.\n20: - I built no fallback.\n21: \n22: The 5 divergences come from two causes, both isolated with evidence below:\n23: 1. **The C++ grammar helper's token coordinates.** This is round-1 divergence (1), now confirmed harmful. It accounts for 2 entries fully, plus the `line` part of a 3rd.\n24: 2. **The ANTLR4 C++ 4.13.2 runtime's `DefaultErrorStrategy`**, which differs from the Java runtime of the same release. It accounts for 3 entries.\n25: \n26: The UTF-8-byte indentation count, round-1 divergence (2), is **shown harmless**.\n27: \n28: | Step | Result | Evidence |\n29: |---|---|---|\n30: | A. Fixed corpus and JVM oracle, as tracked fixtures | **DONE** | 51 entries and 40 probe texts. The oracle is written only by the repo's JVM path; regenerating it gives an identical SHA-256 |\n31: | B. jolt host adapter (`cst.jolt`): ops map plus `make-worker`/`parse-unit`/`parse-transform`/`utf8-bytes`/`first-failure`, synchronous | **DONE** | Five-function `ops` over checked native records. The shared `export/export-nodes`, `export/result`, `export/failure-kind` and the error-span helpers are called unchanged |\n32: | C. Compare to the oracle, per entry, plus completeness probes and the two helper divergences | **DONE, DIVERGES (5/51 packets)** | `r2-parity-final.log`: 93 PASS, 5 DIVERGES, 0 FAIL, exit 1 |\n33: \n34: **Platform:** macOS arm64 only. Linux x86_64 is NOT RUN.\n35: **Worktree:** `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c` (branch `jolt-j0c`, base HEAD `fd0adf80`). Nothing is staged or committed.\n36: **Logs:** `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r2-*`.\n37: \n38: ## A. Corpus and oracle\n39: \n40: **Corpus:** `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_corpus.edn`\n41: - SHA-256 `08d3fbacedcd2efa16b8d0fab42534247e8edc6224176361815d43b1067b7983`.\n42: - **`:entries`, 51 sources:**\n43:   - arithmetic, including int64 max (W8: no integer beyond 64 bits, no floats);\n44:   - nested indent and dedent, a double dedent at EOF, tab, mixed tab/space, form feed, NBSP indentation, non-ASCII first token in a block, dedent at EOF without a newline and with a comment;\n45:   - empty input, a lone `\\n`, a comment only, blank lines, a missing final newline;\n46:   - non-ASCII and astral characters in strings, identifiers, comments, under indentation and before an error;\n47:   - malformed input: trailing operator, `def f(:`, lexer `$`, unexpected INDENT, missing INDENT, unexpected NEWLINE, unexpected DEDENT, stray closer, mismatched brackets;\n48:   - incomplete multiline input: a def header, an open block, a decorator, a backslash continuation;\n49:   - triple-quoted strings, closed and open, including one in a block;\n50:   - open list, call and dict brackets, and a closed multiline bracket;\n51:   - early errors under an open bracket, a call and a paren.\n52: - **`:probe`, 40 texts:** every text from `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` `probe-classifies-test` and `probe-reports-irreparable-errors-at-once-test`, plus 2 non-ASCII texts.\n53: \n54: **Oracle:** `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_oracle.edn`\n55: - SHA-256 `5aee6d10fb382899772c73963d2a0c5bc076e9a9b5f98d53f5b319491a27dd33`, 7,000+ lines.\n56: - **Generator:** `src/dev/jolt_j0c_oracle.clj` (`clojure -M:dev -m jolt-j0c-oracle`). It uses exactly what the existing tests use:\n57:   - `yang.python.antlr.parser/parse-source` with a fresh worker and unit `[:unit 0]`, which runs the generated Java recognizers, the JVM `yang.antlr.cst` and the shared export/packet code;\n58:   - `yang.antlr.cst/first-failure` with a fresh worker;\n59:   - for probes, `yang.python.antlr.repl-probe/probe`, with `with-redefs` recording each `cst/first-failure` call and its result.\n60: - **Order of work:**\n61:   1. The oracle was written and its digest recorded before the first jolt run.\n62:   2. I added 4 indentation entries afterwards, but still before any jolt run, and regenerated.\n63:   3. A final regeneration after all jolt runs gave the same SHA-256 (`r2-oracle-rerun.log`). jolt output never fed the oracle.\n64: \n65: ## B. The jolt adapter\n66: \n67: **Native side** (`native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp`, +187 lines):\n68: - **New entry** `int32_t jolt_parser_export(const uint8_t*, size_t, uint8_t**, size_t*)`. It has the same statuses (−1/−2/−3/−4), the same nonallocating `noexcept` failure path and the same fault seam as `jolt_parser_parse`.\n69: - **Layout `JPX1` v1**, documented in the file header. It is the §7.3 field list:\n70:   - rule, symbolic-name and literal-name tables;\n71:   - preorder records: rule index, child count and context start; for tokens, type, code-point start and stop, token index and UTF-8 text;\n72:   - listener errors: line, column, message and span evidence (offending-token start/stop, or the lexer's token-start char index and input index);\n73:   - first-failure evidence: kind (none, lexical or token), a no-viable-alt flag, the offending token index, and the tokens buffered when the error was reported;\n74:   - the full buffered token list after the parse.\n75: - **\"None\" values:** they travel as i32 −1. `INVALID_INDEX` maps to −1, as the JVM reports it.\n76: - **Untouched:** the round-1 `jolt_parser_parse`/JPT1 is unchanged, so the round-1 probe still passes 11/11 unmodified.\n77: - **dylib SHA-256** `1e4654562541901abcbedb9bea46d43d040c28a48d02c4d3f136bed1ef31c1f1`. It is identical across 2 rebuilds of the same source, and changed from round 1's `00c27f06…` only because the source changed.\n78: \n79: **jolt side** (`src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt`, the design's §7.3 path):\n80: - **Resolution:** the namespace is `yang.antlr.cst`. Because `src/jolt` comes first in `:jolt`, it shadows `src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj`. The parity run checks this: `export-records` resolves, and only `yang.antlr.export` and `yang.antlr.packet` load beside it.\n81: - **Calls:** `:blocking` `defcfn`, pointer and size arguments only. The buffer is copied into a jolt byte array and freed through `jolt_parser_free` in `finally`. No pointer leaves `export-records`.\n82: - **Checked decode, before anything reaches shared code:**\n83:   - magic, layout version, and exact consumption with no trailing bytes;\n84:   - every read is bounds-checked;\n85:   - record kinds;\n86:   - the status field agrees with the error count;\n87:   - topology: one root whose subtree consumes exactly all N records, with rule indexes inside the table.\n88: - **`ops`:** `:token?`, `:token`, `:rule-name`, `:children` and `:start` over record ids.\n89: - **Token names:** EOF (−1) becomes `\"EOF\"`. Otherwise the symbolic name, then the literal name, then `(str type)`, the same as the JVM `ops`.\n90: - **`parse-unit`:** builds error records exactly as the JVM listener does. It then calls `export/result` with a lazy `export/export-nodes`.\n91: - **`parse-transform`:** `packet/transform`. **`utf8-bytes`:** `packet/utf8-bytes`.\n92: - **`first-failure`:** the JVM rule (below), then `export/failure-kind`.\n93: - **A wrapper failure status throws `ex-info {:status :message}`**, so a failed parse never yields a packet. This is tested through the adapter with fault modes 1, 2 and 3, followed by a clean re-parse equal to the oracle: 4 PASS.\n94: \n95: **C++ token-buffer behaviour, observed rather than assumed.** The C++ `BufferedTokenStream` fills lazily, as the JVM's does. At the first no-viable-alt error, fewer tokens are buffered than after the parse in `malformed-trailing-op` (3 then 4), `early-under-bracket` (5 then 7), `early-under-call` (4 then 5) and `early-star-under-paren` (6 then 8).\n96: - So `cst.jolt` uses the **JVM rule**: for a no-viable-alt error, the last buffered token.\n97: - The JS/Dart rule (the offending token) was computed beside it for every entry. It disagrees on `bracket-call-open` `\"f(1,\\n\"`: it would answer `:early`, where the oracle and the JVM rule answer `:end`.\n98: \n99: ## C. Results per entry\n100: \n101: **PASS (46):** arith-add, arith-assign, arith-mixed, arith-int64-max, arith-compare-bool, call-and-literals, indent-one, indent-nested, indent-double-dedent-eof, indent-tab, indent-blank-and-comment-lines, indent-mixed-tab, indent-formfeed, indent-nbsp, indent-nonascii-first-token, dedent-at-eof-no-newline, dedent-at-eof-comment, empty, only-newline, only-comment, missing-final-newline, nonascii-string, nonascii-identifier, astral-comment, nonascii-under-indent, astral-before-error, malformed-trailing-op, malformed-def, malformed-lexer, malformed-missing-indent, malformed-unexpected-newline, malformed-closer, malformed-mismatched, incomplete-def-header, incomplete-block-open, incomplete-decorator, incomplete-continuation, triple-closed, triple-closed-dq, triple-open, triple-open-in-block, bracket-list-open, bracket-call-open, bracket-closed-multiline, early-under-bracket, early-star-under-paren.\n102: \n103: **first-failure:** 51/51 equal. **Probe:** 40/40 answers and call sequences equal (each line is in `r2-parity-final.log`).\n104: \n105: **DIVERGES (5).** These are minimal diffs. Every other field of each packet is equal, including outcome, spans, error count (except entry 5) and first-failure.\n106: \n107: | # | entry (source) | diff (JVM → jolt) | cause |\n108: |---|---|---|---|\n109: | 1 | `blank-lines` `\"\\n\\n  \\n\"` | error 0 (INDENT) line/col `3 2` → `0 0`; error 1 (EOF) line `3` → `4` | helper coordinates |\n110: | 2 | `malformed-unexpected-indent` `\"  x = 1\\n\"` | error 0 (INDENT) `1 2` → `0 0`; error 1 (EOF) line `1` → `2` | helper coordinates |\n111: | 3 | `malformed-unexpected-dedent` `\"if x:\\n    @d\\ny = 1\\n\"` | error 0 message `mismatched input ''…` → `mismatched input '<2>'…`; line `2` → `3` | line: helper; message: runtime `getTokenErrorDisplay` |\n112: | 4 | `bracket-dict-open` `\"d = {'a': [1, 2\\n\"` | error 0 message: expecting a 30-token set `{'and', 'if', …, '@'}` → `expecting ']'` | runtime `sync`/`recoverInline` |\n113: | 5 | `early-under-call` `\"foo(1 2\\n\"` | JVM has 3 errors, jolt has 2: missing `no viable alternative at input '<EOF>'` at 2:0, span `[8 8]` | runtime error recovery (not isolated, see below) |\n114: \n115: ### Cause 1: the helper's token coordinates (round-1 divergence (1): CONFIRMED HARMFUL)\n116: \n117: The generated `Python3LexerBase.cpp` (grammars-v4 upstream, pinned) differs from the Java helper in two ways.\n118: \n119: - **`commonToken`**, used for INDENT and the helper's EOF:\n120:   - C++ takes line and column from `lastToken`, and uses `0 0` when there is no last token.\n121:   - Java's `new CommonToken(_tokenFactorySourcePair, …)` takes them from the lexer.\n122: - **`createDedent`:**\n123:   - Java sets `dedent.setLine(lastToken.getLine())`.\n124:   - C++ does not.\n125: \n126: These coordinates reach the packet only through syntax-error records: the listener's line and column come from the offending token.\n127: \n128: **Attribution experiment (thrown away).**\n129: 1. I edited the generated `build/antlr/python3/cpp/Python3LexerBase.cpp` to the Java behaviour. The diff is `r2-exp-helper.diff`, 2 lines: `getLine(), getCharPositionInLine()` in `commonToken`, and `dedent->setLine(lastToken->getLine())`.\n130: 2. I rebuilt (`r2-exp-build.log`, exit 0, dylib `24a0bf70…`) and re-ran parity (`r2-exp-parity.log`). Result: **48/51**. Entries 1 and 2 pass, and entry 3 keeps only its message diff.\n131: 3. I then restored the pinned output with `clojure -M:antlr-gen --target cpp`. That exit 0 is digest-checked against the manifest's `:cpp :generated`, and `cmp` with the saved copy also matched.\n132: 4. I rebuilt. The dylib is back to `1e465456…`, byte-identical to before the experiment.\n133: 5. All final results above come from the restored build.\n134: \n135: **No helper patch was kept.** Whether to add a manifest helper patch (the Java semantics) is an owner/Architect decision. Note that the existing pinned tests (`cst_export_test.cljc` `error-coordinates-are-independent-test`, `helper-token-error-coordinates-test`) already accept host-specific helper-token coordinates for Node and Dart, while this brief asks for equality with the JVM.\n136: \n137: ### Cause 2: the ANTLR4 C++ 4.13.2 `DefaultErrorStrategy` differs from Java 4.13.2\n138: \n139: Both points are verified in source.\n140: - **`getTokenErrorDisplay`:**\n141:   - C++ (`runtime/src/DefaultErrorStrategy.cpp:272-285`) replaces an **empty** token text with `<type>`.\n142:   - Java tests for **null**. The Java DEDENT's text is `\"\"`, read back from the input, so Java prints `''`.\n143:   - This explains entry 3's message.\n144:   - A related latent difference: C++ writes `\"<no Token>\"` and Java `\"<no token>\"` (from `javap -c` of the 4.13.2 runtime jar).\n145: - **`sync`/`recoverInline`:**\n146:   - Java 4.13.2 records `nextTokensContext`/`nextTokensState` in `sync()` and builds `InputMismatchException` from them in `recoverInline`. I confirmed this with `javap -p`/`-c`: the fields and the `putfield`s in `sync`.\n147:   - C++ `recoverInline` (`:194-211`) throws `InputMismatchException(recognizer)` from the current state, and its `sync` (`:92-134`) records nothing.\n148:   - So Java reports the expected set of the earlier loop state (30 tokens) where C++ reports `']'`. This explains entry 4.\n149: - **Entry 5** (a missing third error under recovery) is the same strategy class. I did **not** isolate which branch causes it, so it is attributed to the C++ runtime's recovery only provisionally.\n150: \n151: Making these equal means changing error reporting below the grammar: for example, a wrapper-installed `ANTLRErrorStrategy` that ports the Java 4.13.2 behaviour, or a runtime patch. **I did not do either.** It goes to the owner/Architect per Q3.\n152: \n153: ### Round-1 divergence (2): indentation counted over UTF-8 bytes (SHOWN HARMLESS)\n154: \n155: - The NEWLINE token's text, which `getIndentationCount` reads, can only be newline characters plus `SPACES: [ \\t]+`. The pinned `Python3Lexer.g4:101,274` define `NEWLINE: (... SPACES | ('\\r'? '\\n' | '\\r' | '\\f') SPACES?)`.\n156: - Both counted strings are therefore ASCII, so bytes equal code points.\n157: - The INDENT start index (`stop - text.size() + 1`) is the same.\n158: - **Tested:** `indent-tab`, `indent-mixed-tab`, `indent-formfeed` and `indent-nbsp` (U+00A0 is not SPACES: it gives the same syntax error on both hosts), plus `indent-nonascii-first-token` and `nonascii-under-indent`. All PASS.\n159: \n160: ## Not done (this round)\n161: \n162: - **Parity itself:** the 5 divergences above are open, for the owner/Architect.\n163: - **Later rounds, by the brief:**\n164:   - worker-owned DFA and prediction-context caches. `make-worker` returns `{:profile p}` with no native handle; parses use the generated static caches;\n165:   - two-worker interleaving and threads, and the static-cache growth audit;\n166:   - thread and stack evidence, and collect-safety under a long parse.\n167: - **Library loading:** the library loads when `cst.jolt` is loaded. A missing library fails the namespace load, not a qualified unavailable-parser result (J5).\n168: - **Profile:** `cst.jolt` ignores the profile's constructors. It always parses with the one Python3 grammar compiled into the dylib, and the grammar id is not cross-checked against the library.\n169: - **Recursion:** both the `children-of` topology walk and the shared exporter recurse with no bound. The §7.6 budget is not set.\n170: - **Not run:** embedded-NUL and invalid-UTF-8 policy, repeated parse/free cycles, sanitizers, the exported-symbols list, Node and Dart oracle cross-checks of these 51 entries (only JVM was the oracle), lowering comparison (W8 lowering), and Linux.\n171: - **The completeness probe on jolt needs a stand-in `yang.python.antlr.parser`.** The real `parser.cljc` `:clj` branch imports the Java recognizers, and `yang.stage`'s `dao.stream` closure is not jolt-audited.\n172:   - The stand-in is `src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub/yang/python/antlr/parser.clj`. It defines only `make-worker` and `grammar-id`, and is put first through `-Sdeps`.\n173:   - It is a harness artifact, not a source change. Registering a namespace in `*loaded-libs*` does not stop jolt's loader, which also keeps a host-side loaded table.\n174:   - The probe code that runs is the tracked `repl_probe.cljc`.\n175: - **kondo:**\n176:   - `clj -M:kondo --lint` reports 1 error, `Unresolved symbol: c-inject-fault`. kondo does not know the `jolt.ffi/defcfn` macro; the round-1 `jolt_parser_probe.clj` gets the same error 4 times.\n177:   - It also reports 3 warnings for `cst/*` vars unresolved without a kondo cache. No other findings.\n178:   - kondo does not lint `.jolt`.\n179: \n180: ## Commands (foreground, in the worktree) and exit codes\n181: \n182: | command | exit | log |\n183: |---|---|---|\n184: | `bb build:jolt-native` (first: `antlr4::INVALID_INDEX` is a macro; fixed) | 1, then 0 | `r2-build.log` |\n185: | `clojure -M:dev -m jolt-j0c-oracle` (47 entries, then 51 after adding indent entries, both before any jolt run) | 0, 0 | `r2-oracle.log` |\n186: | `jolt -Srepro -M:jolt src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj` (the first two runs crashed in the probe section on the stand-in mechanism) | 1, 1 | `r2-parity-1.log`, `r2-parity-2.log` |\n187: | `jolt -Srepro -Sdeps '{:aliases {:j0c-probe-stub {:extra-paths [\"src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub\"]}}}' -M:jolt:j0c-probe-stub src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj` | 1 (5 DIVERGES) | `r2-parity-3.log` |\n188: | experiment: edit the generated C++ helper, `bb build:jolt-native`, parity | 0, 1 (3 DIVERGES) | `r2-exp-helper.diff`, `r2-exp-build.log`, `r2-exp-parity.log` |\n189: | restore: `clojure -M:antlr-gen --target cpp` (digest-checked), `cmp` with the saved copy, `bb build:jolt-native` | 0, 0, 0 | `r2-restore-gen.log`, `r2-build-final.log` |\n190: | final parity (same command as above): 93 PASS, 5 DIVERGES, 0 FAIL | 1 | `r2-parity-final.log` |\n191: | `jolt -Srepro -M:jolt src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj` (round 1, unmodified) | 0, 11 PASS | `r2-probe-r1.log` |\n192: | `bb smoke:jolt` | 0, SUMMARY ok | `r2-smoke.log` |\n193: | `clojure -M:dev -m jolt-j0c-oracle` (determinism re-run; SHA-256 unchanged `5aee6d10…`) | 0 | `r2-oracle-rerun.log` |\n194: | `mise exec -- cljstyle check` on the new files (after `cljstyle fix` of 2) | 0 | n/a |\n195: | `clojure -M:kondo --lint src/dev/jolt_j0c_oracle.clj src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj` | exit nonzero: 5 errors (1 in `jolt_j0c_parity.clj`, 4 in the round-1 probe, all `defcfn` names) and 3 warnings, as described above | n/a |\n196: \n197: ## Files (worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c`, uncommitted, nothing staged)\n198: \n199: - Modified:\n200:   - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp`: `jolt_parser_export`, layout JPX1.\n201:   - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/README.md`: one inventory entry.\n202: - Added:\n203:   - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt`\n204:   - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_oracle.clj`\n205:   - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj`\n206:   - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub/yang/python/antlr/parser.clj`. This is a harness-only root. Its path does not map to a namespace from `src/dev`, so it is never loaded on the JVM. Tools that scan `src/dev` should be checked against it.\n207:   - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_corpus.edn`\n208:   - `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_oracle.edn` (generated by the JVM; do not edit)\n209: - Untouched: `antlr/python3/manifest.edn` (no helper patch kept), the shared exporter and packet code, and dao.stream, dao.data, dao.jing and yin.vm.\n210: \n211: Report: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792489765000-engineer-jolt-j0c-r2.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"1e9e33f2-e576-4d71-859b-dd3b2c8c6f01"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next, read the main files. Priority order:\n1. src/dev/jolt_j0c_oracle.clj (oracle integrity — JVM path only)\n2. src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt (adversarial reading: containment, validation, pointers)\n3. native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp (the diff portion — new export entries; the file is currently about 400 lines)\n4. src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj (check what PASS means, probe stubs)\n5. probe-stub parser.clj\n6. Corpus fixtures (check the coverage and W8 constraints of 7.8)\n7. Manifest digest is unchanged — need to verify that the :cpp digest in the manifest hasn't changed from round 1 (round 1 is in context: a8be1b51..., etc.). Grep for the manifest in the j0c worktree.\n\nQuite a lot; batch the reading. Start with oracle + cst.jolt first.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the oracle generator and the cst.jolt adapter.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_d9a360a26ef84ed28c4ee918","name":"read_file","input":{"paths":["/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_oracle.clj","/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt","/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub/yang/python/antlr/parser.clj"]}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792491173731,"messageId":"7618f7af-088a-4a49-afad-d304fc5b73ee"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_d9a360a26ef84ed28c4ee918","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 3/3 files\n--- File Contents ---\n\n// File: .claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_oracle.clj (clojure)\n;; J0c round 2: write the JVM oracle for the jolt parser corpus\n;; (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §7.8).\n;;\n;;   clojure -M:dev -m jolt-j0c-oracle\n;;\n;; Reads test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_corpus.edn and writes\n;; jolt_j0c_oracle.edn beside it, using only the repo's own JVM path: the\n;; generated Java recognizers through yang.python.antlr.parser, the JVM\n;; yang.antlr.cst and the shared yang.antlr.export/packet code. For each entry,\n;; its packet (unit [:unit 0], a fresh worker) and its cst/first-failure; for\n;; each probe text, repl-probe/probe's answer and every cst/first-failure call\n;; the probe made, in order. jolt output is never an input here.\n(ns jolt-j0c-oracle\n  (:require\n    [clojure.edn :as edn]\n    [clojure.pprint :as pprint]\n    [yang.antlr.cst :as cst]\n    [yang.python.antlr.parser :as parser]\n    [yang.python.antlr.repl-probe :as probe]))\n\n\n(def corpus-path \"test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_corpus.edn\")\n(def oracle-path \"test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_oracle.edn\")\n\n\n(defn- entry\n  [{:keys [id source]}]\n  {:id id,\n   :source source,\n   :packet (parser/parse-source (parser/make-worker) [:unit 0] source),\n   :first-failure (cst/first-failure (parser/make-worker) source)})\n\n\n(defn- probe-entry\n  [text]\n  (let [calls (volatile! [])\n        first-failure cst/first-failure\n        answer (with-redefs [cst/first-failure\n                             (fn [worker source]\n                               (let [r (first-failure worker source)]\n                                 (vswap! calls conj [source r])\n                                 r))]\n                 (probe/probe text))]\n    {:text text, :probe answer, :first-failure-calls @calls}))\n\n\n(defn -main\n  [& _]\n  (let [{:keys [entries], probes :probe} (edn/read-string (slurp corpus-path))\n        oracle {:host :jvm,\n                :grammar-id parser/grammar-id,\n                :written-by \"src/dev/jolt_j0c_oracle.clj\",\n                :entries (mapv entry entries),\n                :probe (mapv probe-entry probes)}]\n    (spit oracle-path\n          (str \";; GENERATED by `clojure -M:dev -m jolt-j0c-oracle` from\\n\"\n               \";; jolt_j0c_corpus.edn on the JVM. Do not edit by hand, and never\\n\"\n               \";; regenerate it from another host's output.\\n\"\n               (with-out-str (pprint/pprint oracle))))\n    (println \"wrote\" oracle-path (count entries) \"entries,\" (count probes) \"probe texts\")\n    (shutdown-agents)))\n\n\n// File: .claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub/yang/python/antlr/parser.clj (clojure)\n;; J0c round 2 harness stand-in (src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj puts this root first\n;; through -Sdeps; never on a JVM, Node or Dart path): the one thing\n;; yang.python.antlr.repl-probe uses from yang.python.antlr.parser is\n;; `make-worker`; the real namespace's :clj branch imports the generated Java\n;; recognizers, which do not exist on jolt. The worker is the jolt adapter's.\n(ns yang.python.antlr.parser\n  (:require [yang.antlr.cst :as cst]))\n\n\n(def grammar-id\n  \"grammars-v4/python/python3@8af0d4c26c796ea27c15c3d85418f2d0f77c3adb antlr-4.13.2\")\n\n\n(defn make-worker\n  []\n  (cst/make-worker {:grammar-id grammar-id}))\n\n\n// File: .claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt (unknown)\n(ns yang.antlr.cst\n  \"The jolt host's parser interpreter (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md\n   §7.3): the same surface as the JVM, Node and Dart `yang.antlr.cst`\n   (`make-worker`, `parse-unit`, `parse-transform`, `utf8-bytes`,\n   `first-failure`), over the ANTLR4 C++ Python3 parser behind\n   native/jolt-parser.\n\n   The wrapper parses and answers one private fixed-layout buffer (\\\"JPX1\\\",\n   see jolt_parser.cpp); this namespace copies it into a jolt byte array,\n   frees it through the wrapper, checks its bounds and topology, and hands the\n   shared `yang.antlr.export` an `ops` view over the decoded records. Packets,\n   spans and synthetic tokens are built by the shared exporter, never in C++.\n   No native pointer leaves `export-records`.\n\n   The calls are synchronous and `:blocking`, as §7.5 prescribes.\n\n   J0c round 2 limits, disclosed: the native library is the one Python3\n   grammar the wrapper was built with, whatever the profile names; a worker\n   is the profile only (no native handle and no worker-owned DFA or\n   prediction-context caches yet, §7.4); the library loads with this\n   namespace, so a missing library fails the load rather than answering a\n   qualified unavailable-parser result (J5).\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.string :as str]\n    [jolt.ffi :as ffi]\n    [yang.antlr.export :as export]\n    [yang.antlr.packet :as packet]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The native library\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def ^:private platform\n  (let [os (str/lower-case (System/getProperty \"os.name\"))\n        arch (System/getProperty \"os.arch\")]\n    (str (if (str/includes? os \"mac\") \"darwin\" \"linux\") \"-\"\n         (if (#{\"aarch64\" \"arm64\"} arch) \"arm64\" \"x86_64\"))))\n\n\n(def library\n  \"The wrapper `bb build:jolt-native` builds, under the working directory.\"\n  (str (System/getProperty \"user.dir\") \"/build/native/\" platform\n       \"/jolt-parser/lib/libjolt_parser\"\n       (if (str/starts-with? platform \"darwin\") \".dylib\" \".so\")))\n\n\n(ffi/load-library library)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-export \"jolt_parser_export\"\n  [:pointer :size_t :pointer :pointer] :int :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-free \"jolt_parser_free\" [:pointer] :void)\n\n\n(defn- call-export\n  \"The wrapper's status and its buffer, copied into jolt memory and freed.\"\n  [^bytes src]\n  (let [n (alength src)\n        in (ffi/alloc (max 1 n))\n        out-cell (ffi/alloc :pointer)\n        len-cell (ffi/alloc :size_t)]\n    (try\n      (when (pos? n) (ffi/write-array in src))\n      (let [status (c-export in n out-cell len-cell)\n            p (ffi/read out-cell :pointer)\n            len (ffi/read len-cell :size_t)]\n        (try\n          [status (if (or (ffi/null? p) (zero? len))\n                    (byte-array 0)\n                    (ffi/read-array p len))]\n          (finally (when-not (ffi/null? p) (c-free p)))))\n      (finally (ffi/free in) (ffi/free out-cell) (ffi/free len-cell)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Decoding the private layout, checked\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- refuse\n  [message data]\n  (throw (ex-info (str \"jolt parser buffer refused: \" message) data)))\n\n\n(defn- reader\n  [^bytes bs]\n  (let [n (alength bs)\n        pos (volatile! 0)\n        need (fn [k]\n               (when (or (neg? k) (> (+ @pos k) n))\n                 (refuse \"read past the end\" {:at @pos, :need k, :length n})))\n        u8 (fn []\n             (need 1)\n             (let [b (bit-and (aget bs @pos) 0xff)] (vswap! pos inc) b))\n        u32 (fn []\n              (need 4)\n              (reduce (fn [acc i] (bit-or acc (bit-shift-left (u8) (* 8 i))))\n                      0\n                      (range 4)))\n        i32 (fn [] (let [v (u32)] (if (> v 0x7fffffff) (- v 0x100000000) v)))\n        text (fn []\n               (let [k (u32)]\n                 (need k)\n                 (let [s (String. bs (int @pos) (int k) \"UTF-8\")]\n                   (vswap! pos + k)\n                   s)))\n        counted (fn [f]\n                  (let [k (u32)]\n                    ;; every record is at least one byte\n                    (need k)\n                    (vec (repeatedly k f))))]\n    {:u8 u8, :u32 u32, :i32 i32, :text text, :counted counted, :pos pos,\n     :length n}))\n\n\n(defn- decode\n  [^bytes bs]\n  (let [{:keys [u8 u32 i32 text counted pos length]} (reader bs)\n        _ (when (or (< length 8) (not= \"JPX1\" (String. bs 0 4 \"US-ASCII\")))\n            (refuse \"bad magic\" {:length length}))\n        _ (vreset! pos 4)\n        layout (u32)\n        _ (when-not (= 1 layout) (refuse \"unknown layout\" {:layout layout}))\n        status (u32)\n        rules (counted text)\n        symbolic (counted text)\n        literal (counted text)\n        nodes (counted\n                (fn []\n                  (case (u8)\n                    0 (let [rule (u32) kids (u32) has-start (u8) start (i32)]\n                        {:kind :rule, :rule rule, :child-count kids,\n                         :start (when (= 1 has-start) start)})\n                    1 {:kind :token, :type (i32), :start (i32), :stop (i32),\n                       :index (i32), :text (text)}\n                    (refuse \"bad record kind\" {:at @pos}))))\n        errors (counted\n                 (fn []\n                   {:line (u32), :column (u32), :message (text),\n                    :span-kind (u8), :a (i32), :b (i32)}))\n        failure {:kind (u8), :no-viable (u8), :index (i32), :buffered (u32)}\n        tokens (counted (fn [] {:type (i32), :index (i32), :start (i32),\n                                :stop (i32)}))]\n    (when-not (= @pos length)\n      (refuse \"trailing bytes\" {:consumed @pos, :length length}))\n    (when-not (= status (if (seq errors) 1 0))\n      (refuse \"status disagrees with the errors\" {:status status}))\n    {:rules rules, :symbolic symbolic, :literal literal, :nodes nodes,\n     :errors errors, :failure failure, :tokens tokens}))\n\n\n(defn- children-of\n  \"Each preorder record's child ids, checked: one root whose subtree is every\n   record, rule indexes inside the table.\"\n  [{:keys [nodes rules]}]\n  (let [n (count nodes)\n        out (transient (vec (repeat n [])))\n        next-id (volatile! 0)]\n    (letfn [(walk\n              []\n              (let [id @next-id]\n                (when (>= id n) (refuse \"child count overruns the records\" {}))\n                (vswap! next-id inc)\n                (let [{:keys [kind rule child-count]} (nth nodes id)]\n                  (when (= :rule kind)\n                    (when-not (< -1 rule (count rules))\n                      (refuse \"rule index outside the table\" {:rule rule}))\n                    (assoc! out id (vec (repeatedly child-count walk)))))\n                id))]\n      (when (pos? n) (walk))\n      (when-not (= @next-id n)\n        (refuse \"records outside the root's subtree\" {:walked @next-id, :n n}))\n      (persistent! out))))\n\n\n(defn- type-name\n  [{:keys [symbolic literal]} t]\n  (let [pick (fn [names] (let [s (when (< -1 t (count names)) (names t))]\n                           (when (seq s) s)))]\n    (if (= -1 t) \"EOF\" (or (pick symbolic) (pick literal) (str t)))))\n\n\n(defn export-records\n  \"Parse UTF-8 `source` natively and answer the checked records. Host-only\n   (the probe harness reads the raw first-failure evidence through it); a\n   wrapper failure status throws, so a failed parse never yields a packet.\"\n  [source]\n  (let [[status bs] (call-export (.getBytes ^String source \"UTF-8\"))]\n    (when-not (zero? status)\n      (throw (ex-info \"jolt parser failed\"\n                      {:status status,\n                       :message (when (pos? (alength ^bytes bs))\n                                  (String. ^bytes bs \"UTF-8\"))})))\n    (let [records (decode bs)]\n      (assoc records :children (children-of records)))))\n\n\n(defn- ops\n  [{:keys [nodes rules children], :as records}]\n  {:token? (fn [i] (= :token (:kind (nth nodes i)))),\n   :token (fn [i]\n            (let [{:keys [type start stop text]} (nth nodes i)]\n              {:type-name (type-name records type),\n               :start start,\n               :stop stop,\n               :text text})),\n   :rule-name (fn [i] (rules (:rule (nth nodes i)))),\n   :children (fn [i] (children i)),\n   :start (fn [i] (:start (nth nodes i)))})\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The host surface\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn make-worker\n  \"A host worker for `profile`. Round 2: the profile only; the worker-owned\n   native caches of §7.4 are later J0c work.\"\n  [profile]\n  {:profile profile})\n\n\n(defn- error-record\n  [offsets {:keys [line column message span-kind a b]}]\n  (export/syntax-error message line column\n                       (case span-kind\n                         1 (export/token-span offsets a b)\n                         2 [(export/offset-at offsets a)\n                            (export/offset-at offsets b)]\n                         nil)))\n\n\n(defn parse-unit\n  \"Parse one sealed unit's `source` with `worker`; return its CST packet.\"\n  [worker unit source]\n  (let [cps (packet/code-points source)\n        offsets (export/byte-offsets cps)\n        records (export-records source)]\n    (export/result (:profile worker) unit\n                   (mapv #(error-record offsets %) (:errors records))\n                   #(export/export-nodes (ops records) cps offsets 0))))\n\n\n(defn parse-transform\n  \"The parser stage's transform over source events (`packet/transform`).\"\n  [worker]\n  (packet/transform parse-unit worker))\n\n\n(defn utf8-bytes\n  \"The UTF-8 byte count of a string: span ends are checked against it.\"\n  [s]\n  (packet/utf8-bytes s))\n\n\n(defn first-failure\n  \"Where the grammar first refuses `source`: nil when it parses, `:end` when\n   the first error failed on an end-of-input token, else `:early`\n   (`export/failure-kind` decides).\n\n   The failing token follows the JVM rule: the offending token of a\n   mismatch, and for a no-viable-alternative error the last token buffered\n   when it was reported, since the C++ runtime's BufferedTokenStream fills\n   lazily as the Java one does (observed against the JVM oracle in J0c round\n   2, not assumed). A lexer error has no token.\"\n  [_worker source]\n  (let [{:keys [failure tokens]} (export-records source)\n        {:keys [kind no-viable index buffered]} failure]\n    (export/failure-kind\n      (case kind\n        0 nil\n        1 :lexical\n        2 (if (= 1 no-viable) (dec buffered) index))\n      (keep (fn [{:keys [type index start stop]}]\n              (when (not= -1 type)\n                {:index index, :start start, :stop stop}))\n            tokens))))\n\n"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"65c519c5-a51c-48d0-acca-bc5a06620edd"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Next up: parity scripts, native cpp diffs, corpus, manifest digest verification. Also a light check on src/jolt/README.md. Things to read: jolt_j0c_parity.clj, jolt_parser.cpp (the new export portion), the top of the corpus fixture, grep for the cpp digest in the manifest.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the parity harness, the new native export entry, the corpus, and the manifest digests.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_77ab950368a244fa98dd0e1b","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_fe921e406a46481b9b19e3f5","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"a8be1b51|a1cb6339|65027edf|8548fd2c|d30cc4df|5e62a9c3|38a2790b|59afaba9","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn","output_mode":"content","-n":true}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792491188072,"messageId":"495f3eac-847a-4b4e-b576-46c6c627ba2a"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_77ab950368a244fa98dd0e1b","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 218 lines\n1: ;; J0c round 2, step C: the jolt yang.antlr.cst adapter against the JVM oracle\n2: ;; (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §7.8).\n3: ;;\n4: ;;   . build/tools/jolt-env.sh\n5: ;;   \"$JOLT_HOME\"/bin/jolt -Srepro \\\n6: ;;     -Sdeps '{:aliases {:j0c-probe-stub {:extra-paths [\"src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub\"]}}}' \\\n7: ;;     -M:jolt:j0c-probe-stub src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj\n8: ;;\n9: ;; For every jolt_j0c_corpus.edn entry: the packet from the jolt adapter\n10: ;; (src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt, the ANTLR4 C++ parser marshalled into the\n11: ;; shared yang.antlr.export) must equal the JVM oracle's packet, and its\n12: ;; cst/first-failure the oracle's. For every probe text,\n13: ;; yang.python.antlr.repl-probe/probe runs under jolt and must answer as on the\n14: ;; JVM, making the same cst/first-failure calls with the same results.\n15: ;;\n16: ;; repl-probe requires yang.python.antlr.parser, whose :clj branch imports the\n17: ;; generated Java recognizers; the command above therefore puts a stand-in\n18: ;; yang.python.antlr.parser (src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub, holding only\n19: ;; `make-worker`, the probe's one use of it, and the grammar id) on a root\n20: ;; before the :jolt roots. The probe's own code is the tracked source.\n21: ;;\n22: ;; Prints PASS/DIVERGES per entry with a minimal diff, RECORD lines for the\n23: ;; observations, and exits nonzero on any divergence or failure.\n24: (ns jolt-j0c-parity\n25:   (:require\n26:     [clojure.edn :as edn]\n27:     [jolt.ffi :as ffi]))\n28: \n29: \n30: (def ^:private before (set (loaded-libs)))\n31: (require '[yang.antlr.cst :as cst] '[yang.antlr.export :as export])\n32: (def ^:private added (sort (remove before (loaded-libs))))\n33: \n34: (def corpus-path \"test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_corpus.edn\")\n35: (def oracle-path \"test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_oracle.edn\")\n36: \n37: (def diverged (atom []))\n38: (def failed (atom 0))\n39: \n40: \n41: (defn- check\n42:   [label ok? detail]\n43:   (when-not ok? (swap! failed inc))\n44:   (println (if ok? \"PASS\" \"FAIL\") label (pr-str detail)))\n45: \n46: \n47: (check \"yang.antlr.cst resolves to the jolt adapter (export-records exists) and loads only export/packet beside it\"\n48:        (and (some? (resolve 'yang.antlr.cst/export-records))\n49:             (= '(yang.antlr.cst yang.antlr.export yang.antlr.packet) added))\n50:        added)\n51: \n52: \n53: ;; The grammar id the JVM profile carries, read from its one definition.\n54: (def grammar-id\n55:   (let [src (slurp \"src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/parser.cljc\")\n56:         [_ s] (re-find #\"\\(def grammar-id\\s+\\\"[^\\\"]*\\\"\\s+(\\\"[^\\\"]*\\\")\\)\" src)]\n57:     (edn/read-string s)))\n58: \n59: \n60: (def profile {:grammar-id grammar-id})\n61: (def corpus (edn/read-string (slurp corpus-path)))\n62: (def oracle (edn/read-string (slurp oracle-path)))\n63: \n64: \n65: (check \"oracle is the JVM's, for this grammar id, and covers the corpus in order\"\n66:        (and (= :jvm (:host oracle))\n67:             (= grammar-id (:grammar-id oracle))\n68:             (= (mapv (juxt :id :source) (:entries corpus))\n69:                (mapv (juxt :id :source) (:entries oracle)))\n70:             (= (:probe corpus) (mapv :text (:probe oracle))))\n71:        {:host (:host oracle), :grammar-id grammar-id})\n72: \n73: \n74: ;; -- minimal diffs -------------------------------------------------------------\n75: \n76: (defn- map-diff\n77:   [a b]\n78:   (into {}\n79:         (keep (fn [k]\n80:                 (when (not= (get a k) (get b k))\n81:                   [k {:jvm (get a k), :jolt (get b k)}])))\n82:         (distinct (concat (keys a) (keys b)))))\n83: \n84: \n85: (defn- seq-diff\n86:   \"Index-wise differences of two record vectors, at most `limit` of them.\"\n87:   [xs ys limit]\n88:   (let [n (max (count xs) (count ys))]\n89:     (->> (range n)\n90:          (keep (fn [i]\n91:                  (let [x (get xs i) y (get ys i)]\n92:                    (when (not= x y)\n93:                      [i (if (and (map? x) (map? y)) (map-diff x y) {:jvm x, :jolt y})]))))\n94:          (take limit)\n95:          vec)))\n96: \n97: \n98: (defn- packet-diff\n99:   [jvm jolt]\n100:   (let [top (dissoc (map-diff (dissoc jvm :yang.cst/nodes :yang.cst/errors)\n101:                               (dissoc jolt :yang.cst/nodes :yang.cst/errors)))]\n102:     (cond-> {}\n103:       (seq top) (assoc :fields top)\n104:       (not= (:yang.cst/nodes jvm) (:yang.cst/nodes jolt))\n105:       (assoc :nodes {:count [(count (:yang.cst/nodes jvm)) (count (:yang.cst/nodes jolt))],\n106:                      :first (seq-diff (vec (:yang.cst/nodes jvm))\n107:                                       (vec (:yang.cst/nodes jolt)) 4)})\n108:       (not= (:yang.cst/errors jvm) (:yang.cst/errors jolt))\n109:       (assoc :errors (seq-diff (vec (:yang.cst/errors jvm))\n110:                                (vec (:yang.cst/errors jolt)) 8)))))\n111: \n112: \n113: ;; -- the corpus ----------------------------------------------------------------\n114: \n115: (defn- alternative-failure\n116:   \"The JS/Dart rule (the listener's offending token even for no-viable-alt),\n117:    as an observation of the C++ token buffer.\"\n118:   [source]\n119:   (let [{:keys [failure tokens]} (cst/export-records source)\n120:         {:keys [kind index]} failure]\n121:     (export/failure-kind\n122:       (case kind 0 nil 1 :lexical 2 index)\n123:       (keep (fn [{:keys [type index start stop]}]\n124:               (when (not= -1 type) {:index index, :start start, :stop stop}))\n125:             tokens))))\n126: \n127: \n128: (def results (atom []))\n129: \n130: \n131: (doseq [{:keys [id source packet first-failure]} (:entries oracle)]\n132:   (let [worker (cst/make-worker profile)\n133:         jolt-packet (try (cst/parse-unit worker [:unit 0] source)\n134:                          (catch Throwable e {:thrown (ex-message e), :data (ex-data e)}))\n135:         jolt-ff (try (cst/first-failure worker source)\n136:                      (catch Throwable e {:thrown (ex-message e)}))\n137:         records (cst/export-records source)\n138:         packet-ok (= packet jolt-packet)\n139:         ff-ok (= first-failure jolt-ff)\n140:         alt (alternative-failure source)]\n141:     (swap! results conj {:id id, :packet packet-ok, :first-failure ff-ok})\n142:     (when-not (and packet-ok ff-ok) (swap! diverged conj id))\n143:     (println (if (and packet-ok ff-ok) \"PASS\" \"DIVERGES\") id\n144:              (pr-str (cond-> {:outcome (:yang.cst/outcome packet),\n145:                               :nodes (count (:yang.cst/nodes packet)),\n146:                               :first-failure first-failure}\n147:                        (not packet-ok) (assoc :packet-diff (if (:thrown jolt-packet)\n148:                                                              jolt-packet\n149:                                                              (packet-diff packet jolt-packet)))\n150:                        (not ff-ok) (assoc :jolt-first-failure jolt-ff))))\n151:     (println \"RECORD token-buffer\" id\n152:              (pr-str {:first-error (:failure records),\n153:                       :buffered-after (count (:tokens records)),\n154:                       :offending-token-rule alt,\n155:                       :rules-agree (= alt jolt-ff)}))))\n156: \n157: \n158: ;; -- a failed native parse never yields a packet --------------------------------\n159: \n160: (ffi/defcfn c-inject-fault \"jolt_parser_inject_fault\" [:int] :int)\n161: \n162: \n163: (doseq [mode [1 2 3]]\n164:   (c-inject-fault mode)\n165:   (let [r (try (cst/parse-unit (cst/make-worker profile) [:unit 0] \"1 + 2\\n\")\n166:                (catch Throwable e {:thrown (ex-data e)})\n167:                (finally (c-inject-fault 0)))]\n168:     (check (str \"[injected mode \" mode \"] parse-unit throws the wrapper status, no packet\")\n169:            (and (contains? r :thrown) (#{-2 -3} (get-in r [:thrown :status])))\n170:            r)))\n171: \n172: \n173: (check \"after the injected failures parse-unit answers the oracle packet again\"\n174:        (= (:packet (first (:entries oracle)))\n175:           (cst/parse-unit (cst/make-worker profile) [:unit 0]\n176:                           (:source (first (:entries oracle)))))\n177:        (:id (first (:entries oracle))))\n178: \n179: \n180: ;; -- the completeness probe ----------------------------------------------------\n181: \n182: (require '[yang.python.antlr.repl-probe :as probe])\n183: \n184: \n185: (check \"repl-probe's parser is the stand-in (no rule-names: the tracked parser.cljc did not load) with the same grammar id\"\n186:        (and (nil? (resolve 'yang.python.antlr.parser/rule-names))\n187:             (= grammar-id @(resolve 'yang.python.antlr.parser/grammar-id)))\n188:        @(resolve 'yang.python.antlr.parser/grammar-id))\n189: \n190: \n191: (doseq [{:keys [text first-failure-calls], answer :probe} (:probe oracle)]\n192:   (let [calls (volatile! [])\n193:         ff cst/first-failure\n194:         jolt-answer (with-redefs [cst/first-failure\n195:                                   (fn [w s] (let [r (ff w s)] (vswap! calls conj [s r]) r))]\n196:                       (probe/probe text))\n197:         ok (and (= answer jolt-answer) (= first-failure-calls @calls))]\n198:     (when-not ok (swap! diverged conj [:probe text]))\n199:     (println (if ok \"PASS\" \"DIVERGES\") \"probe\" (pr-str text)\n200:              (pr-str (cond-> {:probe answer, :calls first-failure-calls}\n201:                        (not ok) (assoc :jolt-probe jolt-answer, :jolt-calls @calls))))))\n202: \n203: \n204: (let [rs @results]\n205:   (println \"RECORD totals\"\n206:            (pr-str {:entries (count rs),\n207:                     :packets-equal (count (filter :packet rs)),\n208:                     :first-failure-equal (count (filter :first-failure rs)),\n209:                     :probe-texts (count (:probe oracle)),\n210:                     :diverged @diverged})))\n211: \n212: \n213: (println \"SUMMARY\" (if (and (empty? @diverged) (zero? @failed))\n214:                      \"ok\"\n215:                      (str (count @diverged) \" diverged, \" @failed \" failed\")))\n216: \n217: \n218: (System/exit (if (and (empty? @diverged) (zero? @failed)) 0 1))"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_fe921e406a46481b9b19e3f5","content":[{"type":"text","text":".claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:27:                   \"Cpp/Python3ParserBase.h\" {:blake3 \"59afaba901ed2d546e2e7f9cc90f9e1b6f7512d53bd5b44567d7154909c83349\"},\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:28:                   \"Cpp/Python3ParserBase.cpp\" {:blake3 \"38a2790b2ad40d3a8c2e19f1c84a157b60f100c565f5e294fcdc8fc16c46b28b\"}},\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:147:               \"a8be1b511222602ef3e2728cacc7201a6b567347bc336dec886b38ae8263cdde\",\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:149:               \"a1cb6339cead5dd6f20cb85825b309dc7a576c77a1b17113fba447b92d307dc3\",\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:151:               \"65027edf9cf43a5d09c745b4936576d3bd5da466b733f337420f73ae368a4bed\",\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:153:               \"8548fd2cd8b7176c70b3761b8de779c6471d238f1ea675587d9c7936538a56e8\",\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:155:               \"d30cc4df41981ed4ae6ced58727fe758004d49a1626cb966844c700b9ece48e4\",\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:157:               \"5e62a9c37bf2e582fc11ccce60f863a2705d79d491b4be0f029bc7e81fe6d816\",\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:159:               \"38a2790b2ad40d3a8c2e19f1c84a157b60f100c565f5e294fcdc8fc16c46b28b\",\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:161:               \"59afaba901ed2d546e2e7f9cc90f9e1b6f7512d53bd5b44567d7154909c83349\"},"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"76f4bb7c-b692-405f-8c85-79a00bb06856"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The manifest digest is identical to round 1 (a8be1b51, a1cb6339, 65027edf, 8548fd2c, d30cc4df, 5e62a9c3, 38a2790b, 59afaba9) ✓ — the experimental helper patch was not retained in the manifest. But were the generated files under build/ actually restored? Those are untracked. The author claims regeneration + cmp + dylib returning to 1e465456. Can't verify the bytes without a shell, but the digest-check on regeneration provides strong assurance (exit 0 = digest matches the manifest). Also, could read the generated helper files under build/antlr and compare… build/antlr/python3/cpp/Python3LexerBase.cpp — I could read this and verify whether it contains the experiment's edits (`getLine(), getCharPositionInLine()` inside commonToken / `dedent->setLine`). Let me check the generated files under build and the dylib… reading the dylib won't help. Verify that the generated Python3LexerBase.cpp lacks the experiment's 2 lines, and that positionedToken from the manifest patch is present.\n\nAdditional things needed:\n- Diff of jolt_parser.cpp (new export entries) — adversarial reading.\n- Verify corpus fixtures (7.8 coverage, no floats, no integers exceeding 64 bits).\n- Quick check of src/jolt/README.md (1 inventory entry).\n\nThe word budget is 600 for output. Reading budget is fine. Let me read jolt_parser.cpp (the new parts) and the corpus header. The cpp is currently around 400 lines. Read the whole file? Last time it was 212 lines; +187 = about 399. Read that.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Manifest digests match round 1. Now the modified wrapper and the corpus fixture.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_34fe8eba8b0047bb9923ffd9","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792491209310,"messageId":"55e2ce40-e2b0-44ad-a13a-db5c77bdc01e"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_34fe8eba8b0047bb9923ffd9","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 399 lines\n1: // jolt-parser: the extern \"C\" boundary between jolt and the ANTLR4 C++\n2: // Python3 parser (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §7.3).\n3: //\n4: // J0c round 1 probe surface. Bytes in, bytes out: the caller passes UTF-8\n5: // source by pointer and length and receives one private marshalling buffer,\n6: // which it copies and returns through jolt_parser_free. No C++ class, layout\n7: // or exception crosses this boundary; every entry catches std::exception and\n8: // (...) and answers an explicit status. This is process-local marshalling (like\n9: // a sockaddr), not a value codec: it never enters a stream. Packets are built\n10: // by the shared jolt/Clojure exporter from these records, never here.\n11: //\n12: // Buffer layout, all integers little-endian:\n13: //\n14: //   magic   \"JPT1\"\n15: //   u32     layout version (1)\n16: //   u32     status (0 ok, 1 syntax errors)\n17: //   u32     rule-name count R,     then R  x (u32 len, UTF-8 bytes)\n18: //   u32     symbolic-name count S, then S  x (u32 len, UTF-8 bytes)\n19: //   u32     node count N, then N preorder records:\n20: //             u8  kind (0 rule, 1 token)\n21: //             rule:  u32 rule index, u32 child count\n22: //             token: i32 token type, u32 start, u32 stop (code-point indexes,\n23: //                    0xFFFFFFFF = none), u32 text len, UTF-8 text\n24: //   u32     error count E, then E x (u32 line, u32 column, u32 len, UTF-8 msg)\n25: //\n26: // Status codes returned by jolt_parser_parse:\n27: //    0  parsed, buffer written (status field says whether errors occurred)\n28: //   -1  invalid arguments\n29: //   -2  C++ exception (std::exception); *out holds its message, UTF-8\n30: //   -3  unknown exception\n31: //   -4  output would exceed the bound\n32: // On -2/-3 the message buffer is built without anything that can throw\n33: // (strlen/malloc/memcpy only); if even that malloc fails, *out stays NULL and\n34: // only the status reports the failure. No handler can throw.\n35: //\n36: // jolt_parser_inject_fault is a TEST SEAM (off unless called): it makes the\n37: // next parses fail deterministically so the containment paths can be shown\n38: // from jolt without exhausting memory.\n39: //\n40: // jolt_parser_export (J0c round 2) answers the same statuses and failure\n41: // paths with the records the jolt yang.antlr.cst adapter hands to the shared\n42: // exporter (§7.3). Indexes are i32, -1 for ANTLR's \"none\" (INVALID_INDEX, a\n43: // stop before a zero-width start), as the JVM reports them. Its layout:\n44: //\n45: //   magic   \"JPX1\"\n46: //   u32     layout version (1)\n47: //   u32     status (0 ok, 1 syntax errors)\n48: //   u32     rule-name count R,     then R x (u32 len, UTF-8 bytes)\n49: //   u32     symbolic-name count S, then S x (u32 len, UTF-8 bytes; \"\" none)\n50: //   u32     literal-name count S,  then S x (u32 len, UTF-8 bytes; \"\" none)\n51: //   u32     node count N, then N preorder records:\n52: //             u8  kind (0 rule, 1 token)\n53: //             rule:  u32 rule index, u32 child count,\n54: //                    u8 has-start, i32 context start (start token's start)\n55: //             token: i32 type, i32 start, i32 stop, i32 token index,\n56: //                    u32 text len, UTF-8 text\n57: //   u32     error count E, then E x\n58: //             u32 line, u32 column, u32 len, UTF-8 message,\n59: //             u8 span kind (0 none, 1 offending token, 2 lexer),\n60: //             i32 a, i32 b (token start/stop, or lexer token-start char\n61: //             index / input index when the error was reported)\n62: //   first-failure evidence (the first error reported, lexer or parser):\n63: //             u8 kind (0 none, 1 lexical, 2 token), u8 no-viable-alt,\n64: //             i32 offending token index, u32 tokens buffered at the report\n65: //   u32     buffered token count T after the parse, then T x\n66: //             (i32 type, i32 token index, i32 start, i32 stop)\n67: \n68: #include <atomic>\n69: #include <cstdint>\n70: #include <cstdlib>\n71: #include <cstring>\n72: #include <limits>\n73: #include <string>\n74: #include <string_view>\n75: #include <vector>\n76: \n77: #include \"antlr4-runtime.h\"\n78: #include \"Python3Lexer.h\"\n79: #include \"Python3Parser.h\"\n80: \n81: #define JOLT_EXPORT extern \"C\" __attribute__((visibility(\"default\")))\n82: \n83: namespace {\n84: \n85: constexpr size_t kMaxOutput = 64u * 1024u * 1024u;\n86: \n87: // 0 off; 1 throw std::bad_alloc inside the parse; 2 throw a non-std\n88: // exception; 3 as 1, and the failure handler's malloc fails too.\n89: std::atomic<int32_t> g_fault{0};\n90: \n91: struct Out {\n92:   std::vector<uint8_t> buf;\n93:   void u8(uint8_t v) { buf.push_back(v); }\n94:   void u32(uint32_t v) {\n95:     for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) buf.push_back(static_cast<uint8_t>(v >> (8 * i)));\n96:   }\n97:   void str(std::string_view s) {\n98:     u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(s.size()));\n99:     buf.insert(buf.end(), s.begin(), s.end());\n100:   }\n101: };\n102: \n103: uint32_t idx(size_t i) {\n104:   return i > std::numeric_limits<uint32_t>::max()\n105:              ? std::numeric_limits<uint32_t>::max()\n106:              : static_cast<uint32_t>(i);\n107: }\n108: \n109: struct Error {\n110:   uint32_t line, column;\n111:   std::string msg;\n112: };\n113: \n114: class Collect : public antlr4::BaseErrorListener {\n115:  public:\n116:   std::vector<Error> errors;\n117:   void syntaxError(antlr4::Recognizer*, antlr4::Token*, size_t line,\n118:                    size_t column, const std::string& msg,\n119:                    std::exception_ptr) override {\n120:     errors.push_back({idx(line), idx(column), msg});\n121:   }\n122: };\n123: \n124: void walk(antlr4::tree::ParseTree* t, Out& out, uint32_t& count) {\n125:   ++count;\n126:   if (auto* term = dynamic_cast<antlr4::tree::TerminalNode*>(t)) {\n127:     antlr4::Token* tok = term->getSymbol();\n128:     out.u8(1);\n129:     out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(static_cast<int32_t>(tok->getType())));\n130:     out.u32(idx(tok->getStartIndex()));\n131:     out.u32(idx(tok->getStopIndex()));\n132:     out.str(tok->getText());\n133:     return;\n134:   }\n135:   auto* ctx = dynamic_cast<antlr4::ParserRuleContext*>(t);\n136:   out.u8(0);\n137:   out.u32(idx(ctx->getRuleIndex()));\n138:   out.u32(idx(ctx->children.size()));\n139:   for (auto* c : ctx->children) walk(c, out, count);\n140: }\n141: \n142: int32_t hand_off(const std::vector<uint8_t>& bytes, uint8_t** out,\n143:                  size_t* out_len) {\n144:   if (bytes.size() > kMaxOutput) return -4;\n145:   auto* p = static_cast<uint8_t*>(std::malloc(bytes.empty() ? 1 : bytes.size()));\n146:   if (!p) return -3;\n147:   if (!bytes.empty()) std::memcpy(p, bytes.data(), bytes.size());\n148:   *out = p;\n149:   *out_len = bytes.size();\n150:   return 0;\n151: }\n152: \n153: // The failure answer: `status`, plus the message copied into a malloc'd\n154: // buffer when that is possible. Nonallocating apart from that one malloc,\n155: // whose failure leaves *out NULL. Never throws.\n156: int32_t message(const char* what, int32_t status, uint8_t** out,\n157:                 size_t* out_len) noexcept {\n158:   *out = nullptr;\n159:   *out_len = 0;\n160:   const char* m = what ? what : \"\";\n161:   size_t n = std::strlen(m);\n162:   void* p = g_fault.load() == 3 ? nullptr : std::malloc(n == 0 ? 1 : n);\n163:   if (p) {\n164:     std::memcpy(p, m, n);\n165:     *out = static_cast<uint8_t*>(p);\n166:     *out_len = n;\n167:   }\n168:   return status;\n169: }\n170: \n171: // --- jolt_parser_export ------------------------------------------------------\n172: \n173: int32_t sidx(size_t i) {\n174:   if (i == std::numeric_limits<size_t>::max()) return -1;  // INVALID_INDEX\n175:   return i > static_cast<size_t>(std::numeric_limits<int32_t>::max())\n176:              ? std::numeric_limits<int32_t>::max()\n177:              : static_cast<int32_t>(i);\n178: }\n179: \n180: struct ExportError {\n181:   uint32_t line, column;\n182:   std::string msg;\n183:   uint8_t span_kind;\n184:   int32_t a, b;\n185: };\n186: \n187: class ExportCollect : public antlr4::BaseErrorListener {\n188:  public:\n189:   explicit ExportCollect(antlr4::CommonTokenStream* tokens) : tokens_(tokens) {}\n190:   std::vector<ExportError> errors;\n191:   uint8_t failure_kind = 0;  // 0 none, 1 lexical, 2 token\n192:   uint8_t no_viable = 0;\n193:   int32_t failure_index = -1;\n194:   uint32_t buffered = 0;\n195: \n196:   void syntaxError(antlr4::Recognizer* recognizer, antlr4::Token* offending,\n197:                    size_t line, size_t column, const std::string& msg,\n198:                    std::exception_ptr e) override {\n199:     ExportError err{idx(line), idx(column), msg, 0, -1, -1};\n200:     auto* lexer = dynamic_cast<antlr4::Lexer*>(recognizer);\n201:     if (offending) {\n202:       err.span_kind = 1;\n203:       err.a = sidx(offending->getStartIndex());\n204:       err.b = sidx(offending->getStopIndex());\n205:     } else if (lexer) {\n206:       err.span_kind = 2;\n207:       err.a = sidx(lexer->tokenStartCharIndex);\n208:       err.b = sidx(lexer->getInputStream()->index());\n209:     }\n210:     if (failure_kind == 0) {\n211:       if (!offending) {\n212:         failure_kind = 1;\n213:       } else {\n214:         failure_kind = 2;\n215:         failure_index = sidx(offending->getTokenIndex());\n216:         if (e) {\n217:           try {\n218:             std::rethrow_exception(e);\n219:           } catch (const antlr4::NoViableAltException&) {\n220:             no_viable = 1;\n221:           } catch (...) {\n222:           }\n223:         }\n224:       }\n225:       buffered = idx(tokens_->getTokens().size());\n226:     }\n227:     errors.push_back(std::move(err));\n228:   }\n229: \n230:  private:\n231:   antlr4::CommonTokenStream* tokens_;\n232: };\n233: \n234: void walk_export(antlr4::tree::ParseTree* t, Out& out, uint32_t& count) {\n235:   ++count;\n236:   if (auto* term = dynamic_cast<antlr4::tree::TerminalNode*>(t)) {\n237:     antlr4::Token* tok = term->getSymbol();\n238:     out.u8(1);\n239:     out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(static_cast<int32_t>(tok->getType())));\n240:     out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(sidx(tok->getStartIndex())));\n241:     out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(sidx(tok->getStopIndex())));\n242:     out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(sidx(tok->getTokenIndex())));\n243:     out.str(tok->getText());\n244:     return;\n245:   }\n246:   auto* ctx = dynamic_cast<antlr4::ParserRuleContext*>(t);\n247:   out.u8(0);\n248:   out.u32(idx(ctx->getRuleIndex()));\n249:   out.u32(idx(ctx->children.size()));\n250:   out.u8(ctx->start ? 1 : 0);\n251:   out.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(ctx->start ? sidx(ctx->start->getStartIndex()) : -1));\n252:   for (auto* c : ctx->children) walk_export(c, out, count);\n253: }\n254: \n255: }  // namespace\n256: \n257: // Parse UTF-8 `src[0, len)` from `file_input` and answer the exporter's\n258: // records (layout \"JPX1\" above). Statuses as jolt_parser_parse.\n259: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_export(const uint8_t* src, size_t len,\n260:                                        uint8_t** out, size_t* out_len) {\n261:   if (!out || !out_len || (!src && len != 0)) return -1;\n262:   *out = nullptr;\n263:   *out_len = 0;\n264:   try {\n265:     switch (g_fault.load()) {\n266:       case 1:\n267:       case 3: throw std::bad_alloc();\n268:       case 2: throw 42;\n269:       default: break;\n270:     }\n271:     std::string text(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(src), len);\n272:     antlr4::ANTLRInputStream input(text);\n273:     Python3Lexer lexer(&input);\n274:     antlr4::CommonTokenStream tokens(&lexer);\n275:     Python3Parser parser(&tokens);\n276:     ExportCollect errors(&tokens);\n277:     lexer.removeErrorListeners();\n278:     parser.removeErrorListeners();\n279:     lexer.addErrorListener(&errors);\n280:     parser.addErrorListener(&errors);\n281: \n282:     antlr4::tree::ParseTree* tree = parser.file_input();\n283: \n284:     Out o;\n285:     o.buf.insert(o.buf.end(), {'J', 'P', 'X', '1'});\n286:     o.u32(1);\n287:     o.u32(errors.errors.empty() ? 0 : 1);\n288:     const auto& rules = parser.getRuleNames();\n289:     o.u32(idx(rules.size()));\n290:     for (const auto& r : rules) o.str(r);\n291:     const auto& vocab = parser.getVocabulary();\n292:     size_t max_type = vocab.getMaxTokenType();\n293:     o.u32(idx(max_type + 1));\n294:     for (size_t i = 0; i <= max_type; ++i) o.str(vocab.getSymbolicName(i));\n295:     o.u32(idx(max_type + 1));\n296:     for (size_t i = 0; i <= max_type; ++i) o.str(vocab.getLiteralName(i));\n297:     size_t count_at = o.buf.size();\n298:     o.u32(0);\n299:     uint32_t count = 0;\n300:     walk_export(tree, o, count);\n301:     for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i)\n302:       o.buf[count_at + i] = static_cast<uint8_t>(count >> (8 * i));\n303:     o.u32(idx(errors.errors.size()));\n304:     for (const auto& e : errors.errors) {\n305:       o.u32(e.line);\n306:       o.u32(e.column);\n307:       o.str(e.msg);\n308:       o.u8(e.span_kind);\n309:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(e.a));\n310:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(e.b));\n311:     }\n312:     o.u8(errors.failure_kind);\n313:     o.u8(errors.no_viable);\n314:     o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(errors.failure_index));\n315:     o.u32(errors.buffered);\n316:     const auto buffered = tokens.getTokens();\n317:     o.u32(idx(buffered.size()));\n318:     for (auto* t : buffered) {\n319:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(static_cast<int32_t>(t->getType())));\n320:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(sidx(t->getTokenIndex())));\n321:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(sidx(t->getStartIndex())));\n322:       o.u32(static_cast<uint32_t>(sidx(t->getStopIndex())));\n323:     }\n324:     return hand_off(o.buf, out, out_len);\n325:   } catch (const std::exception& e) {\n326:     return message(e.what(), -2, out, out_len);\n327:   } catch (...) {\n328:     return message(\"unknown exception\", -3, out, out_len);\n329:   }\n330: }\n331: \n332: // Parse UTF-8 `src[0, len)` from the `file_input` rule.\n333: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_parse(const uint8_t* src, size_t len,\n334:                                       uint8_t** out, size_t* out_len) {\n335:   if (!out || !out_len || (!src && len != 0)) return -1;\n336:   *out = nullptr;\n337:   *out_len = 0;\n338:   try {\n339:     switch (g_fault.load()) {\n340:       case 1:\n341:       case 3: throw std::bad_alloc();\n342:       case 2: throw 42;\n343:       default: break;\n344:     }\n345:     std::string text(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(src), len);\n346:     antlr4::ANTLRInputStream input(text);\n347:     Python3Lexer lexer(&input);\n348:     antlr4::CommonTokenStream tokens(&lexer);\n349:     Python3Parser parser(&tokens);\n350:     Collect errors;\n351:     lexer.removeErrorListeners();\n352:     parser.removeErrorListeners();\n353:     lexer.addErrorListener(&errors);\n354:     parser.addErrorListener(&errors);\n355: \n356:     antlr4::tree::ParseTree* tree = parser.file_input();\n357: \n358:     Out o;\n359:     o.buf.insert(o.buf.end(), {'J', 'P', 'T', '1'});\n360:     o.u32(1);\n361:     o.u32(errors.errors.empty() ? 0 : 1);\n362:     const auto& rules = parser.getRuleNames();\n363:     o.u32(idx(rules.size()));\n364:     for (const auto& r : rules) o.str(r);\n365:     const auto& vocab = parser.getVocabulary();\n366:     size_t max_type = vocab.getMaxTokenType();\n367:     o.u32(idx(max_type + 1));\n368:     for (size_t i = 0; i <= max_type; ++i) o.str(vocab.getSymbolicName(i));\n369:     size_t count_at = o.buf.size();\n370:     o.u32(0);\n371:     uint32_t count = 0;\n372:     walk(tree, o, count);\n373:     for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i)\n374:       o.buf[count_at + i] = static_cast<uint8_t>(count >> (8 * i));\n375:     o.u32(idx(errors.errors.size()));\n376:     for (const auto& e : errors.errors) {\n377:       o.u32(e.line);\n378:       o.u32(e.column);\n379:       o.str(e.msg);\n380:     }\n381:     return hand_off(o.buf, out, out_len);\n382:   } catch (const std::exception& e) {\n383:     return message(e.what(), -2, out, out_len);\n384:   } catch (...) {\n385:     return message(\"unknown exception\", -3, out, out_len);\n386:   }\n387: }\n388: \n389: // Release a buffer jolt_parser_parse handed out. NULL is accepted.\n390: JOLT_EXPORT void jolt_parser_free(uint8_t* p) { std::free(p); }\n391: \n392: // TEST SEAM: set the fault mode (0 off, 1-3 as g_fault above) for later\n393: // parses. Answers the previous mode.\n394: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_inject_fault(int32_t mode) {\n395:   return g_fault.exchange(mode);\n396: }\n397: \n398: // The marshalling layout version this library writes.\n399: JOLT_EXPORT int32_t jolt_parser_layout_version(void) { return 1; }"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"b575b61e-5f4f-40a5-8d12-ff33a07e93a6"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"jolt_parser_export: same containment structure — try/catch std::exception/(...), messages are noexcept and non-allocating, seam is shared. ExportCollect::syntaxError: inside the listener, rethrow of std::exception_ptr and catch — safe (inside catch(...) {}), and it's within an outer try anyway. tokens_ pointer: ExportCollect holds a pointer to CommonTokenStream, but that stream outlives the parse (declared beforehand). Note: ExportCollect is constructed with &tokens before parser.file_input() — no problem.\n\nOne subtle point: inside syntaxError, `buffered = idx(tokens_->getTokens().size())` — only on the first error. `getTokens()` fills the buffer completely, doesn't it? In ANTLR C++, BufferedTokenStream::getTokens() returns the vector of tokens buffered up to that point (does not force fill). OK — matches the claim of \"number of tokens buffered at the time of the report\".\n\nwalk_export: writes start/stop as u32(sidx(...)) — sidx returns int32, cast to u32; -1 → 0xFFFFFFFF, decoded as i32 -1. Consistent.\n\nLayout version entry: jolt_parser_layout_version still returns 1 (JPT1). JPX1 has its own version field inside the buffer; no problem.\n\nNext the corpus, and checking the restored generated helper. Read the corpus fixture (check W8: no floating point numbers, no integers exceeding 64 bits) — probably medium size. Also grep for the experiment lines (getLine(), getCharPositionInLine() inside commonToken, dedent->setLine) against the generated Python3LexerBase.cpp, and confirm that it has been restored (they must not be present) and that positionedToken is present.\n\nAlso quickly check src/jolt/README.md. Also check the DIVERGES/PASS counts in r2-parity-final.log — grep the SUMMARY.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Now the corpus, the restored generated helper, and the final parity log summary.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_730b38105a014dab91311cbd","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/test/yang/python/antlr/fixtures/jolt_j0c_corpus.edn"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_daba9a5369534dab87ff4059","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"getLine\\(\\), getCharPositionInLine\\(\\)|dedent->setLine|positionedToken","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/antlr/python3/cpp/Python3LexerBase.cpp","output_mode":"content","-n":true}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_096db81331e1488490f92e67","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"SUMMARY|RECORD totals","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r2-parity-final.log","output_mode":"content","-n":true}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792491255903,"messageId":"74808c29-bd99-4129-b3ac-388ecd71ba80"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_730b38105a014dab91311cbd","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 88 lines\n1: ;; J0c round 2 fixed corpus (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §7.8):\n2: ;; the Python sources whose CST packets and first-failure results the jolt\n3: ;; adapter must reproduce. W8: no integer beyond 64 bits and no floats.\n4: ;; The oracle for every entry is jolt_j0c_oracle.edn, written on the JVM by\n5: ;; src/dev/jolt_j0c_oracle.clj; never edit it to match another host.\n6: ;;\n7: ;; :entries   one packet (unit [:unit 0], a fresh worker) and one\n8: ;;            cst/first-failure result per source\n9: ;; :probe     texts for yang.python.antlr.repl-probe/probe: the oracle records\n10: ;;            the probe's answer and every cst/first-failure call it made\n11: ;;            (from yin.repl.python-antlr-test probe-classifies-test and\n12: ;;            probe-reports-irreparable-errors-at-once-test)\n13: {:entries\n14:  [;; arithmetic\n15:   {:id :arith-add, :source \"1 + 2\\n\"}\n16:   {:id :arith-assign, :source \"x = 1\\n\"}\n17:   {:id :arith-mixed, :source \"x = (1 + 2) * 3 - 4 // 5 % 6 ** 2\\n\"}\n18:   {:id :arith-int64-max, :source \"x = 9223372036854775807 - -9223372036854775807\\n\"}\n19:   {:id :arith-compare-bool, :source \"y = not 1 < 2 and 3 >= 4 or x == 5\\n\"}\n20:   {:id :call-and-literals, :source \"def f(a):\\n    return [a, {1: 'x'}]\\n\"}\n21:   ;; nested indentation and dedent\n22:   {:id :indent-one, :source \"if x:\\n    y = 1\\nz = 2\\n\"}\n23:   {:id :indent-nested,\n24:    :source \"def f(a):\\n    if a:\\n        return 1\\n    else:\\n        while a:\\n            a = a - 1\\n    return 2\\nf(3)\\n\"}\n25:   {:id :indent-double-dedent-eof, :source \"if a:\\n    if b:\\n        c = 1\\n\"}\n26:   {:id :indent-tab, :source \"if x:\\n\\ty = 1\\n\"}\n27:   {:id :indent-blank-and-comment-lines, :source \"if x:\\n\\n    # c\\n    y = 1\\n\\nz = 2\\n\"}\n28:   ;; the C++ helper counts indentation over UTF-8 bytes (the Java one over\n29:   ;; UTF-16 chars); NEWLINE's text is newline characters plus [ \\t]* only\n30:   {:id :indent-mixed-tab, :source \"if x:\\n  \\ty = 1\\n  \\tz = 2\\n\"}\n31:   {:id :indent-formfeed, :source \"if x:\\n\\f    y = 1\\n\"}\n32:   {:id :indent-nbsp, :source \"if x:\\n\\u00a0   y = 1\\n\"}\n33:   {:id :indent-nonascii-first-token, :source \"if x:\\n    é = 'ü'\\n    π = 1\\nz = é\\n\"}\n34:   {:id :dedent-at-eof-no-newline, :source \"if x:\\n    y = 1\"}\n35:   {:id :dedent-at-eof-comment, :source \"if x:\\n    y = 1  # c\"}\n36:   ;; empty input and EOF\n37:   {:id :empty, :source \"\"}\n38:   {:id :only-newline, :source \"\\n\"}\n39:   {:id :only-comment, :source \"# c\\n\"}\n40:   {:id :blank-lines, :source \"\\n\\n  \\n\"}\n41:   {:id :missing-final-newline, :source \"x = 1\"}\n42:   ;; non-ASCII and astral characters\n43:   {:id :nonascii-string, :source \"s = 'é𝄞'\\nt = 2\\n\"}\n44:   {:id :nonascii-identifier, :source \"é = 1\\nπ2 = é + 1\\n\"}\n45:   {:id :astral-comment, :source \"x = 1  # 𝄞\\ny = 2\\n\"}\n46:   {:id :nonascii-under-indent, :source \"if 'é':\\n    y = '𝄞'\\n    z = 'ü'\\n\"}\n47:   {:id :astral-before-error, :source \"s = '𝄞' +\\n\"}\n48:   ;; malformed syntax\n49:   {:id :malformed-trailing-op, :source \"1 +\\n\"}\n50:   {:id :malformed-def, :source \"def f(:\\n    return 1\\n\"}\n51:   {:id :malformed-lexer, :source \"x = $\\n\"}\n52:   {:id :malformed-unexpected-indent, :source \"  x = 1\\n\"}\n53:   {:id :malformed-missing-indent, :source \"if x:\\npass\\n\"}\n54:   {:id :malformed-unexpected-newline, :source \"if x:\\n    x =\\n\"}\n55:   {:id :malformed-unexpected-dedent, :source \"if x:\\n    @d\\ny = 1\\n\"}\n56:   {:id :malformed-closer, :source \"x = )\\n\"}\n57:   {:id :malformed-mismatched, :source \"x = (]\\n\"}\n58:   ;; incomplete multiline input\n59:   {:id :incomplete-def-header, :source \"def f(n):\\n\"}\n60:   {:id :incomplete-block-open, :source \"def f(n):\\n    return n\\n\"}\n61:   {:id :incomplete-decorator, :source \"@dec\\n\"}\n62:   {:id :incomplete-continuation, :source \"x = 1 \\\\\\n\"}\n63:   ;; triple-quoted strings\n64:   {:id :triple-closed, :source \"s = '''a\\nb'''\\n\"}\n65:   {:id :triple-closed-dq, :source \"s = \\\"\\\"\\\"a\\n  b\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\"}\n66:   {:id :triple-open, :source \"s = '''a\\nb\\n\"}\n67:   {:id :triple-open-in-block, :source \"if x:\\n    s = '''a\\n\"}\n68:   ;; open brackets\n69:   {:id :bracket-list-open, :source \"x = [1,\\n2,\\n\"}\n70:   {:id :bracket-call-open, :source \"f(1,\\n\"}\n71:   {:id :bracket-dict-open, :source \"d = {'a': [1, 2\\n\"}\n72:   {:id :bracket-closed-multiline, :source \"x = [1,\\n  2]\\n\"}\n73:   ;; an early error under an open bracket\n74:   {:id :early-under-bracket, :source \"x = [1 2,\\n\"}\n75:   {:id :early-under-call, :source \"foo(1 2\\n\"}\n76:   {:id :early-star-under-paren, :source \"x = (1 + * 2\\n\"}]\n77: \n78:  :probe\n79:  [\"x = 1\" \"print(\" \"print(\\n\" \"print((1,\" \"d = {'a': [1, 2\"\n80:   \"if x:\\n  y = {1: (2\" \"f(a for a in b\" \"x = (1 if y else\" \"def f(n):\"\n81:   \"def f(n): # body next\" \"@dec\" \"def f(n):\\n    return n\"\n82:   \"def f(n):\\n    return n\\n\" \"s = \\\"\\\"\\\"hello\" \"s = \\\"\\\"\\\"a\\n  b\\\"\\\"\\\"\"\n83:   \"s = \\\"\\\"\\\"(\\\"\\\"\\\"\" \"s = '(' # )\" \"s = 'it\\\\'s ('\" \"s = 'abc\\\\\"\n84:   \"s = 'abc\\\\  \" \"foo(\\\"\\\"\\\"a\" \"if x:\\n    s = '''a\" \"x = * \\\"\\\"\\\"\"\n85:   \"x = 1 \\\\\" \"x = 1 \\\\\\n+ 2\" \"x = 1 # \\\\\" \"x = (1 + * 2\" \"x = )\"\n86:   \"foo(1 2\" \"x = (]\" \"x =\" \"1 +\" \"lambda x:\" \"s = 'abc\"\n87:   \"x = 1 \\\"\\\"\\\"abc\" \"x = = \\\"\\\"\\\"\" \"foo(1 2 '''\" \"x = (1 + * 2 'a\\\\\"\n88:   \"s = '𝄞'\\nif é:\" \"if x:\\n    y = 'é'\\n\"]}"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_daba9a5369534dab87ff4059","content":[{"type":"text","text":".claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/antlr/python3/cpp/Python3LexerBase.cpp:28:        emit(positionedToken(Python3Parser::NEWLINE, getCharIndex(), getCharIndex() - 1));\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/antlr/python3/cpp/Python3LexerBase.cpp:48:    std::unique_ptr<antlr4::CommonToken> dedent = positionedToken(Python3Parser::DEDENT, getCharIndex(), getCharIndex() - 1);\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/antlr/python3/cpp/Python3LexerBase.cpp:52:std::unique_ptr<antlr4::CommonToken> Python3LexerBase::positionedToken(size_t type, size_t start, size_t stop) {\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/antlr/python3/cpp/Python3LexerBase.cpp:53:    return _factory->create({ this, _input }, type, \"\", DEFAULT_TOKEN_CHANNEL, start, stop, getLine(), getCharPositionInLine());\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/antlr/python3/cpp/Python3LexerBase.cpp:105:        emit(positionedToken(Python3Lexer::NEWLINE, tokenStartCharIndex, tokenStartCharIndex + newLine.size() - 1));"}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_096db81331e1488490f92e67","content":[{"type":"text","text":".claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r2-parity-final.log:150:RECORD totals {:entries 51, :packets-equal 46, :first-failure-equal 51, :probe-texts 40, :diverged [:blank-lines :malformed-unexpected-indent :malformed-unexpected-dedent :bracket-dict-open :early-under-call]}\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r2-parity-final.log:151:SUMMARY 5 diverged, 0 failed"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"f93b7cb0-82f5-458a-a3ab-cebd7a4fb856"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The generated helpers were restored correctly: `commonToken` does not have the experimental `getLine(), getCharPositionInLine()` edits (grep matched only positionedToken on line 53, which is the manifest patch itself); `dedent->setLine` does not exist. The generated files in build/ match the pinned manifest patches. ✓ (e) is nearly closed: the manifest is unchanged (digest identical to round 1), the generated files have been reverted to the post-patch pinned state, and the dylib bytes have been re-verified via rebuild hash (the report's claim; the dylib cannot be hashed).\n\nCorpus check against §7.8: arithmetic ✓ (int64 max value 9223372036854775807, `- -9223372036854775807` — note: this is sign-flip of int64 min expressed as positive literal 9223372036854775807 minus; both literals are ≤ int64 max; no integers exceeding 64 bits, no floating-point numbers ✓). Nested indentation/dedent ✓, double dedent at EOF ✓, empty/EOF ✓, non-ASCII + astral plane (é, 𝄞 U+1D11E, π, ü) ✓, malformed input ✓, incomplete multi-line ✓, triple quotes (closed/open, within block) ✓, unclosed brackets (list, call, dict, closed multi-line) ✓, early errors under unclosed brackets ✓ (bracket, call, paren-star). Probe text from tracked tests + 2 non-ASCII ✓.\n\nWait — corpus count: entries in the file are… the report says 51. Counting from the list: arith 6, call-and-literals 1 =7? Let me count: arith-add, arith-assign, arith-mixed, arith-int64-max, arith-compare-bool (5), call-and-literals (6), indent-one (7), indent-nested (8), indent-double-dedent-eof (9), indent-tab (10), indent-blank-and-comment-lines (11), indent-mixed-tab (12), indent-formfeed (13), indent-nbsp (14), indent-nonascii-first-token (15), dedent-at-eof-no-newline (16), dedent-at-eof-comment (17), empty (18), only-newline (19), only-comment (20), blank-lines (21), missing-final-newline (22), nonascii-string (23), nonascii-identifier (24), astral-comment (25), nonascii-under-indent (26), astral-before-error (27), malformed-trailing-op (28), malformed-def (29), malformed-lexer (30), malformed-unexpected-indent (31), malformed-missing-indent (32), malformed-unexpected-newline (33), malformed-unexpected-dedent (34), malformed-closer (35), malformed-mismatched (36), incomplete-def-header (37), incomplete-block-open (38), incomplete-decorator (39), incomplete-continuation (40), triple-closed (41), triple-closed-dq (42), triple-open (43), triple-open-in-block (44), bracket-list-open (45), bracket-call-open (46), bracket-dict-open (47), bracket-closed-multiline (48), early-under-bracket (49), early-under-call (50), early-star-under-paren (51). = 51 ✓. Probe text: 40 ✓ (claimed; roughly 40 items in the list).\n\nOracle integrity (a): the oracle generator only requires yang.antlr.cst (JVM), yang.python.antlr.parser, yang.python.antlr.repl-probe — all JVM paths; jolt is nowhere. Runs with `clojure -M:dev`. The output header states \"GENERATED ... never regenerate from another host's output\". Ordering reliability: r2-oracle.log then r2-oracle-rerun.log have identical SHA-256 after all jolt runs — digest stability indicates determinism; the ordering claim (before first jolt run) cannot be fully proven from files alone, but corroborated by: oracle regeneration determinism + author's log ordering + parity script checks that oracle's :host :jvm and grammar-id match, and corpus/oracle id ordering. Could the corpus have been tuned to pass? The 4 added indent entries were added before the jolt run, and all 4 PASS; 5 entries diverge — a tuned corpus would have avoided divergence, or the diverging entries would have been removed; instead the author reports divergence and halts. The corpus includes entries that expose known round-1 divergences (blank-lines, unexpected-indent/dedent) — the opposite of tuning. Honest.\n\n(b) cst.jolt adversarial review:\n- call-export: frees native buffers in finally, frees cells too; no pointer leakage; copies to jolt byte-array ✓.\n- decode: bounds-checked reader (`need` before every read; negative k rejected); magic JPX1; layout==1; exact consumption (no trailing bytes); status/errors consistency; record kinds validated; children-of topology: single root consumes exactly all records; rule indices within table (`(< -1 rule (count rules))` — interesting: allows rule == -1? Rule index -1 permitted? nodes' rule from walk_export is u32 of idx(getRuleIndex()), never -1… `(< -1 rule ...)` allows rule ≥ 0. OK, -1 excluded. Good).\n- Potential DoS: `counted`'s k can be huge (u32) — `(need k)` checks k ≤ remaining bytes before `(vec (repeatedly k f))` — since every record is ≥ 1 byte, k is bounded by buffer length. Good — prevents huge allocation. Inner f may throw refuse → propagates as ex-info. Good.\n- text: `(need k)` followed by String. UTF-8 decode with (int k) — k ≤ length is bounded; int cast of k ≤ 2^31 is fine.\n- Wrapper failure throws ex-info {:status} — parse-unit: export-records throws before building packet ✓; parity tests assert throw at modes 1-3 and re-parse cleanly ✓ (4 checks).\n- first-failure: JVM rule for no-viable-alt = (dec buffered) — matches JVM cst.clj? Cannot read JVM cst.clj here (didn't fetch). The evidence presented is 51/51 first-failure matches with the oracle — empirical. Sufficient.\n- ops view over record ids — shared exporter unchanged (report: export/packet unchanged; parity confirms loading of only export+packet).\n- One residual: `decode`'s u32 reader: `(reduce ... (range 4))` — fine.\n- children-of uses recursion in `walk` — unbounded recursion on deep tree (report acknowledges no recursion budget in §7.6) — disclosed.\n- `platform` detection uses os.name lowercased includes \"mac\" — on jolt shim os.name may be nil on Linux (per J0b notes: \"possibly nil on Linux\"); `(str/lower-case nil)`? System/getProperty returns nil → str/lower-case on nil → NPE? In Clojure, (str/lower-case nil) throws NPE. On jolt on Linux, os.name is nil → load fails! But J0b probe also used `(System/getProperty \"os.name\")` only for header println (nil prints fine). Here cst.jolt computes platform from os.name at load. On shiva Linux, jolt's os.name shim — J0b's shiva log shows \"os.name= Linux os.arch= amd64\" — actually shiva log prints os.name= Linux! So on Linux the shim returns \"Linux\". And jolt_native.clj (J0c round 1) already used the same detection and ran on… wait, jolt_native.clj runs on bb (JVM), not jolt. jolt_parser_probe.clj also uses same platform detection and ran on shiva? The shiva J0b run uses udp_probe.clj which doesn't use os.name for platform (uses native/os). But jolt_parser_probe.clj (round 1) has identical platform code — was it run on shiva? Not run (Linux not executed in J0c). But shiva J0b log shows os.name=Linux printed from jolt — so the shim does return os.name on Linux. OK, low risk. However, cst.jolt uses os.name detection instead of native/os which J0b deemed more reliable (\"os.name is never read here (jolt's System/getProperty shim may return nil)\"). Inconsistency: cst.jolt should use jolt.socket.native/os, or at least be robust. Evidence from shiva shows os.name works (\"Linux\"), arch \"amd64\" — wait: cst.jolt arch mapping: `(#{\"aarch64\" \"arm64\"} arch)` → arm64, else x86_64. shiva shows amd64 → x86_64 ✓. os \"linux\" → contains \"mac\"? \"linux\" doesn't contain \"mac\" ✓ → linux-x86_64 ✓. And J0b's own probe shiva log header shows os.name= Linux (uppercase L; lowercased fine). So works, but the comment in udp_probe.clj says the shim may return nil — if nil, `(str/includes? (str/lower-case nil) \"mac\")` → NPE at load. Actually `(str/lower-case nil)`: clojure.string/lower-case calls .toLowerCase on s — nil → NPE… in jolt, likely similar. Given shiva evidence shows os.name = \"Linux\", risk is low, but the codebase itself documents distrust of os.name. Finding: LOW — use jolt.socket.native/os or guard nil for consistency with the probe's own rationale. Note round 1's jolt_parser_probe.clj also has same pattern (pre-existing, reviewed, worked on macOS; untested on jolt on Linux). cst.jolt is load-time: a nil os.name would fail namespace load with cryptic NPE. Deserves a low finding.\n\n(c) first-failure rule evidence: RECORD's token-buffer lines per entry show first-error's buffered count and post-parse count differing (e.g., 3 then 4) — observed laziness; JS/Dart alternative rules computed on all entries, disagreeing on bracket-call-open where oracle says :end and JVM rule says :end. Evidence is empirical (40/40 probe + 51/51 ff match oracle) + RECORD lines. Solid within scope of this corpus. Note: \"same lazy as Java\" claim is backed by buffered-at-report < post-parse counts. ✓.\n\n(d) probe stubs: stand-ins provide only make-worker + grammar-id; parity script checks stubs are active (`rule-names` unresolved — proves tracked parser.cljc not loaded) and grammar-id matches the tracked parser.cljc definition (read via regex from src/cljc). Probe code itself is tracked repl_probe.cljc which runs under jolt. Could stubs hide gaps? The real parser.cljc's :clj branch imports Java recognizers — impossible on jolt until the audit; stubs' make-worker delegates to cst/make-worker (real adapter). So the probe runs real shared probe code + real jolt cst. Gap: real parser.cljc's jolt branch (:jolt guard §4.2) not written — disclosed as harness artifact in the report; correct for feasibility round. One residual: stub root is under src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub — not on JVM classpath (src/dev is under :dev/:clean aliases; stubs are in subdirectory jolt-j0c-probe-stub/yang/... — if src/dev were on classpath, yang.python.antlr.parser wouldn't map because root is src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub not src/dev). ✓ Never loaded on JVM. Author flags \"should match tools that scan src/dev\" — honest.\n\n(e) Experimental rollback: manifest digests identical to round 1; regenerated generated files match manifest patch state (positionedToken present, no experiment lines); report claims digest-checked regeneration exit 0 + cmp + byte-identical dylib hash 1e465456 on rebuild. Cannot hash dylib myself; backed by regeneration digest check. ✓ CLOSED (to the extent verifiable).\n\n(f) Honesty: extensive not-done list (worker cache, two workers, thread/stack, collect-safety, library-load failure modes, profile constructor ignoring, no grammar-id cross-check with library, recursion budget, NUL/invalid UTF-8, sanitizer, exported symbols, Linux, lowering). Divergences are reported not patched; halts per Q3; no fallback. Very honest. One check: report says \"93 PASS, 5 DIVERGES, 0 FAIL, exit 1\" — log shows RECORD's total 46 packet matches, 51 ff matches, SUMMARY 5 divergences 0 failures. 93 PASS lines plausible (51 entries + 40 probes + ~6 checks − 5 DIVERGES replacing entry PASSes: 46+40+4+… ≈ 93). ✓.\n\nFindings:\n- G1 low: cst.jolt:38-49 platform via System/getProperty os.name at load; udp_probe.clj documents that jolt shim \"may return nil\" on Linux (nil → NPE at load). shiva J0b log shows os.name=\"Linux\" so it works empirically, but for consistency use jolt.socket.native/os or nil guard.\n- G2 info: `jolt_parser_layout_version` returns 1 = JPT1 version; JPX1's version only exists within buffer; adapter checks buffer layout field, so fine; consider renaming/versioning the entry when layout evolves.\n- G3 info: corpus is faithful; note `- -9223372036854775807` stays within int64 ✓.\n- G4 info: stub root under src/dev — never resolves on JVM (subdirectory), but tools scanning src/dev (kondo/affected.clj) might pick it up; author already flags. affected.clj treats src/dev as wide change anyway.\n- Also ExportCollect holds raw pointer tokens_ — lifetime fine (same scope).\n- decode's `counted` guard \"all records ≥ 1 byte\" — true for these record shapes ✓.\n\nVerdict: SIGNED OFF (G1 is low, recommend fix in next round; not blocking? The load-time NPE only bites on hypothetical nil shim, and shiva evidence contradicts nil on Linux. Make it SIGNED OFF with G1 noted for J0c round 3 / Linux run.) Hmm — \"SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES\" would demand a fix before merge. Given Linux not run and shim behavior empirically fine on shiva, I'll go SIGNED OFF with recommendation. Actually adversarial stance: this is production-track source (src/jolt), and repo's own probe documents distrust of os.name on jolt. A load-time crash on Linux would be discovered immediately in shiva run and os.name=\"Linux\" is proven in shiva. So risk is minimal → SIGNED OFF with G1 as recommendation.\n\nWord cap 600. Structure tight.\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 10:12:02 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 17:12:02 +07\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\n# Review: J0c round 2 (corpus parity, cst.jolt adapter, export entry)\nRole: Reviewer (read-only) | Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max\n\n## Verdict: SIGNED OFF\n\nThe 5 divergences are reported, isolated and left for the Architect per instruction; the work itself is correct and honest.\n\n## Findings\n\n| id | sev | file:line | evidence | correction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| G1 | low | src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:38-49 | Platform is derived at load from `System/getProperty \"os.name\"`; the J0b probe's own comment (udp_probe.clj:13-14) says jolt's shim \"can answer nil\" on Linux, and `(str/lower-case nil)` would NPE the namespace load. shiva's J0b log shows `os.name= Linux`, so it works empirically, but the distrust is documented in-repo. | Use `jolt.socket.native/os` (as the J0b probe does) or nil-guard, before the Linux J0c run. |\n| G2 | info | jolt_parser.cpp:398-399 | `jolt_parser_layout_version` still answers 1 (the JPT1 version); JPX1's version travels only inside its buffer. The adapter checks it there, so no gap today. | Give the export layout its own version entry when layouts evolve. |\n| G3 | info | src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub/ | Stand-in root sits under `src/dev`; it cannot resolve on the JVM (namespace maps from the subdirectory, not `src/dev`), but scanners (kondo, affected.clj) may pick it up. Author already flags this. | Keep the flag; consider `build/` or a `jolt-` prefix convention if it lingers past J0c. |\n\n## Checks\n\n- (a) Oracle integrity: `jolt_j0c_oracle.clj` requires only JVM-path namespaces (`yang.antlr.cst`, `yang.python.antlr.parser`, `yang.python.antlr.repl-probe`) and runs under `clojure -M:dev`; no jolt input exists in the generator, and the fixture header forbids cross-host regeneration. Ordering is credible: determinism re-run after all jolt runs gives the identical SHA-256 (r2-oracle-rerun.log), and the parity script itself asserts `:host :jvm`, grammar-id equality and corpus/oracle id+source alignment (jolt_j0c_parity.clj:65-71). No tuning: the corpus deliberately includes the entries that expose the known round-1 helper divergence (`blank-lines`, `malformed-unexpected-indent/dedent`), and divergences were reported rather than removed. §7.8 coverage complete (I counted 51 entries: arithmetic incl. int64-max, nested indent/dedent + double-dedent-at-EOF, empty/EOF, non-ASCII + astral, malformed ×9, incomplete multiline ×4, triple quotes ×4, open brackets ×4, early-error-under-bracket ×3) and W8 respected: no floats, nothing beyond int64 (`9223372036854775807 - -9223372036854775807` stays in range).\n- (b) `jolt_parser_export` keeps the round-1 containment exactly: same -1/-2/-3/-4 statuses, shared `noexcept` nonallocating `message()`, shared fault seam; listener-side `rethrow_exception` is wrapped in its own catch-all inside the outer try. cst.jolt: buffer copied then freed in `finally` (call-export), no pointer escapes; decode is fully bounds-checked (`need` before every read, negative lengths refused), validates magic/layout/exact-consumption/status-vs-errors/record-kinds, and `counted`'s ≥1-byte-per-record bound prevents count-driven overallocation; `children-of` enforces one root consuming all records with rule indexes in-table. Nonzero wrapper status throws before any packet (parity modes 1-3 + clean re-parse: 4 PASS). Shared export/packet code loaded unchanged (load-closure assertion :47-50).\n- (c) First-failure evidence is solid: per-entry RECORD lines show buffered-at-report < buffered-after-parse (lazy fill, as Java), the JVM rule matches 51/51 oracle results, and the JS/Dart alternative was computed for every entry and demonstrably disagrees on `\"f(1,\\n\"` where the oracle says `:end`. Empirical, not assumed.\n- (d) The stand-in supplies only `make-worker` (delegating to the real adapter) and `grammar-id`; the parity run proves the tracked `parser.cljc` did NOT load (`rule-names` unresolved) and cross-reads the grammar id from the tracked source. The probe code exercised is the tracked `repl_probe.cljc`, so no shared-code gap is hidden; the missing `:jolt` branch of the real `parser.cljc` is disclosed as later work.\n- (e) Revert verified: manifest `:cpp :generated` digests byte-identical to round 1; the on-disk generated `Python3LexerBase.cpp` shows only the manifest's `positionedToken` patch and neither experiment line (`getLine(), getCharPositionInLine()` in `commonToken`, `dedent->setLine`); digest-checked regeneration exit 0. Dylib hash `1e465456…` rests on the report.\n- (f) Honesty: extensive NOT-done list (worker caches/§7.4, two-worker and thread tests, collect-safety, §7.6 recursion budget, load-time library failure vs J5, profile constructors ignored, grammar-id not cross-checked against the dylib, NUL/invalid-UTF-8, sanitizers, exported-symbols list, lowering, Linux). Stopped at reporting per Q3; no oracle edit, no fallback, no kept patch. Totals in the log (46/51 packets, 51/51 first-failure, 40/40 probes, `SUMMARY 5 diverged, 0 failed`) match the report.\n\n## Could not verify\n\n- Digests/hashes myself (no shell): corpus `08d3fba…`, oracle `5aee6d1…`, dylib `1e465456…`, and the `cmp` against the saved helper copy.\n- Timestamps/order of oracle vs first jolt run beyond log names and the determinism re-run.\n- The JVM `yang.antlr.cst` first-failure rule source (parity is empirical via 51/51).\n- Linux behaviour of cst.jolt (G1) and all shiva runs for this round (none claimed)."}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792491409196,"messageId":"4d1b02e0-23c4-40c7-9dcc-89bf1b79528c"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 11:47:32 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 18:47:32 +07\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\n# Task: review J0c round 3 (E1 standard, C++ helper patch, layout v2)\nRole: Reviewer (read-only)\nImplementers:\n- Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max | Assigned: 2026-10-20 18:47:32 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: owner routing \"cmd for review\"; author claude-opus-5-5\n\nREAD-ONLY review of the UNCOMMITTED round-3 change in /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c (branch jolt-j0c, HEAD 29986f9a): modified antlr/python3/manifest.edn, native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp, src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj, src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt. Author report: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792491448000-engineer-jolt-j0c-r3.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md. The Architect's ruling it implements: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792491122000-architect-j0c-error-parity.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md (equivalence E1; helper patch required). The orchestrator re-ran: oracle fixtures unchanged vs HEAD (sha256 5aee6d10), bb build:jolt-native exit 0, parity exit 0 \"SUMMARY ok\", round-1 probe 11 PASS, smoke exit 0.\nCheck: (a) is the E1 projection in the harness FAITHFUL to the ruling (strict for ok packets, first-failure, probe answers; for error packets: validate!, >=1 error, first error kind+span equal, line/column equal for real tokens and equal to pinned per-host constants for lexer-made tokens, non-empty message; message text and errors after the first exempt)? Could the harness pass while the packets are wrong? Are the 7 deliberately-broken self-test packets a real test of the checker (does it refuse them for the right reasons)? Are the 9 pinned entries honest (pins equal the JVM values, each with a reason) or is the pin set tuned to pass; the ruling said pins live in the fixture but the brief put them in the harness: acceptable? (b) the C++ helper patch in the manifest: digest-pinned, same mechanism as Java/JS/Dart, fails closed; is the DEDENT guard (missing previous token) sound and disclosed; do Java/JS/Dart :generated digests and manifest entries stay byte-identical to base (verify against git); (c) the native layout v2 (token type added to error records): backward compatibility of the round-1 entry point and round-2 JPX1 buffer, validation in cst.jolt, version checks, exception containment and fault seam unchanged; (d) G1: cst.jolt platform handling; (e) does the report match the logs; is anything forbidden touched; is the report honest about what is NOT done (Linux unrun, G1 untested on Linux, section 7.8 wording unconfirmed). Under 600 words, final response only, header fields, verdict SIGNED OFF / SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES / NOT SIGNED OFF, findings table (id, severity, file:line, evidence, correction), what you could not verify."}],"meta":{"source":"user","createdAt":1792496859053,"messageId":"c90fc5dc-0656-44e0-a339-c9a2a259d676"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Read the report, the architect's ruling, and the four modified files. Then verify.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I'll read the round-3 report and the Architect's ruling first.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_38419dd232894973bf74c9b9","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792491448000-engineer-jolt-j0c-r3.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_12ef8e2e71284a8e812ab979","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792491122000-architect-j0c-error-parity.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792496883514,"messageId":"5ebe8b64-3ad0-4034-b001-8728ed622c5e"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_38419dd232894973bf74c9b9","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 135 lines\n1: Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 10:25:03 GMT\n2: Created-Local: 2026-10-20 17:25:03 +07\n3: Coding-Agent: claude\n4: Session-ID: 4f6a4fdd-c79b-44eb-97f2-1ce81ad82aa3\n5: # Findings: J0c round 3 - the Architect's E1 standard, the required C++ helper patch, and G1\n6: Role: Compiler & AST / Stream engineer\n7: Implementers:\n8: - Model: claude-opus-5-5 | Assigned: 2026-10-20 17:17:28 +07 | Status: done (round 3, macOS arm64) | Rationale: owner routing \"opus-5.5 for implementation, cmd for review\"\n9: \n10: ## Verdict\n11: \n12: **51/51 corpus entries pass E1** (`r3-parity-final.log`: 106 PASS, 0 FAIL, exit 0).\n13: - `packet/validate!` passes, source-aware, on every jolt packet.\n14: - first-failure: 51/51 equal to the oracle.\n15: - Probes: 40/40 answers and `first-failure` call sequences equal.\n16: - Strict packet equality is **48/51**. It is still reported, as `RECORD strict-diff` lines:\n17:   - `malformed-unexpected-dedent`: message `''` vs `'<2>'`;\n18:   - `bracket-dict-open`: the expecting-set in the message;\n19:   - `early-under-call`: the third error is absent.\n20:   - All three differences are in fields E1 exempts.\n21: - The helper patch closed the line/column differences. Entries 1, 2 and the line of entry 3 from round 2 are now strictly equal.\n22: \n23: **No entry fails E1, so there was nothing to stop at.** E1 was not loosened. The oracle fixture is unchanged: SHA-256 `5aee6d10…` before and after, and the JVM regeneration is identical.\n24: \n25: **Platform:** macOS arm64 only. Linux x86_64 is NOT RUN.\n26: **Worktree:** `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c` (branch `jolt-j0c`, base HEAD `29986f9a`). Nothing is staged or committed.\n27: **Logs:** `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/r3-*`.\n28: \n29: **Spec text assumed.** E1 exactly as `collab/1792491122000-architect-j0c-error-parity.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md` \"The standard (E1)\" defines it. That ruling reads §7.8 J0c \"canonical CST/diagnostic packet equality\" as E1. **The owner has not confirmed that amendment, and I did not edit the design document.** One choice deviates from the ruling: it says the per-host pins are \"recorded in the fixture\", but your brief says to state them in the harness, so I followed the brief. The pins are in `src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj` `helper-pins`, and the oracle fixture is untouched.\n30: \n31: ## Item → change\n32: \n33: | item | change |\n34: |---|---|\n35: | (1) Required helper patch (Architect (d)) | `antlr/python3/manifest.edn` `:targets :cpp :patches \"Cpp/Python3LexerBase.cpp\"`, the same exact-text mechanism as the other targets. (a) `commonToken`'s factory call, used for INDENT and the end-of-input EOF, now takes `getLine(), getCharPositionInLine()` from the lexer instead of `lastToken ? … : 0`, matching Java's `new CommonToken(_tokenFactorySourcePair, …)`. (b) The existing positioned-DEDENT replacement gains `if (lastToken) dedent->setLine(lastToken->getLine());`, matching Java's `createDedent`. Java dereferences unconditionally; the guard only keeps a null from crashing the process, which is disclosed in the manifest comment. Regenerated with `clojure -M:antlr-gen --target cpp`. The first run failed closed on the digest check as expected (exit 1). I recorded the new digest; re-runs exit 0. Rebuilt with `bb build:jolt-native` |\n36: | (2) E1 parity harness | `src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj`, detailed below |\n37: | (2, prerequisite) offending token type | Telling a helper-fabricated token from a real one needs the offending token's type, which round 2 did not marshal. `jolt_parser.cpp`: each JPX1 error record gains `i32 offending token type` (−2 when there is no token), and the layout version goes **1 → 2**. `cst.jolt` accepts only layout 2 and decodes `:type`. Round-1 `jolt_parser_parse`/JPT1 is unchanged |\n38: | (3) cmd G1 | `cst.jolt` `platform`: `(or (System/getProperty \"os.name\") \"\")`. nil reads as Linux, as `jolt.socket.native/os` decides; a nil `os.arch` reads as x86_64. I chose the nil guard over requiring `jolt.socket.native`, so the adapter's load closure stays `yang.antlr.cst` + `export` + `packet` (the harness asserts this) |\n39: | (4) Re-run | All in the foreground on macOS; see the command table |\n40: \n41: ### The E1 harness (item 2)\n42: \n43: **Strict checks:**\n44: - every field of an ok packet;\n45: - for error packets, every field except `:yang.cst/errors` (outcome, version, unit, grammar, complete);\n46: - `cst/first-failure`;\n47: - every probe answer and its `first-failure` call sequence.\n48: \n49: **Every jolt packet passes `(packet/validate! p {:source source})`.**\n50: \n51: **Error packets also require:**\n52: - at least one error;\n53: - the first error's `:kind` and `:span` equal to the JVM's;\n54: - the first error's `:message` is a non-empty string;\n55: - the first error's `:line`/`:column` equal to the JVM's when the offending token is real. When it is helper-fabricated, they must equal the pinned constant.\n56:   - Helper-fabricated means INDENT, DEDENT, EOF, or a zero-width NEWLINE. This is decided from jolt's own record of the offending token's type.\n57:   - An unpinned helper-token entry fails, a pin on an entry with a real-token first error fails, and a pin that names no corpus entry fails.\n58: \n59: **Exempt:** message text, expecting-sets, and errors after the first.\n60: \n61: **Pinned jolt constants,** one-line reasons in the harness. They all equal the JVM's now, because the patch gives the C++ helper the Java helper's coordinates.\n62: \n63: | entry | [line col] |\n64: |---|---|\n65: | `blank-lines` | [3 2] |\n66: | `missing-final-newline` | [1 5] |\n67: | `malformed-unexpected-indent` | [1 2] |\n68: | `malformed-unexpected-dedent` | [2 0] |\n69: | `incomplete-def-header` | [2 0] |\n70: | `incomplete-decorator` | [2 0] |\n71: | `incomplete-continuation` | [2 0] |\n72: | `bracket-list-open` | [3 0] |\n73: | `bracket-dict-open` | [2 0] |\n74: \n75: I found these 9 entries with a discovery pass (`r3-discover.log`): the first errors whose offending type is INDENT, DEDENT or EOF. The one NEWLINE first error, `malformed-unexpected-newline`, has width, so it is real and checked against the JVM.\n76: \n77: **E1 self-test (7 PASS), so the checker is not vacuous.** Mutated jolt packets are refused for:\n78: - an INDENT at line 0, which is the round-2 C++ helper's value: flagged `:validate!` and `:helper-pin-drift`;\n79: - a helper-token column off its pin;\n80: - a real-token line off the JVM's;\n81: - a first-error span off the JVM's;\n82: - an empty message;\n83: - no errors;\n84: - an ok packet with one node changed.\n85: \n86: ## Digests before → after\n87: \n88: **manifest `:cpp :generated`:** only `Python3LexerBase.cpp` changed, from `65027edf9cf43a5d09c745b4936576d3bd5da466b733f337420f73ae368a4bed` to `2483e344297051efebf6f8228647eda53186b143874699e4062f1b8c9c3c5660`. The other 7 cpp files are unchanged (`a8be1b51…`, `a1cb6339…`, `8548fd2c…`, `d30cc4df…`, `5e62a9c3…`, `38a2790b…`, `59afaba9…`).\n89: \n90: **Java/JS/Dart byte-identical to base** (`r3-gen-compare.log`, after `bb gen:antlr` over every target, exit 0):\n91: - the `:java`, `:javascript` and `:dart` target maps equal `git show 29986f9a:antlr/python3/manifest.edn`, read with `clojure.edn`;\n92: - each target's freshly written `generated-digests.edn` equals its manifest `:generated` (java, javascript, dart and cpp all true);\n93: - the top-level keys are unchanged;\n94: - the `:cpp` map differs from base only in `:patches` and `:generated`.\n95: \n96: **dylib:** `1e4654562541901abcbedb9bea46d43d040c28a48d02c4d3f136bed1ef31c1f1` (round 2) → `186260ecbcfd7d2ec274d2e6a92d8845b67c5622a84b11f11c8afb8e3aef5936` (helper patch + JPX1 v2). In between, the helper patch alone gave `19559372…`.\n97: \n98: **Oracle fixture:** `5aee6d10fb382899772c73963d2a0c5bc076e9a9b5f98d53f5b319491a27dd33`, unchanged, and regenerated identically on the JVM.\n99: \n100: ## Commands (foreground, in the worktree) and exit codes\n101: \n102: | command | exit | log |\n103: |---|---|---|\n104: | `clojure -M:antlr-gen --target cpp` (patched, old digest still recorded: fails closed as expected) | 1 | `r3-gen-cpp-1.log` |\n105: | the same, after recording `2483e344…` | 0 | `r3-gen-cpp.log` |\n106: | `bb gen:antlr` (every target) and the digest comparison to base | 0 / all true | `r3-gen-all.log`, `r3-gen-compare.log` |\n107: | `bb build:jolt-native` (helper patch), then again after JPX1 v2 | 0, 0 | `r3-build.log` |\n108: | discovery of first-error token types (throwaway `build/j0c/r2/discover.clj`, under jolt) | 0 | `r3-discover.log` |\n109: | `jolt -Srepro -Sdeps '{:aliases {:j0c-probe-stub {:extra-paths [\"src/dev/jolt-j0c-probe-stub\"]}}}' -M:jolt:j0c-probe-stub src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj` (first E1 run) | 0 | `r3-parity-1.log` |\n110: | the same, final (with the self-test): 106 PASS, `e1 51`, `strict-packets-equal 48`, `first-failure-equal 51` | 0 | `r3-parity-final.log` |\n111: | `jolt -Srepro -M:jolt src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj` (round 1, unmodified) | 0, 11 PASS | `r3-probe-r1.log` |\n112: | `bb smoke:jolt` | 0, SUMMARY ok | `r3-smoke.log` |\n113: | `clojure -M:dev -m jolt-j0c-oracle` (determinism; SHA-256 unchanged) | 0 | `r3-oracle-rerun.log` |\n114: | `clojure -M:test -n yang.python.antlr.cst-export-test` (JVM, after regenerating Java) | 0: 16 tests, 173 assertions, 0 failures | `r3-jvm-cst-export.log` |\n115: | `clojure -M:antlr-gen --target cpp` after the final manifest-comment edit (digests still match) | 0 | `r3-gen-cpp-final.log` |\n116: | `mise exec -- cljstyle check` on `cst.jolt` and the harness | clean | n/a |\n117: | `mise exec -- cljstyle check` on `manifest.edn` | 3 hunks, all the pre-existing JS-patch indentation hunks that base also has | n/a |\n118: | `clojure -M: