ses, `:end` when\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-300-   the first error failed on an end-of-input token, else `:early`\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt-301-   (`export/failure-kind` decides)."}]},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_6ac6ff0f954d45beb40d9bf2","content":[{"type":"text","text":".claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_native.clj:138:   in one of two variants: :test (-DJOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS=ON, installed at\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_native.clj:139:   jolt-parser/, the library the probes and harnesses load) and :release (the\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_native.clj:140:   CMake default, seams OFF, at jolt-parser-release/). The generated sources\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_native.clj:146:        dir (if (= :test variant) \"jolt-parser\" \"jolt-parser-release\")\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_native.clj:157:          (str \"-DJOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS=\" (if (= :test variant) \"ON\" \"OFF\")))\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_native.clj:179:        release (build-parser! built cmake-bin plat :release)\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/src/dev/jolt_native.clj:186:                :jolt-parser-release release}"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"ea815daf-8230-4b19-bfea-e6bb4fd44683"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"One issue with CMakeLists to point out: `if(JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS) target_compile_definitions(... JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS)` — the C++ side uses `#ifdef JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS`. The compile definition is set without a value → defined as 1 → ifdef works. OK.\n\nexports.darwin `_jolt_parser_*` — wildcard export list; test build exports 12, release 7 (seams are excluded at compile time). Orchestrator verified via nm. Note: exports.darwin exports all jolt_parser_* including seams in test builds — as intended.\n\nWait — a fine point: in the release build, `maybe_fault()`/`fail_handler_malloc()` are still defined (no-op) — good; seams are excluded at compile time so the release binary contains no g_fault at all ✓ (confirmed 7 exports with nm).\n\nBOM fix correctness: SourceStream decodes itself via strictDecode, preserves U+FEFF, throws IllegalArgumentException on invalid input → caught as std::exception → -2. Used at both entry points ✓. Completeness: are there other paths that strip BOM? ANTLRInputStream's constructor from string also strips BOM, but they bypass load() by assigning directly to _data. `p = 0` reset ✓. One concern: strictDecode returns vector<uint32_t>; ANTLRInputStream::_data's type — in 4.13.2 C++, `_data` is `std::vector<uint32_t>` (UTF32). Assignment works; the empirical result of parity 13/13 strict match backs up the behavior. ✓ Fix is correct and complete within the wrapper; the upstream behavioral difference is documented in comments with file:line citations.\n\nWorker isolation evaluation (A): C++ matches the claim: worker owns the DFA vector + context cache; a fresh simulator per call via setInterpreter; Recognizer::setInterpreter deletes the previous interpreter (their citation); the generated destructor deletes the installed interpreter — backed by ASan/leaks 0. Busy guard: atomic CAS, Hold's RAII releases on all paths including exceptions (Hold is declared before try — yes, line 425, try is line 427; Hold's destructor runs on return/throw ✓). -5 is non-allocating ✓. Tag-based handle validation rejects NULL/zero-initialized; arbitrary garbage pointers are inherently unchecked (info). Evidence for 2 workers 1 thread + 4 workers 4 threads is meaningful: interleaved/reverse-order passes compared against per-source isolated references — equivalence with isolated runs is §7.4's requirement (\"packets equal to isolated runs\"). Static counter: real (positive control 2 shows the round 1 entry growing them). ATN memo 0→99: recorded, not asserted; author's analysis: pure function of ATN state, filled only once under lock, JVM shares the same memo (Java's ATN.nextTokens caches nextTokenWithinRule — true in ANTLR 4.13 Java). The claim that JVM's cst.clj shares the ATN across workers is plausible (Java's static _sharedContextCache/decisionToDFA pattern). The evidence backs up \"deterministic, locked, also shared on JVM\" — the design question is left to the Architect as instructed. My evaluation: the memo is set-once and read-only thereafter; concurrency-safe due to _mutex; content is input-independent; equivalence risk is nil. It is \"mutable shared state\" if §7.4 is read literally; the author's option (a) pre-fill at initialization is low-cost and would eliminate this issue — but I won't decide.\n\nB: child-process isolation demonstrated (harness writes a complete summary after numerous crashes; fail-exit is recorded). All the numbers in the report are present in the logs (verified parens/list/call/unary/not/power/lambda/ternary/indent rows). Method is sound: doubling + 16-step subdivision + fresh child-process full-path reconfirmation at the last passing depth; native-only control attributes overflow to native recursion. Unary thread row: report states \"640 passes (fresh full rerun 20.7s)\" and subdivision stops at time cap 672, fail-exit 0 — matches log row 8 (:fails 672, :fail-exit 0). Honest labeling as time-constrained. GC claim is credible: numbers match; a 64ms GC within a 36-second blocking call with 5,237 unrelated ticks — consistent with J0a's observation; no overclaiming (plain control is recorded, not asserted).\n\nC: input corpus 13 entries; oracle is JVM-generated (fixture header + parity check \"input oracle is the JVM's\" PASS); all strict true (stronger than E1); BOM entries are now syntax-error :early, matching JVM. Invalid UTF-8 6/6 fails closed at -2 with no buffer; size overflow -4 + reparse; invalid args 4/4 -1. Fixture comment references non-existent `src/dev/jolt_j0c_input.clj` — the tests exist in jolt_j0c_parity.clj. INFO finding.\n\nD: cycles: log numbers match the report exactly (1304240→1416912 = +112,672; bare is +114,448); \"flat after initial rise\" is visible in samples. Assertion is honest as \"growth under 1 MiB\" (not zero). ASan+UBSan is HARNESS ok with exit 0; leaks 0. LSan not available on macOS — disclosed, detect_leaks set for Linux (unexecuted). Sound within the scope of a standalone C++ harness — caveat: the harness validates the wrapper, not jolt's FFI paths; jolt-side cycles cover that via heap flatness. TSan not done — disclosed.\n\nE: exported symbols list + version script; option default OFF; both variants built and recorded; release exports 7, no hooks (orchestrator's nm); layout-version entry added and checked both at cst.jolt load time (:68-71) and per-buffer (:141) — closes G2/H3; non-allocating failure paths maintained (message() unchanged except for the seam-gated fail_handler_malloc); Linux version script is untested — disclosed.\n\nF: prohibited regions: round 2/3 oracle/corpus are unchanged (report; parity totals identical 51/48/40 + new input section), manifest digest unchanged (2483e344 still present, cpp patch unchanged), no changes to shared code. The report is honest about incomplete items: Linux, close-worker not integrated into shared callers (design gap A.1 disclosed with the repl_probe worker leak), J5 budget policy undecided, no input size cap, TSan, §7.4 memo issue unresolved.\n\nFindings table:\n- K1 info: fixture comment cites non-existent src/dev/jolt_j0c_input.clj (tests are in jolt_j0c_parity.clj). Fix the comment.\n- K2 info: worker handle tag check cannot defend against arbitrary dangling/garbage pointers (inherent); close-while-busy is documented as caller responsibility. Fine as a probe; note for J5 hardening (possibly a busy check in worker_free).\n- K3 info: generated_atns() constructs and discards lexer/parser per worker_new (minor cost, plus an interpreter allocation/deallocation); could potentially be cached under call_once. Cosmetic issue.\n- Also: no bound on walk recursion in `Out::room` (known, §7.6 J5).\n- Unary's time explosion: no defense; author explicitly flags that only input bounds are effective; deferred to J5 — honest.\n\nVerdict: SIGNED OFF. Under 700 words.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 14:53:56 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 21:53:56 +07\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\n# Review: J0c round 4 (worker isolation, stack/thread, input policy, leaks, hardening)\nRole: Reviewer (read-only) | Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max\n\n## Verdict: SIGNED OFF\n\n## Findings\n\n| id | sev | file:line | evidence | correction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| K1 | info | test/.../jolt_j0c_input_corpus.edn:7-9 | Header cites `src/dev/jolt_j0c_input.clj`, which does not exist; the C-ABI tests live in `jolt_j0c_parity.clj` (log lines 134-145). | Fix the comment. |\n| K2 | info | jolt_parser.cpp:375-378, :407-412 | The tag check rejects NULL/zeroed handles but cannot detect a dangling or arbitrary garbage pointer; `worker_free` while busy is caller-prohibition by comment only. cst.jolt's CAS-close is the real guard. | For J5: refuse `worker_free` on a busy worker (leak instead of UB). |\n| K3 | info | jolt_parser.cpp:350-356 | `generated_atns()` constructs and destroys a throwaway lexer/parser per `worker_new` (extra interpreter alloc/delete); the ATNs are process statics. | Cache under `call_once`; cosmetic. |\n\n## Checks\n\n- (A) **Worker isolation is real in the C++**: `Worker` owns fresh DFA vectors + two `PredictionContextCache`s (:358-373); each export installs new simulators over them via `setInterpreter` (:434-439), replacing the generated static-cache defaults; RAII `Hold` (:381-391, constructed before the `try`) gives the fail-closed −5 busy refusal on every exit path, allocating nothing. The test evidence is meaningful: 51 sources parsed under isolated reference vs 2-workers-interleaved-opposite-orders vs 4-workers-on-4-gated-threads, all equal (r4-workers-final.log:2,4); the static counters are proven live by two positive controls - the worker's own caches fill (89/265, 143/254/213) and a round-1 static-cache parse grows the statics (33/64, 57/60/105) while worker parses leave them byte-identical (:6,:8). **ATN memo**: the evidence supports the author's characterization - `ATN::nextTokens` is a lock-guarded, set-once memo of a pure function of immutable ATN state, it grew 0→99 (RECORD only, :7), and Java's runtime caches the same field on the same shared generated ATN, so the JVM oracle shares it too. I do not decide whether §7.4's \"mutable caches may not be shared\" covers it; the author correctly flagged it, proposed two remedies, and chose neither.\n- (B) **Stack/thread**: every depth ran in a child process - the harness survived 11+ recorded crashes (exit 139/132 captured per row) and wrote the full summary, which is itself proof of isolation. Every number in the report appears verbatim in r4-stack.log (parens main 10752/11264/139, thread 544/576/132, unary main time-limited 512 ok 10.3 s → 1024 at 93,547 ms, thread unary fail-exit 0 at the 20 s cap, honestly labelled time-bound). Method (doubling → 16-step refinement → fresh cold-cache child at the last pass through the full jolt path, plus native-only controls attributing the overflow to native recursion) is sound; the JVM comparison (512 throws) is honest about there being no oracle past the JVM's own limit. **GC claim credible**: r4-gc.log matches exactly (call 0.1→35,826 ms; gc 206.8→271.1; 5,237 unrelated ticks; plain control recorded not asserted); no `:blocking`-semantics claim is built on it.\n- (C) **Input policy**: the 13-entry oracle is JVM-only (generator requires only JVM namespaces; parity asserts `:host :jvm` + grammar-id; fixture forbids cross-host edits), and all 13 pass **strictly** - stronger than E1. The BOM fix is correct and complete: `SourceStream` decodes via the runtime's own `strictDecode`, keeping U+FEFF, and bypasses `ANTLRInputStream::load`'s strip at both entries; invalid UTF-8 becomes −2 fail-closed. Invalid UTF-8 (6 vectors incl. overlong and encoded surrogate), oversize (−4 mid-growth via `Out::room`, then a clean re-parse), and bad args (4) all fail closed with no buffer. The pre-fix BOM behaviour (a wrong *successful* packet, caught by `validate!`) is exactly what C exists to catch, and it was disclosed.\n- (D) **Leaks**: cycles log matches the report digit-for-digit (1,304,240→1,416,912; flat after ~1,000/3,000); the assertion is honestly \"growth under 1 MiB\", not zero. ASan+UBSan harness exit 0 \"HARNESS ok\", `leaks --atExit` 0 leaks; LSan-unavailable-on-macOS and no-TSan disclosed. Note the sanitizer harness is standalone C++ (never loaded into jolt) - the jolt FFI path is covered by the cycles flatness instead; that split is reasonable and described.\n- (E) **Hardening**: `option(JOLT_PARSER_TEST_SEAMS ... OFF)` gates the seams AND the fault branches (`maybe_fault`/`fail_handler_malloc` are no-ops in release); exported-symbols list/version script in CMake; release exports exactly 7, no test hooks (orchestrator nm); both variants built and recorded with their symbol lists; `jolt_parser_export_layout_version()` = 2 checked at cst.jolt load (:68-71) and per buffer (:141) - G2/H3 closed; `message()` nonallocating path preserved; Linux version script untested - disclosed.\n- (F) Nothing forbidden touched: round-2/3 oracle (`5aee6d10…`) and manifest digests unchanged (`2483e344…` still recorded; totals identical at e1 51/strict 48/probes 40), no shared code edits. Report is honest: Linux unrun, `close-worker` not wired into shared callers (probe/stage worker leak explicitly disclosed), J5 budget policy unchosen, no input-size bound, TSan missing.\n\n## Could not verify\n\n- Any hash/dylib bytes or `nm` output myself (no shell); shiva runs (none claimed); the `Recognizer.cpp:88-91` and `ANTLRInputStream.cpp:43-48` citations against the pinned runtime source; Java `ATN.nextTokens` memo sharing (from my knowledge of 4.13.x, consistent); the JVM stack-comparison numbers (no log cited); Linux seams (`detect_leaks`, version script, `mallinfo2`, glibc `thread_info`) - all compile-guarded but unexercised."}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792508363098,"messageId":"98ed17bf-9819-47b3-a595-30ec281e2068"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 18:07:54 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-21 01:07:54 +07\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\n# Task: review the libx11-dev declaration commit on jolt-j0a\nRole: Reviewer (read-only)\nImplementers:\n- Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max | Assigned: 2026-10-21 01:07:54 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: owner: \"perform a cmd review\"; the author of the change is the orchestrator (glm), the commit was made on the owner's explicit instruction without a prior review\n\nREAD-ONLY review of commit d5b4ce95 on branch jolt-j0a (worktree /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0a; use git show d5b4ce95 and git log there; base 380ab4f2): one file, jolt-toolchain.edn, 7 insertions adding \"libx11-dev\" to :os-packages :debian :required. Context: owner decision \"lets assume debian distribution and apt get for dependency management of package\"; bb bootstrap:jolt (src/dev/jolt_bootstrap.clj, committed in 380ab4f2, cmd signed off then) checks the required packages with dpkg-query before any mise build and never runs sudo or apt. Evidence the orchestrator produced: on jupiter (Ubuntu 24.04.5) the mise Chez build failed with \"c/expeditor.c:1191: fatal error: X11/Xlib.h: No such file or directory\" until libx11-dev was installed (log /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/build/jolt-spike/j0c-jupiter/jupiter-mise-install.log, grep the error); shiva already had the X11 headers (so it never failed); after the change: the EDN parses, the test seam JOLT_BOOTSTRAP_TEST_DEBIAN_INSTALLED=gcc,make,git,libncurses-dev names libx11-dev with the exact \"sudo apt-get install -y libx11-dev\", macOS bootstrap and smoke pass, the real Debian path on shiva reports \"all 5 required Debian packages installed: gcc git libncurses-dev libx11-dev make\", and dpkg on jupiter shows all five installed.\nCheck: (a) is the comment's evidence accurate (open the jupiter log; is the failing line c/expeditor.c:1191 and the include X11/Xlib.h; is the claim that the expression editor uses X11 for the clipboard supported by the Chez source at c/expeditor.c:1189-1212 in the mise-installed or downloaded tarball, e.g. under ~/.local/share/mise/downloads/chezscheme-10.4.1/ or the extracted sources you can find; are --disable-x11 and --disable-curses really configure options in that source); (b) is the package name right for the Debian family (libx11-dev on Debian/Ubuntu); is anything else the Chez source build includes that the same machine lacked (grep the jupiter log for other missing headers/libraries; note that the first jupiter failure list had only X11); (c) does the declaration stay consistent with the file's conventions (comment style, the :unverified entries incl. the already-corrected libssl3 note); (d) one-entry change, no other file touched; (e) honest description in the commit message. Under 300 words, final response only, header fields, verdict SIGNED OFF / SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES / NOT SIGNED OFF, findings table (id, severity, file:line, evidence, correction), and what you could not verify."}],"meta":{"source":"user","createdAt":1792519675776,"messageId":"65e24b91-6edf-4157-9ee1-fd23b340349c"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Cannot execute git commands (shell is blocked). I need to read jolt-toolchain.edn in the worktree (the committed state should match), grep for errors in the jupiter logs, and check the Chez source in the mise download for c/expeditor.c:1189-1212 and the configure options.\n\nSteps:\n1. Read jolt-toolchain.edn in the jolt-j0a worktree (os-packages section).\n2. Grep for X11/expeditor errors in jupiter-mise-install.log.\n3. Look for the Chez source: ~/.local/share/mise/downloads/chezscheme-10.4.1/ — search with Glob. The workspace root is /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld; the home directory is outside the workspace — can read_file read files outside the workspace? \"Must be within the workspace (current directory or its additional directories).\" ~ is probably not within the workspace. Try Glob with a path specification... it will probably be rejected. I can try read_file directly against ~/.local/share/mise/downloads/chezscheme-10.4.1/..., but it might be rejected. Let me try.\n4. Commit message: can't run git show. Maybe I can read the .git files? The worktree's git directory: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0a/.git is a file that points to the main repository's worktrees directory. I can read commit objects — they're zlib-compressed so read_file can't read them. COMMIT_EDITMSG might exist in the worktree's git directory: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.git/worktrees/jolt-j0a/COMMIT_EDITMSG. Let me try reading that.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I'll read the declaration, the jupiter log, and try to reach the commit message and Chez source.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_c99280377a9c4f60a7a1a203","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0a/jolt-toolchain.edn"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_64b0daa051b54fe7a6f14233","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"X11|expeditor|fatal error|No such file","path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/build/jolt-spike/j0c-jupiter/jupiter-mise-install.log","output_mode":"content","-n":true,"-C":3}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792519717232,"messageId":"ff2022a3-49e8-481e-ad96-aefdb893f832"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_c99280377a9c4f60a7a1a203","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 96 lines\n1: ;; The jolt host lane's toolchain declaration\n2: ;; (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §6.1, §6.4).\n3: ;;\n4: ;; `bb bootstrap:jolt` materializes exactly this into build/tools/jolt and\n5: ;; verifies it; ordinary runs never pull. Changing any pin here goes through\n6: ;; the upgrade gate (`qualify:jolt-upgrade`, §6.4), never silently.\n7: {:jolt\n8:  {:origin \"https://github.com/jolt-lang/jolt\"\n9:   :sha \"96d7b8662d7c491722445bf976b2e25e36ce5d63\"\n10:   :committed \"2026-10-19T08:26:43-04:00\"\n11:   ;; ruling 4 (§13.4): kept although untagged\n12:   :tag :none\n13:   :nearest-release-tag {:tag \"v0.8.23\"\n14:                         :sha \"a0ac71c3f638fe55158bd01b4ecbb0dd3d7545e5\"}\n15:   :describe \"vnightly-16-g96d7b866\"\n16:   :dir \"build/tools/jolt\"\n17:   ;; recursive submodule SHAs at :sha (git submodule status --recursive);\n18:   ;; bootstrap refuses a checkout whose submodules differ\n19:   :submodules {\"vendor/cli\" \"14f07644080070246974942415a104228d9a6b04\"\n20:                \"vendor/clojure-test-suite\" \"489b6743e8421687ef96cec557830acf258d1886\"\n21:                \"vendor/fs\" \"5b273b8a943a622593fbc85fca6761c5a39d6d66\"\n22:                \"vendor/grenadine\" \"688e0ed6618582eccc8c8b745243ceac92835e03\"\n23:                \"vendor/irregex\" \"c948a704fc732914a243c1643bfe359913d11c7b\"\n24:                \"vendor/process\" \"43bdd65e189f2ccacc56662c53a21f5470ff1500\"\n25:                \"vendor/sci\" \"32d62a5136ad3dc148588752f5bcc4cc30b14752\"}\n26:   ;; proposed default 2 (§13.2): none recorded, so none applied\n27:   :patches []}\n28: \n29:  ;; Host OS packages (owner 2026-10-20: Linux = the Debian family with apt).\n30:  ;; `bb bootstrap:jolt` checks :required with dpkg-query on hosts whose\n31:  ;; /etc/os-release ID or ID_LIKE names debian, BEFORE anything mise builds,\n32:  ;; and fails naming the missing packages and the exact\n33:  ;; `sudo apt-get install -y ...` command. It never runs sudo or apt.\n34:  ;; :unverified entries are candidates with no step shown to need them yet;\n35:  ;; they are listed, not checked. Chez and CMake stay mise-provided.\n36:  :os-packages\n37:  {:debian\n38:   {:required\n39:    {;; mise builds Chez 10.4.1 from source and links it against ncurses;\n40:     ;; verified on shiva (Ubuntu 24.04.5): \"cannot find -lncurses\" until\n41:     ;; this was installed (docs/orchestrator-log.md, J0b-on-shiva entry)\n42:     \"libncurses-dev\" \"mise Chez source build (link)\"\n43:     ;; Chez's expression editor (c/expeditor.c:1189-1212) reads the X11\n44:     ;; clipboard; verified on jupiter (Ubuntu 24.04.5): the build failed with\n45:     ;; \"c/expeditor.c:1191: fatal error: X11/Xlib.h: No such file or\n46:     ;; directory\" until this was installed (shiva had the headers already,\n47:     ;; so it never failed there). Chez's configure also offers --disable-x11\n48:     ;; and --disable-curses, which the mise plugin does not pass.\n49:     \"libx11-dev\" \"mise Chez source build (compile, X11 clipboard in the editor)\"\n50:     ;; the same Chez source build compiles and links C (the failing step\n51:     ;; above was its link); present on shiva, so not independently shown\n52:     \"gcc\" \"mise Chez source build (C compiler)\"\n53:     \"make\" \"mise Chez source build (make)\"\n54:     ;; bootstrap:jolt clones jolt and its submodules (src/dev/jolt_bootstrap.clj)\n55:     \"git\" \"bootstrap:jolt clone and verification\"}\n56:    :unverified\n57:    {;; J0c native build (jolt-j0c branch, bb build:jolt-native) compiles the\n58:     ;; ANTLR4 C++ runtime and wrapper and unzips the runtime source; shown on\n59:     ;; macOS only, not yet on a Debian host\n60:     \"g++\" \"J0c native build (C++ compiler)\"\n61:     \"unzip\" \"J0c native build (runtime source archive)\"\n62:     ;; §6.3 / Q4: system libcrypto via apt, version-checked before use (J1).\n63:     ;; The package NAME differs per release: on Ubuntu 24.04 libcrypto.so.3 is\n64:     ;; owned by libssl3t64 (dpkg -S verified on shiva) and libssl3 is not\n65:     ;; installed there; \"libssl3\