ouble/isNaN ^Double a)) (not (Double/isNaN ^Double b))))\n82:          :cljs (and (number? a) (number? b)\n83:                     (not (js/isNaN a)) (not (js/isNaN b))))"}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"8951e407-a8f1-444f-80ec-a3e853c31988"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Confirmed: the actual host arm on line 78 (instance? Long/Double, js/isNaN, dart/is?) matches the :h0-unlisted table entry exactly. Not exempt (the arms differ, it's not a hint, and it's not a catch either).\n\nNow going to write the review. Under 700 words. Verdict: SIGNED OFF (F1 was escalated by design; the author's own gate conditions H1 on resolving F1). Or SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES? The :h0-unlisted mechanism makes the gate green even though §8.1 states that list 1 must match the baseline, and \"any difference in either direction is an H0 finding that should be resolved before H1.\" The author recorded this and deferred it to the architect—the design's own process. The gate still freezes the tree (new arms outside the list will fail; list 2 is a count ceiling everywhere). My take is SIGNED OFF, with an explicit condition being that F1 must be resolved before H1 (which the design already requires). Findings table: some info items.\n\nFindings:\n- M1 (low/info): due to list 1's file granularity, the 16 :h0-unlisted files can have new non-exempt arms added without the gate failing (only the effect count is ceiling'd). This is inherent to the file-granularity list 1 in §8.1, but it's the :h0-unlisted batch that broadens that allowance. F1 must be resolved before H1 (design's own rule; author also complies). Optionally: record the arm line numbers for :h0-unlisted files and fail on new lines—but that exceeds the design's granularity, so I'll just recommend it to the architect.\n- M2 info: the tool's location deviates from §8's literal `dev/host_boundary/` → src/dev/host_boundary is legitimate and disclosed. It runs via an inline -Sdeps alias (acceptable: no edits to deps.edn, and the wide-change exit 2 of test:changed is disclosed). Recommend adding a proper alias if the tool becomes permanent from H1 onward.\n- M3 info: the syntax-quote (`) form is not skipped in the effect-calls walk (only 'quote and comment are)—symbols inside syntax-quoted data end up being counted. The direction is over-counting (fail-closed), and list-2-equals-observed pins the current tree. Mention as a note.\n- M4 info: the SHA-256 vs BLAKE3 choice is disclosed.\n\nChecks (a)–(e) summary and evidence. Things I could not verify: test execution (counts 10/65), --check rc 0, lane counts (the author's quick-tier runs; orchestrator only partially), target/h0 logs, r3 reviewer's totals, whether tools.namespace 1.5.0's deps-from-ns-decl actually excludes :as-alias/string libspecs (from library knowledge; traversal B agrees empirically, and its libspec-deps explicitly handles both—this agreement is cross-validated).\n\nWord budget: target around 650 words.\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 18:56:02 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-21 01:56:02 +07\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\n# Review: host-boundary H0 (closure tool, gate, corpus hashes)\nRole: Reviewer (read-only) | Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max\n\n## Verdict: SIGNED OFF\n\nCondition the design itself imposes: F1 (list 1 vs §8.1 baseline) must be resolved by the Architect before H1; the author gated exactly that way.\n\n## Findings\n\n| id | sev | file:line | evidence | correction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| M1 | low | gate_test.clj:515-531 | List 1 is file-granular (§8.1's own design), so the 16 `:h0-unlisted` files may gain NEW non-exempt arms without failing the gate; only effect counts have ceilings. Each entry is line-cited (I verified `dao/space/index.cljc:78` - a genuine `instance? Long/Double`/`js/isNaN`/`dart/is?` arm, non-exempt), and F1 is escalated, not buried. | Acceptable for H0; when the Architect categories the 16, consider line-level pins for any file kept on the list. |\n| M2 | info | closure.clj ns doc; report §\"Location\" | Tool lives at `src/dev/host_boundary/` (repo convention; §8's literal `dev/` exists nowhere) and runs via inline `-Sdeps`, so deps.edn/bb.edn stay untouched; `bb test:changed` exits 2 (wide), disclosed. | Fine now; add a real alias if the tool persists past H1. |\n| M3 | info | gate_test.clj:393 | Only `'quote`/`comment`/`ns` are skipped; syntax-quoted data would count its symbols as calls. Direction is over-counting (fail-closed), and `list-2-equals-the-observed-calls` pins today's tree exactly, so no slack exists. | Note for H2's library mode. |\n\n## Checks\n\n- (a) **Closure tool faithful**: lane features `#{:clj}/#{:cljs}/#{:cljd :clj}`, first-arm-wins via tools.reader; resolution `src/<lane>` then `src/cljc` exactly as §3.1; ns-name/file mismatch throws (fail-closed). `#?@` is handled natively by `read-ns-decl` with `:read-cond :allow` - the r3 defect class (dropped spliced requires, 141/140/139) cannot recur; `strip-nils` for `#?(:cljd nil :clj [...])` matches compiler behaviour. **Traversal B is genuinely independent**: own tools.reader `:preserve` read, own `select-arms` (splices `#?@` itself), own `libspec-deps` (`:as-alias`-only and string libspecs excluded explicitly); it shares only the design-fixed resolution rule, as disclosed. Agreement is asserted file-by-file both directions (`set/difference` both ways), not by counts - and B's independent handling of `:as-alias`/strings cross-validates tools.namespace's. Under-inclusion would show as B⊄A; over-inclusion as A⊄B; both are tested.\n- (b) **Gate**: exemptions are applied to read `ReaderConditional` forms with ancestor context (never text); comments skipped; lane-specific files count as one whole-file arm. NEG1/NEG2 are in-memory (`update` on a slurped copy; disk untouched), target chosen programmatically, and each asserts the EXACT violation list plus zero of the other kind - they fail for the right reason only. Zero files and nil-text both give `:scan-failure` with the file named, distinct from `:pass`. `list-1-equals-the-observed-arms` and `list-2-equals-the-observed-calls` assert set/map equality with observations, so no stale entry and no count slack. The `:h0-unlisted` device keeps the gate green on today's tree while still catching any new arm outside list 1 - acceptable H0 behaviour given F1 is escalated (see M1).\n- (c) **Corpus**: 18 files (6 + 12), `bb ... corpus_hashes.clj [--check]` regenerates/checks, and `corpus-bytes-match-the-frozen-hash-set` asserts both the exact file set and every digest on the JVM lane. SHA-256-vs-BLAKE3 choice disclosed with rationale.\n- (d) **Findings honest - F2 verified myself**: `random-uuid` occurs exactly 27 times in `src/cljc`; `dao/await.cljc` (2) has no requirer anywhere in `src`, and `yin.vm.ffi.remote-serve` (3) is required only by its own holder/responder, which nothing requires; neither appears in `closure-clj.edn`. 27 − 1 (terminal, design's own exclusion) − 5 = 21, matching list 2's sum. The author reported the design mismatch instead of tuning the tool; F1/F3/F4 are likewise recorded with line cites and left for the Architect (F4's `antlr/repl.cljc:71` quoted-program exclusion is consistent with the gate's quote handling, unit-tested at gate_test.clj:771).\n- (e) **Scope clean**: no `:hb`/host-boundary additions in deps.edn or bb.edn; nothing under `src/clj{,c,s,d}` touched (the change adds only `src/dev/host_boundary/`, `test/dao/host/`, `docs/design/host-boundary/`); no codec-track, jolt or design-doc edits.\n\n## Could not verify\n\n- Executing anything: the 10-test/65-assertion run, `--check` rc 0, the 142/140/141/133 totals and the quick-tier lane counts (4121→4131 JVM; Node/Dart single-run argument is sound but unrerun) rest on the orchestrator's re-runs and author logs under gitignored `target/h0/`.\n- tools.namespace 1.5.0's exact `:as-alias`/string-libspec behaviour (library knowledge + the independent-traversal agreement).\n- The r3 reviewer totals cited in §3.1."}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792522891826,"messageId":"c8bcd889-91ff-48d3-908a-387ec253bff5"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 19:04:25 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-21 02:04:25 +07\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\n# Task: confirm the host-boundary design r7 reconciliation against the code\nRole: Reviewer (read-only)\nImplementers:\n- Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max | Assigned: 2026-10-21 02:04:25 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: owner routing \"cmd for review\"; the document is by claude-fable-5-1, so a non-Claude reviewer must confirm r7 before H1 is dispatched\n\nREAD-ONLY, read ONLY under /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld. Document: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md (r7, 1,318 lines). Rationale: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792522578000-architect-host-boundary-r7.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md. H0's measured closure and report: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/host-h0/docs/design/host-boundary/closure-clj.edn (and cljs, cljd) and /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792518355000-engineer-host-boundary-h0.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md (F1-F4); the H0 gate test is /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/host-h0/test/dao/host/gate_test.clj. Check, opening the code lines: (1) F1: the document categorises the 16 unlisted files (nine profile consumers H2 migrates; two real cross-host behaviour differences; six retained dialect arms; one deferred entropy): open at least eight of the 16 cited sites in the repo (e.g. dao/space/index.cljc:78, yin/vm.cljc:404 bytes->str, yang/php.cljc:95,98) and confirm each category is right; verify the two claimed cross-host divergences (bytes->str decodes char codes on JS/Dart but UTF-8 on the JVM; js/parseInt loses precision above 2^53) against the code; confirm the six dropped names (four outside the closure, two with only exempt catch arms); (2) F2/F3/F4 statements: the three random-uuid scopes and the gate's scope; the out-of-closure namespaces removed from the 3.1 rows and the waitset drivers added to the seams row; main.cljc 13 print calls; the quoted-program println withdrawn; section 2.4 obligation (iv) now says what the gate can see (B-tree 33 calls) and what H2's audit must do; (3) the cost table (24 edited + 3 deleted) is consistent with the slice list and the false claim about dao.space.index fixed; (4) a consistency pass (duplicate headings, section numbers and cross-references, ASCII table borders, lines over 170 columns, em-dashes, counts that disagree between sections: 76 profile entries, seven profile files, five test namespaces, the closure totals, the random-uuid counts 27/26/21), the header says r7 with an accurate review status (astra's SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES covers the narrowed scope at r4; r5/r6/r7 changes were confirmed by cmd, a non-Claude reviewer, only for mechanical corrections: say whether r7's categorisation is more than mechanical and therefore deserves astra); (5) what H0 must change per the document versus what it need not (the retag) is stated unambiguously. Under 600 words, final response only, header fields, verdict CONFIRMED / CONFIRMED WITH DEFECTS (list) / NOT CONFIRMED, findings table (id, severity, file:line, evidence, correction), and what you could not verify."}],"meta":{"source":"user","createdAt":1792523071112,"messageId":"a0c5d001-baea-4303-bed3-b93b2b1c0d67"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Task: Verify r7's consistency by cross-checking the design document against the code. Read r7's document (1318 lines) — narrow down to relevant sections: header/review status, classification of 16 files in §3.1/8.1, descriptions of F2/F3/F4, cost table, §2.4(iv). Also read the architect's rationale report. After that, open at least 8 out of the 16 cited sites in the code and verify the two cross-host discrepancies.\n\nPlan:\n1. Read the r7 findings document (architect's rationale).\n2. Read the main sections of the design document. It's 1318 lines, so should I read the whole thing in chunks? That would be 2 reads of about 700 lines each. Probably better to narrow it down to the target sections with grep. But the consistency check (headings, table borders, lines over 170 columns, em dashes, counts) requires a full scan. Em dashes can be grep'd: pattern \"—\". Long lines: grep '.{171,}'. Headings: '^#'. Counts: grep for \"76\", \"27\", \"26\", \"21\", etc.\n\nWhat to execute:\n- Read the collab r7 findings.\n- Read the design document in full (2 reads).\n- grep for sites in the code.\n\nDesign document is 1318 lines — can be handled in 2 reads (the 2000-line limit). Actually, if it's 1318 lines it fits in a single read (upper limit 2000, though the output cap is 128KB — the document is probably around 120KB… risky). Read it in 2 chunks of about 660 lines each.\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"I'll read the Architect's r7 rationale and then the document itself.\n\n"},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_e42b7e71b59946ffacbfec26","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792522578000-architect-host-boundary-r7.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md"}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792523124330,"messageId":"57cdfdbc-10fe-4ff6-868a-5bad71aa4c6e"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_e42b7e71b59946ffacbfec26","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 63 lines\n1: Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 19:30:00 GMT\n2: Created-Local: 2026-10-21 02:30:00 +07\n3: Coding-Agent: claude\n4: Model: claude-fable-5-1\n5: Session-ID: 36134bec-9cbd-4852-b78c-4901af66fd02\n6: Task: host-boundary design r7, reconcile the design with what H0 measured (F1-F4)\n7: Role: Architect\n8: Deliverable: docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md edited in place (r7); no src edits, nothing staged.\n9: Labels: V verified by reading the cited lines and H0's closure edn files. No lane run. A non-Claude reviewer must confirm r7 before H1 is dispatched.\n10: \n11: # Rationale\n12: \n13: Each measurement was checked against the code; the design yields to it.\n14: \n15: **F1.** The 16 `:h0-unlisted` files split three ways. Nine are profile\n16: consumers the manifest covers and H2 migrates: byte length in\n17: `repl/query:460`, `linker/closure:32`, `ucf/rows:282`; `bytes->str` at\n18: `vm.cljc:404` (a real divergence: JS and Dart decode byte-as-char-code, the\n19: JVM decodes UTF-8, so strict UTF-8 is a named behaviour change); PHP number\n20: parsing at `php.cljc:95,98` (`js/parseInt` above 2^53 is a second); the two\n21: `isSafeInteger` checks; the index number fast path; code points in the\n22: packet IR (the portable `code-points` moves to table B). Six are retained\n23: dialect arms: three runtime-type diagnostics, two EDN reader aliases, the\n24: ring buffer's own atom test. One (`rpc.cljc:114`) is deferred entropy. The\n25: six names H0 could not find are dropped (four outside the closure, two\n26: exempt-only). H0 must retag the 16 as data; totals, tool, tests and hashes\n27: stand. §10's \"index untouched\" was false; cost is 24 edited plus 3 deleted.\n28: \n29: **F2.** Three scopes: 27 lines in `src/cljc`, 26 under OQ1's directory\n30: reading, 21 in the transitive closure. The gate uses the closure: it is\n31: what a composition can reach, it is committed and regenerated, and an\n32: unrequired file cannot affect a host until required, when it enters the\n33: closure. The five `dao.await` and `remote_serve` sites go to the deferred\n34: design's inputs with the scope question stated.\n35: \n36: **F3.** §3.1 no longer names `dao.stream.udp`, the browser adapter,\n37: `content.async` or `space.schema`; the waitset drivers and the ws seams'\n38: clock reads sit in the seams row; the `random-uuid` count reads 21.\n39: \n40: **F4.** `main.cljc` has 13 resolved print calls (r3 counted text lines).\n41: `antlr/repl.cljc:71` is a `println` inside a quoted yin program, withdrawn\n42: from §3.1, §3.2 and §9.3. §2.4 (iv) states what the gate sees (only\n43: `dao.data.btree`, 33 calls), that `chunks`, `cbor` and `sign` mutate host\n44: arrays inside arms until H2, that the freeze rule is never gate-checkable,\n45: and the four steps H2's audit takes.\n46: \n47: # Disposition\n48: \n49: +-----+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n50: | Id  | Result    | Where in r7                                                                                    |\n51: +=====+===========+================================================================================================+\n52: | F1  | accepted  | §3.1 two new rows; §8.1 first, codec, dialect-arms, deferred rows and reconciliation paragraph |\n53: |     |           | (what H0 must and need not change; how H2 clears the tag); §8.3 file list; §10 cost and risk.  |\n54: +-----+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n55: | F2  | accepted  | §3.2 \"three scopes\" paragraph; §9.1 new required input.                                        |\n56: +-----+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n57: | F3  | accepted  | §3.1 seams, entropy, timers, locking rows; §1 and §8.1 out-of-closure notes.                   |\n58: +-----+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n59: | F4  | accepted  | §3.2 counts; §3.1 stage row; §9.3; §2.4 (iv).                                                  |\n60: +-----+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n61: \n62: Tool path read as `src/dev/host_boundary/closure.clj` (H0's justified\n63: deviation) in §3.1, the H0 row and §14. Header carries the r7 line."}]}],"meta":{"source":"tool","messageId":"5c48a947-2ed3-44c1-9fdc-ddfc5817a604"}},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Alright, now let's read r7 of the design document. 1318 lines. Reading it split into two chunks. Key sections: header (review status), §2.4(iv), §3.1, §3.2, §3.3, §8.1, §8.3, §9.1, §9.3, §10, §14. For now, just reading the whole document (split into 2 parts).","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call_55e42b055ece4913b59a3806","name":"read_file","input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md","offset":1,"limit":470}}],"meta":{"source":"model","createdAt":1792523139670,"messageId":"046dd908-9aa2-4a93-bba0-9daf3cc48539"}},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call_55e42b055ece4913b59a3806","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read 1/1 file, 470 lines\nNote: showing lines 1-470. More of the file remains. Use offset=471 to continue.\n1: # dao.stream.host-boundary: dao.stream wherever the boundary can be an IO action\n2: \n3: The governing test is the owner's: **dao.stream is used wherever a host\n4: boundary can be an IO action; a pure, total, synchronous function on the same\n5: host may be a direct call.** This document's commissionable scope is that\n6: rule (§2) and the pure-primitive slices H0-H4 (§8): freeze and allowlist; the\n7: `dao.host.*` integer, float, number, bytes and text profile; migration of\n8: every consumer; hash and signature entries; the portable Jing CBOR writer.\n9: Entropy and scheduling, the durable-storage seam, the parser stage and the\n10: stream conformance suite are **deferred** to a separate design (§9) and are\n11: not commissionable here.\n12: \n13: Status: design only; nothing commissioned. **Revision r5**, 2026-10-20, by\n14: the Architect (claude-fable-5-1). **Review status: SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES\n15: by a non-Claude architect (gpt-6-astra, 2026-10-20,\n16: `collab/1792508286000-architect-host-boundary-review-r4.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`)\n17: for the narrowed scope only, §2 plus H0-H4; H5-H8 (§9) are not signed off\n18: and remain deferred.** The four mechanical corrections M1-M4 that sign-off\n19: required were applied in r5 (rationale\n20: `collab/1792508512000-architect-host-boundary-r5.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`)\n21: and should be confirmed by a non-Claude reviewer before H0 is dispatched.\n22: r7 (2026-10-21): reconciled with what slice H0 measured (F1-F4: the 16\n23: unlisted host arms categorised, the closure-based effect counts, the §3.1\n24: rows, the print and M-entry facts); rationale\n25: `collab/1792522578000-architect-host-boundary-r7.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\n26: **a non-Claude reviewer must confirm this r7 before H1 is dispatched.**\n27: r6: D1-D3 applied (a non-Claude reviewer's confirmation check,\n28: `collab/1792517656000-reviewer-host-boundary-r5.qwen3.8-max.findings.md`,\n29: found M1-M4 applied and three low defects; rationale\n30: `collab/1792518203000-architect-host-boundary-r6.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`).\n31: Lineage: r1 rationale\n32: `collab/1792495909000-architect-host-boundary.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\n33: r1 review NOT SIGNED OFF\n34: (`collab/1792498045000-architect-host-boundary-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\n35: A1-A8); r2 rationale `collab/1792502807000-...-r2.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\n36: r2 review NOT SIGNED OFF\n37: (`collab/1792503557000-architect-host-boundary-review-r2.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\n38: R1-R6); r3 rationale and disposition\n39: `collab/1792506992000-architect-host-boundary-r3.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`;\n40: r3 scoped review NOT SIGNED OFF\n41: (`collab/1792507576000-architect-host-boundary-review-r3.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\n42: N1-N6); **r4** answers N1-N6 within the unchanged scope (rationale\n43: `collab/1792507856000-architect-host-boundary-r4.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`).\n44: Owner decision for r3 (verbatim): \"yes, proceed with the split\". No code was\n45: changed; every claim about the tree is labelled. **Because this document was\n46: written by a Claude model, a non-Claude architect must sign off this final\n47: text (§2 plus H0-H4) before anything is commissioned from it** (§14).\n48: \n49: Owner direction, verbatim. Original: \"maybe this is an opportunity to stop\n50: and refactor so that the dao.stream is the boundary that crosses over host\n51: specific dependency. once that is done, the only thing needs porting is\n52: dao.stream implementations\". Refined: \"maybe my dao.stream ABI framing has to\n53: be refined.  when a host-boundary is cross should it be used. when its on the\n54: same host, then direct function call is fine. In other words, when the host\n55: boundary can be an IO monad then dao.stream is used. If it cannot be a monad\n56: than dao.stream doesnt have to be used\". Asked which formulation governs:\n57: \"yes, test 2 governs\".\n58: \n59: Authorities:\n60: - [datom.world.md](./datom.world.md) `:43-48`, `:70`.\n61: - [datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) §3 custody; §4 machine-internal\n62:   work executes inside the instance; §5 the floor and internal linkage; §6\n63:   effects out, events in; §7 adapter discipline (`:143-148`). §12 is an\n64:   owner proposal to amend it; nothing here edits it.\n65: - [dao.stream.md](./dao.stream.md): the contract; cursors are non-destructive\n66:   (`src/cljc/dao/stream.cljc:159-173`, V via the r2 review).\n67: - [dao.stream.wasm.md](./dao.stream.wasm.md) §1 `:29-52`: seven imports.\n68: - [dao.stream.codec.md](./dao.stream.codec.md): the codec track; untouched\n69:   here (§8.3).\n70: - [yin.vm.engine.md](./yin.vm.engine.md) §E.5; [dao.jing.md](./dao.jing.md);\n71:   [dao.jing.cbor.md](./dao.jing.cbor.md).\n72: \n73: Labels: V = verified by reading this tree on 2026-10-20 (line cites); R =\n74: read, computed by script, or inferred, not run under a compiler; P =\n75: proposed. Nothing was executed on a lane.\n76: \n77: Standalone: a future host lane is an example consumer, never a dependency.\n78: \n79: ---\n80: \n81: ## 1. Problem\n82: \n83: V: the portable core (`src/cljc`, 183 files) reaches hosts two ways and the\n84: doctrine sanctions only one. The sanctioned way is the transports' pattern:\n85: a portable core plus a per-host seam value the composition hands in\n86: (`yin.repl.host/websocket`, `host.cljc:20-37`; `datagram.jvm/bind!`,\n87: `jvm.clj:22-34`). The other way is host contact inside portable files: reader\n88: arms reaching a host library, and resolved calls to host-backed core\n89: functions that compile everywhere and still touch the world.\n90: \n91: The in-scope files (V, `rg -c '#\\?'`; what the arms reach):\n92: \n93: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n94: | File                              | `#?`  | Host contact (V)                                                                                        |\n95: +===================================+=======+=========================================================================================================+\n96: | `dao/jing/cbor.cljc` (1562 lines) | 52    | Big-integer helpers (`:106-254`); `Float64`/`Rational`/`Decimal` carriers (`:342-460`); JVM             |\n97: |                                   |       | `BigDecimal`/`Ratio` construction and access (`:483-632`); float bits (`:525-541`); writer selection    |\n98: |                                   |       | (`:58-60`: Boring on JVM and JS, the Dart `cbor` package).                                              |\n99: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n100: | `yin/vm/integer/host.cljc` (299)  | 30    | 25 public functions (`:41-298`): carrier recognition, promotion, 13 big operations.                     |\n101: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n102: | `dao/jing.cljc` (629)             | 12    | BLAKE3 and SHA-256 libraries (`:32-40, :59-135`); an opaque sentinel (`:43-51`).                        |\n103: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n104: | `yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc` (274)   | 16    | Ed25519 libraries; `jvm-pair` replays a given seed through a `SecureRandom` proxy (`:75-87`, V:         |\n105: |                                   |       | deterministic, not fresh entropy); `generate` draws fresh entropy (`:114-124`, deferred, §9).           |\n106: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n107: | `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`       | 14    | The two wire writers (`:cljd nil`-gated Boring wrapper; 107-line Dart twin).                            |\n108: | (132), `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cl |       |                                                                                                         |\n109: | jd.cljd`                          |       |                                                                                                         |\n110: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n111: | `yin/vm/debruijn.cljc` (16)       | 16    | Bytes predicate (`:167`), char access (`:241`), NFC (`:310-321`, Unicode 16.0 owner-settled), float     |\n112: |                                   |       | bits (`:855-885`), carrier tests (`:1255-1257`).                                                        |\n113: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n114: | `yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc` (13)  | 13    | Float64 little-endian hex (`:232-242`); JVM big-integer predicate (`:315-317`).                         |\n115: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n116: | `yin/vm/data.cljc` (7),           | 7+9   | Code units and `units->string` (`:119-173`); `integral` (`:77-92`); `Closure`/`Continuation` equality,  |\n117: | `yin/vm/values.cljc` (9)          |       | hash and print arms (`:39-119`).                                                                        |\n118: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n119: | `dao/stream/chunks.cljc` (5),     | 5+4+4 | `Uint8List` length, alloc, slice, copy (`chunks.cljc:8-32`); base64 libraries; host-bytes predicate     |\n120: | `base64.cljc`, `codec.cljc`       |       | (`codec.cljc:45`, codec-track file, §8.3).                                                              |\n121: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n122: | `dao/jing/dht.cljc` (10),         | 10+14 | Bytes helpers (`dht.cljc:100-121`); bytes length (`linker.cljc:1232`); `host-bytes`/`utf8`              |\n123: | `yin/vm/linker.cljc`, `datagram`  |       | (`sign.cljc:55-66`); byte length (`datagram.cljc:63-66`).                                               |\n124: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n125: | `dao/data/arrays.cljc` (21)       | 21    | Host arrays, `^long` hints, `parallelSort` (`:145`).                                                    |\n126: +-----------------------------------+-------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n127: \n128: Out of scope here and listed in §9: `dao/jing/file.cljc`, `dao/space/store/\n129: fs.cljc`, `dao/space/store.cljc`, `dao/stream/journal/file.cljc`,\n130: `dao/data/btree/storage.cljc` (storage); `dao/jing/content/async.cljc`\n131: (outside the closure, H0; recorded for the deferred design),\n132: `dao/jing/coordinate.cljc:80` and `src/clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj`\n133: (scheduling, blocking driver); every entropy site; `yang/python/antlr/\n134: parser.cljc` and the `yang.antlr.cst` hosts (parser stage).\n135: \n136: V, the duplication: `yin.vm.integer.host` says it \"does not reach into\n137: `dao.jing.cbor`'s private big-integer helpers\" (`host.cljc:23-25`); `cbor.cljc`\n138: keeps its own (`:112-216`). Two shims, one contract.\n139: \n140: ## 2. The governing rule\n141: \n142: ### 2.1 The boundary, defined first (review R1)\n143: \n144: **The machine** is the running instance and the state it owns: its values,\n145: and the mutable runtime state its own code created and no other party can\n146: observe or change: atoms and volatiles, host arrays and byte arrays it\n147: allocated, a B-tree's weak or soft references, the collector that clears\n148: them, thread-local locks between threads the driver created. **The world** is\n149: everything else: files, sockets, other processes, the operating system's\n150: clock, entropy sources, environment, the terminal, and any other instance.\n151: \n152: A host contact is **external** when it observes or changes the world. It is\n153: **internal** when it is a function of the machine's values and owned runtime\n154: state only.\n155: \n156: ### 2.2 Statement\n157: \n158: **An external contact uses `dao.stream`: it can be an IO action. An internal\n159: contact that is a pure, total, synchronous function of its arguments, or a\n160: declared mutation of an owned argument (§2.4), may be a direct call and is\n161: then an entry of the primitive profile (§4.3).**\n162: \n163: This is the owner's test 2. Same-host-ness is not the criterion: a same-host\n164: file read is external and is a stream; a hash from a library linked into the\n165: instance is internal and is a direct call.\n166: \n167: ### 2.3 \"Can be an IO action\", operationally\n168: \n169: A contact can be an IO action when any of these holds **with respect to the\n170: world** (§2.1):\n171: \n172: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n173: | #  | Property                       | Meaning and the doctrine that names it                                                                     |\n174: +====+================================+============================================================================================================+\n175: | 1  | Observation of the world       | The result depends on world state: a file, a socket, another process, the environment, a resource's        |\n176: |    |                                | current condition. `abi.md` §5: \"world facts that shipped code cannot compute\".                            |\n177: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n178: | 2  | Effect on the world            | Calling it changes world state: bytes on disk, a packet sent, a lock taken, text printed.                  |\n179: |    |                                | `datom.world.md:46`: \"Side effects must appear as stream emissions.\"                                       |\n180: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n181: | 3  | World nondeterminism           | Two calls with equal arguments and equal owned state may answer differently because of the world: entropy, |\n182: |    |                                | a clock, an OS-assigned pid, a process-wide counter shared with code the machine does not own.             |\n183: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n184: | 4  | Asynchrony                     | The answer is not available at return, or completion invokes something later: a timer, a thread, a         |\n185: |    |                                | promise. `dao.stream.md`: \"No operation waits\"; `abi.md` §6: a callback is an event on a stream.           |\n186: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n187: | 5  | World-dependent failure        | Whether it completes depends on the world (disk full, peer gone), not on the input. `dao.stream.md` Result |\n188: |    |                                | Convention: such outcomes are data.                                                                        |\n189: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n190: | 6  | Another process or host        | The work runs in a different process, machine or trust domain. `abi.md` §3.                                |\n191: +----+--------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n192: \n193: A contact cannot be an IO action when none holds. Then it is a function of\n194: the machine's values and owned state, computed synchronously in the same\n195: instance, whose only failures are functions of the input, answered as data\n196: or a refusal. Linking a library to compute it is internal linkage (`abi.md`\n197: §5 `:80-81`); executing it is machine-internal work (`abi.md` §4 `:60-62`).\n198: The basis of the primitive profile is therefore **internality plus purity,\n199: totality and synchrony over the declared domain**, with mutation admitted\n200: only as §2.4 declares it.\n201: \n202: A read of owned runtime state that may differ between calls (a weak\n203: reference after a collection, an atom another owned thread advanced) fails\n204: none of the six properties: it is a fact about the machine, not the world.\n205: It is a **runtime prerequisite** (table C), neither IO nor a profile entry.\n206: r2 classified it as both; that contradiction is withdrawn.\n207: \n208: ### 2.4 Ownership and mutation laws for the profile\n209: \n210: The profile's purity claim is exact, not blanket:\n211: \n212: - **Pure entries** (marked P in table A) are deterministic, total over their\n213:   declared domain, synchronous, and allocate only fresh values; equal\n214:   arguments give equal results on every host.\n215: - **Mutating entries** (marked M: `bytes/set!`, `bytes/copy!`, `arrays/aset`,\n216:   `arrays/acopy`, `arrays/asort`) mutate **exactly the argument named as the\n217:   target** and nothing else, and never retain it. Return values are per\n218:   entry, not uniform: `aset` returns the assigned value (Clojure's `aset`\n219:   semantics, V `arrays.cljc:50-57`, unchanged); `set!`, `copy!` and `acopy`\n220:   return nil; `asort` returns the sorted array. Overlapping `copy!` and\n221:   `acopy` behave as memmove (V: `acopy` already handles same-array overlap,\n222:   `:97-105`). **Prevalidation**: every index and length is checked before\n223:   any write, so a refused call leaves the target byte-identical. This is a\n224:   **behaviour change** on JS and Dart, where today's `acopy` loop writes\n225:   element by element and can write before meeting an invalid index\n226:   (`arrays.cljc:97-105`, V; the JVM's `System/arraycopy` checks first); it\n227:   is assigned to H2 with a no-partial-write test (table D).\n228: - **Aliasing**: `bytes/slice`, `arrays/asub`, `arrays/aclone`, `from-vector`\n229:   always return a fresh value; no entry returns a view onto an argument.\n230:   Nothing else is promised about copying: a value appended to a stream is\n231:   **not** copied by the stream in general. The ring buffer stores the value\n232:   object itself and reads return it (`ringbuffer.cljc:124-131`, V), and the\n233:   ABI permits in-instance streams with no crossing and no serialization\n234:   (`dao.stream.wasm.md:22-27`). Aliasing across a stream is therefore\n235:   possible, and the rule below is what makes it safe.\n236: - **Exclusive mutation right, ownership transfer, and freezing\n237:   publication.** A mutable profile value (bytes, a host array) has exactly\n238:   one party with the right to mutate it. Two distinct events move or end\n239:   that right:\n240:   - **Ownership transfer** (the right moves, the value stays mutable): when\n241:     a profile entry marked \"fresh\" (`alloc`, `slice`, `from-vector`,\n242:     `magnitude-bytes`, `anew`, `aclone`, `asub`, `aconcat`, `ainsert-at`,\n243:     `aremove-at`, `from-coll`, `utf8-encode`) returns a value, the **caller**\n244:     holds the exclusive mutation right from that return, and may `set!`,\n245:     `copy!`, `aset`, `acopy` or `asort` it. Returning fresh storage is not\n246:     publication.\n247:   - **Freezing publication** (the right ends for everyone): appending the\n248:     value to a stream (in-memory or not); placing it inside a value that is\n249:     appended; handing it to a seam or a content handle. On publication the\n250:     value is **frozen**: no party, the publisher included, may mutate it\n251:     afterwards, and a reader may not mutate what it reads. Freezing is\n252:     transitive: a collection is frozen with every mutable value nested in\n253:     it. A party that needs a mutable copy of a frozen value takes `slice`,\n254:     `aclone` or `from-vector`, and then holds the right to that copy.\n255: - **Obligations enforceable within H0-H4**: (i) every P entry leaves every\n256:   argument byte-identical, asserted by a property test in H1 over the bytes,\n257:   text, float and integer namespaces; (ii) every M entry mutates only its\n258:   named target and only after prevalidation, asserted in H1 (bytes) and H2\n259:   (arrays); (iii) the Jing writer (H4) never mutates its input and returns\n260:   fresh bytes, asserted by re-encoding the same input twice and comparing\n261:   the input's bytes before and after; (iv) the callers of M entries inside\n262:   the adopted closure are enumerated and audited in H2 for the freeze rule.\n263:   **What the H0 gate can and cannot see (V, H0 report).** The gate resolves\n264:   a call as an M entry only when it goes through `dao.data.arrays` or\n265:   `dao.host.bytes` by alias; today that is `dao.data.btree` alone (33 calls,\n266:   owned node arrays, never appended). `dao.stream.chunks` (`absorb`,\n267:   `chunks.cljc:27-34`), `dao.jing.cbor` (`magnitude-bytes` and the\n268:   byte-string assembly at `:254,1313-1317`) and `yin.vm.linker.sign` (`:82`)\n269:   mutate host arrays **directly inside reader arms** (`System/arraycopy`,\n270:   `.setRange`, `.set`, `aset`) and call no M entry until H2 migrates them,\n271:   so the gate cannot count them now. Obligations (i)-(iii) are tests, not\n272:   gate checks; the freeze rule itself (no mutation after publication) is\n273:   not mechanically checkable by the gate at all and remains a review\n274:   obligation. **What H2's audit must do instead**: (a) list every direct\n275:   host-array mutation inside an arm across the closure (the gate's arm\n276:   scan plus a search for `arraycopy`, `setRange`, `.set`, `aset`, `aclone`\n277:   on byte arrays); (b) replace each with an M entry or a fresh-allocating P\n278:   entry, so that after H2 the only mutation sites in the closure are M-entry\n279:   calls; (c) add the migrated callers to list 2 as `:m-entry` callers with\n280:   their ceilings and a one-line reason each; (d) record any site that keeps\n281:   a direct mutation (expected: none) as a named exception. After H2 a new\n282:   M-entry caller fails the gate's list 2 until reviewed; before H2 the gate\n283:   catches only new `dao.data.arrays` callers.\n284:   What is **not** enforceable here: a reader mutating a frozen value it\n285:   obtained from a stream; that is a composition obligation stated by this\n286:   rule and left to review until the deferred conformance suite (§9.4).\n287: \n288: ### 2.5 Borderline cases, decided\n289: \n290: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n291: | Case                              | Reasoning under §2.3                                                                                    | Verdict    |\n292: +===================================+=========================================================================================================+============+\n293: | UTF-8 decode, CBOR decode, base64 | Fallible, but the failure is a function of the input (property 5 does not hold); deterministic,         | Direct     |\n294: | decode, NFC normalization         | synchronous. Malformed input is a refusal value. NFC depends on a Unicode version, which the profile    | call       |\n295: |                                   | pins (16.0, V `debruijn.cljc:314`).                                                                     |            |\n296: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n297: | Hashing, signing, seed replay in  | Deterministic functions of bytes (and of the seed). The library is linkage. They execute in the same    | Direct     |\n298: | a library (BLAKE3, SHA-256,       | instance; for wasm, compiled in, never imported (§7). `sign.cljc:75-87`'s `SecureRandom` proxy replays  | call, in-  |\n299: | Ed25519; `sign.cljc:75-87`)       | the given seed bytes and is deterministic (V, R3 correction).                                           | instance   |\n300: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n301: | Fresh entropy, identities, key    | Property 3. Deferred design (§9); the profile contains no generator.                                    | Stream;    |\n302: | generation (`sign.cljc:114-124`)  |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n303: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n304: | Time; scheduling; the JVM         | Properties 1, 3, 4. `coordinate.cljc:80` selects `driver.clj:169-206`, which reads the clock and sleeps | Stream /   |\n305: | blocking content driver           | (V): JVM-lane IO inside a portable composition. Deferred (§9).                                          | driver;    |\n306: |                                   |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n307: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n308: | The Python parser                 | In-process, synchronous, deterministic over source text and the pinned grammar: internal, direct call.  | Either;    |\n309: |                                   | As another process: property 6, a stream. The stage shape (`parser.cljc:65-75`) serves both. Deferred.  | deferred   |\n310: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n311: | Same-host file read, lock, fsync, | Properties 1, 2, 5. External though same-host. Deferred (§9).                                           | Stream;    |\n312: | rename, directory listing         |                                                                                                         | deferred   |\n313: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n314: | Weak and soft references,         | Owned runtime state (§2.1). Not IO, not a profile entry: table C. The B-tree's default reference mode   | Runtime    |\n315: | atomics, `locking`, atoms         | is `:soft` on the JVM and `:strong` on JS and Dart (V `btree.cljc:39-44`); correctness is independent   | prereq.    |\n316: |                                   | of the mode, only retention differs.                                                                    |            |\n317: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n318: | Host `=`, `hash`, `compare`,      | Internal and pure per host, not identical across hosts for mixed types and floats. A portability hazard | Dialect    |\n319: | `sort`, `str` of a double, regex  | only where persisted bytes depend on it; those sites sort by canonical bytes (`cbor.cljc:1070,1177`,    | semantics  |\n320: |                                   | `ucf/rows.cljc:139`, `debruijn.cljc:921,929`). Regex sites (`custody.cljc:53`, `datagram.cljc:86-100`,  |            |\n321: |                                   | `jing.cljc:107-111,216-221`, `fs.cljc:105`) match ASCII grammars identical on every host.               |            |\n322: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n323: | `gensym` in a compiler            | A process-wide counter shared with code the machine does not own: property 3 in a weak form. Deferred   | Deferred   |\n324: |                                   | (§9), with the hygiene requirement the r2 review added.                                                 |            |\n325: +-----------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+------------+\n326: \n327: ### 2.6 Where the owner's original sentence is and is not met\n328: \n329: Met for every external contact: after this design and the deferred one, a\n330: host's world contact is the stream seams and its `main`. Not met literally:\n331: a port also implements the primitive profile and meets table C, because the\n332: dialect's own carriers cannot be supplied by a stream and must not be. The\n333: profile is exact, enumerated and oracle-checked, so that part is a list, not\n334: a design.\n335: \n336: ## 3. Classification over the adopted closure\n337: \n338: ### 3.1 The transitive closure, per lane (R, computed)\n339: \n340: Method: a require-form traversal from `yin.repl.main`, choosing each lane's\n341: reader arms (`:cljd` also reads `:clj`) and resolving each namespace to its\n342: file under `src/<lane>` first (shadow resolution: `yin.repl.host` is\n343: `host.cljc` on the JVM and the `host.cljs`/`host.cljd` shadows elsewhere),\n344: then `src/cljc`. It is not a compiler-resolved call graph: it can\n345: over-include a namespace required but unused on a path, cannot see dynamic\n346: loading, and **can under-include** when its reader-arm parsing fails; the\n347: r3 script did exactly that (it dropped requires inside `#?@` arms and\n348: reported 141/140/139). The r3 claim that it \"cannot under-include\" is\n349: withdrawn. The reconciled totals, counted by **file** and confirmed by the\n350: r3 reviewer's independent traversal (R):\n351: \n352: +-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n353: | Lane  | Total | Shared   | Lane-specific files (V, the files exist)                                                                            |\n354: +=======+=======+==========+=====================================================================================================================+\n355: | clj   | 142   | 133      | `clj/dao/jing/content/driver.clj` (blocking driver: clock, sleep, `locking`), `clj/dao/stream/datagram/jvm.clj`,    |\n356: |       |       |          | `clj/dao/stream/waitset/driver.clj`, `clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj` (clock, `CompletableFuture`), `clj/yang/antlr/     |\n357: |       |       |          | cst.clj`, `clj/yin/repl/host/jvm.clj`, `cljc/yin/repl/host.cljc` (the JVM selector),                                |\n358: |       |       |          | `cljc/dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`, `cljc/dao/stream/cbor/boring.cljc`. Nine.                                         |\n359: +-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n360: | cljs  | 140   | 133      | `cljs/dao/stream/datagram/node.cljs`, `cljs/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljs` (`setTimeout`), `cljs/dao/stream/ws/    |\n361: |       |       |          | node.cljs` (`js/Date`), `cljs/yang/antlr/cst.cljs`, `cljs/yin/repl/host.cljs` (shadow), `cljc/dao/jing/cbor/        |\n362: |       |       |          | boring.cljc`, `cljc/dao/stream/cbor/boring.cljc`. Seven.                                                            |\n363: +-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n364: | cljd  | 141   | 133      | `cljd/dao/stream/cbor/cljd.cljd`, `cljd/dao/stream/transit/cljd.cljd`, `cljd/dao/stream/datagram/dart.cljd`,        |\n365: |       |       |          | `cljd/dao/stream/waitset/driver.cljd` (`Timer`), `cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd` (`DateTime.now`), `cljd/yang/antlr/ |\n366: |       |       |          | cst.cljd`, `cljd/yin/repl/host.cljd` (shadow), `cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd`. Eight.                               |\n367: +-------+-------+----------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n368: \n369: The 133 shared files are `.cljc`. H0 commits the reproducible evidence\n370: (§8): the traversal is re-implemented over `clojure.tools.namespace`'s\n371: `ns` parsing (which handles reader conditionals per platform) rather than a\n372: regex, committed as `src/dev/host_boundary/closure.clj` (H0 placed it\n373: under `src/dev/` with the repo's other dev tools), and its output as\n374: `docs/design/host-boundary/closure-{clj,cljs,cljd}.edn`; H0's acceptance is\n375: that the committed output lists exactly the 142/140/141 files above, and\n376: that the two traversals (the committed tool and the r3 reviewer's method)\n377: agree, recorded in the slice report. Until that lands, the totals here are\n378: R.\n379: \n380: Classification of every file in the closure that has any host contact (the\n381: remaining files have none beyond §3.3 syntax; the committed H0 output marks\n382: each file with its class, which is what makes \"remaining\" checkable):\n383: \n384: +-----------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------+\n385: | Namespaces                                          | Contact (V)                            | Class and slice                                          |\n386: +=====================================================+========================================+==========================================================+\n387: | `yin.vm.integer.host`, `dao.jing.cbor` (helpers,    | Big integers, floats, Decimal/Ratio,   | Internal pure: profile. H1-H2 (writer: H4).              |\n388: | carriers, Decimal/Ratio, float bits, writer),       | NFC, bytes, code units, host arrays    |               